<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Picard scheme<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The author is grateful to Allen Altman, Ethan Cotterill,
Eduardo Esteves, Rebecca Lehman, Jean-Pierre Serre, and Angelo Vistoli
for their many valuable comments on earlier versions of these notes.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Steven L. Kleiman
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Math Department, MIT 
<br class="ltx_break">77 Massachusetts Avenue
<br class="ltx_break">Cambridge, MA 02139, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:kleiman@math.mit.edu">kleiman@math.mit.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop in detail most of the theory of the Picard scheme that
Grothendieck sketched in two Bourbaki talks and in commentaries on them.
Also, we review in brief much of the rest of the theory developed by
Grothendieck and by others. But we begin with a historical
introduction.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Picard scheme, Picard functor, relative divisor,
intersection theory
</div>
