<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On any ringed space <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, the isomorphism classes of invertible sheaves
form a group; it is denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and called the (absolute) <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Picard group</span>. Suppose <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a “projective variety”; in other words,
<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an integral scheme that is projective over an algebraically
closed field <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, as is proved in these notes, the group
<math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> underlies a natural <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, which is a disjoint union of
quasi-projective schemes, and the operations of multiplying and of
inverting are given by <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-maps. This scheme is denoted by
<math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and called the <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Picard scheme</span>. It is reduced in
characteristic zero, but not always in positive characteristic. When
<math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> varies in an algebraic family, correspondingly, <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> does
too.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Picard scheme was introduced in 1962 by Grothendieck. He sketched
his theory in two Bourbaki talks, nos. 232 and 236, which were reprinted
along with his commentaries in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>]</cite>. But Grothendieck advanced an
old subject, which was actively being developed by many others at the
time. Nevertheless, Grothendieck’s theory was revolutionary, both in
concept and in technique.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to appreciate Grothendieck’s contribution fully, we have to
review the history of the Picard scheme. Reviewing this history serves
as well to introduce and to motivate Grothendieck’s theory.
Furthermore, the history is rich and fascinating, and it is a
significant part of the history of algebraic geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">So let us now review the history of the Picard scheme up to 1962. We
need only summarize and elaborate on scattered parts of Brigaglia,
Ciliberto, and Pedrini’s article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BCP</a>]</cite> and of the author’s article
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl04</a>]</cite>. Both articles give many precise references to the original
sources and to the secondary literature; so few references are given
here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Picard scheme has roots in the 1600s. Over the course of that
century, the Calculus was developed, through the efforts of many
individuals, in order to design lenses, to aim cannons, to make clocks,
to hang cables, and so on. Thus interest arose in the properties of
functions appearing as indefinite integrals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, in 1694, James Bernoulli analyzed the way rods bend, and was
led to introduce the “lemniscate,” a figure eight with equation
<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{2}+y^{2})^{2}=a^{2}(x^{2}-y^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{2}+y^{2})^{2}=a^{2}(x^{2}-y^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero. In polar coordinates, he found the arc length
<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to be given by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\int_{0}^{r}\frac{a^{2}\,dr}{\sqrt{a^{4}-r^{4}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\int_{0}^{r}\frac{a^{2}\,dr}{\sqrt{a^{4}-r^{4}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">He surmised that <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> can not be expressed in terms of the elementary
functions. Similar integrals had already arisen in attempts to rectify
elliptical orbits; so these integrals became known as “elliptic
integrals.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In 1698, James’s brother, John, recalled there are algebraic relations
among the arguments of sums and differences of logarithms and of the
inverse trigonometric functions. Then he showed that, similarly, given
two arcs from the origin on the cubical parabola <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, their lengths
differ by the length of a certain third such arc. And he posed the
problem of finding more cases of this phenomenon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Sure enough, between 1714 and 1720, Fagnano found, in an ad hoc manner,
similar relations for the cords and arcs of ellipses, hyperbolas, and
lemniscates. In turn, Fagnano’s work led Euler in 1757 to discover the
“addition formula”</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{x_{1}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\pm\int_{0}^{x_{2}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}=\int_{0}^{x_{3}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">±</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{x_{1}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}\pm\int_{0}^{x_{2}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}=\int_{0}^{x_{3}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{1-x^{4}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where the variables <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\,x_{2},\,x_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\,x_{2},\,x_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy the symmetric relation</p>
<table id="S1.p8.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{4}x_{2}^{4}x_{3}^{4}+2x_{1}^{4}x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{2}+2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{4}x_{3}^{2}+2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{4}\\
+x_{1}^{4}+x_{2}^{4}+x_{3}^{4}-2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2}-2x_{1}^{2}x_{3}^{2}-2x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{2}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{4}x_{2}^{4}x_{3}^{4}+2x_{1}^{4}x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{2}+2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{4}x_{3}^{2}+2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{4}\\
+x_{1}^{4}+x_{2}^{4}+x_{3}^{4}-2x_{1}^{2}x_{2}^{2}-2x_{1}^{2}x_{3}^{2}-2x_{2}^{2}x_{3}^{2}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.4" class="ltx_p">In 1759, Euler generalized this formula to some other elliptic
integrals. Specifically, Euler found the sum or difference of two to be
equal to a certain third plus an elementary function. Moreover, he
expressed regret that he could handle only square roots and fourth
powers, but not higher roots or powers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In 1826, Abel made a great advance: he discovered an addition theorem of
sweeping generality. It concerns certain algebraic integrals, which
soon came to be called “Abelian integrals.” They are of the following
form:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}R(x,y)\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}R(x,y)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent complex variable, <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational
function, and <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is an integral algebraic function; that is,
<math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the implicit multivalued function defined by an irreducible
equation of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y):=y^{n}+f_{1}(x)y^{n-1}+\dots+f_{n}(x)=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y):=y^{n}+f_{1}(x)y^{n-1}+\dots+f_{n}(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomials in <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be the genus of the curve <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1},\dotsc,h_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1},\dotsc,h_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
be rational numbers. Then Abel’s addition theorem asserts that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}\psi x_{1}+\cdots+h_{\alpha}\psi x_{\alpha}=v+\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}\psi x_{1}+\cdots+h_{\alpha}\psi x_{\alpha}=v+\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is an elementary function of the independent variables
<math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and where <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are algebraic
functions of them. More precisely, <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex-linear combination
of one algebraic function of <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and of logarithms of
others; moreover, <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> work for every choice
of <math id="S1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly, <math id="S1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal: given algebraic functions
<math id="S1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p-1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{\prime},\dots,x_{p-1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,\penalty x_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,\penalty x_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an
integral <math id="S1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for any elementary function <math id="S1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}\neq v+\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p-1}^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}\neq v+\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p-1}^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Abel finished his 61-page manuscript in Paris, and submitted it in
person on 30 October 1826 to the Royal Academy of Sciences, which
appointed Cauchy and Legendre as referees. However, the Academy did not
publish it until 1841, long after Abel’s death from tuberculosis on 6
April 1829.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, Abel feared his manuscript was lost forever. So in Crelle’s
Journal, <span id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span> (1828), he summarized his general addition theorem
informally. Then he treated in detail a major special case, that in
which <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y):=y^{2}-\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y):=y^{2}-\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a square-free
polynomial of degree <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, Abel found</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\begin{cases}(d-1)/2,&amp;\text{if $d$ is odd};\\
(d-2)/2,&amp;\text{if $d$ is even}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>d</mi><mtext> is odd</mtext></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>d</mi><mtext> is even</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\begin{cases}(d-1)/2,&amp;\text{if $d$ is odd};\\
(d-2)/2,&amp;\text{if $d$ is even}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, if <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and so Euler’s formula does not extend.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">With Jacobi’s help, Legendre came to appreciate the importance of this
case. To it, Legendre devoted the third supplement to his long treatise
on elliptic integrals, which are recovered when <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3,4</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, the integrals share many of the same formal properties. So
Legendre termed them “ultra-elliptiques.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Legendre sent a copy of the supplement to Crelle for review on 24 March
1832, and Crelle asked Jacobi to review it. Jacobi translated
“ultra-elliptiques” by “hyperelliptischen,” and the prefix “hyper”
has stuck. In his cover letter, Legendre praised Abel’s addition
theorem, calling it, in the immortal words of Horace’s Ode 3, XXX.1, “a
monument more lasting than bronze” (monumentum aere perennius). In his
review, Jacobi said that the theorem would be a most noble monument were
it to acquire the name <span id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abel’s Theorem.</span> And it did!</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Jacobi was inspired to give, a few months later, the first of several
proofs of Abel’s Theorem in the hyperelliptic case. Furthermore, he
posed the famous problem, which became known as the “Jacobi Inversion
Problem.” He asked, “what, in the general case, are those functions
whose inverses are Abelian integrals, and what does Abel’s theorem show
about them?”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Jacobi solved the inversion problem when <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5,6</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, he formed</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}}\text{ and }\psi_{1}x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}}\text{ and }\psi_{1}x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p16.2" class="ltx_p">and he set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x+\psi y=u\text{ and }\psi_{1}x+\psi_{1}y=v." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x+\psi y=u\text{ and }\psi_{1}x+\psi_{1}y=v.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p16.3" class="ltx_p">He showed <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy</annotation></semantics></math> are single-valued functions of <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
with four periods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Some historians have felt Abel had this inversion in mind, but ran out
of time. At any rate, in 1827, Abel had originated the idea of
inverting elliptic integrals, obtaining what became known as “elliptic
functions.” He and Jacobi studied them extensively. Moreover, Jacobi
introduced “theta functions” as an aid in the study; they were
generalized by Riemann in 1857, and used to solve the inversion problem
in arbitrary genus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Abel’s paper on hyperelliptic integrals fills twelve pages. Eight are
devoted to a computational proof of a key intermediate result. A half
year later, in Crelle’s Journal, <span id="S1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span> (1829), Abel published a 2-page
paper with a conceptual proof of this result for any Abelian integral
<math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x</annotation></semantics></math>. The result says that <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is
equal to an elementary function <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> are not
independent, but are the abscissas of the variable points of
intersection of the curve <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> with a second plane curve that varies in
a linear system  — although this geometric formulation is Clebsch’s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">In each of the first two papers, Abel addressed two more, intermediate
questions: First, when is the sum <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>
constant? Second, what is the number <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that can vary
independently? Remarkably, the answers involve the genus <math id="S1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">For hyperelliptic integrals, Abel found <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is
constant if <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of the following <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
integrals:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}},\ \int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}},\dotsc,\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x^{p-1}\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}},\ \int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}},\dotsc,\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{x^{p-1}\,dx}{\sqrt{\varphi(x)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p20.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> are the abscissas of the variable points of
intersection of the curve <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{2}=\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{2}=\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the curve
<math id="S1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}(x)y=\theta_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}(x)y=\theta_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose coefficients vary, but <math id="S1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> retain a fixed common factor <math id="S1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, Abel found</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu-\alpha\geq p;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu-\alpha\geq p;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p20.3" class="ltx_p">equality does not always hold, but can be achieved, given <math id="S1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, by choosing the degrees of <math id="S1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
appropriately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu-\alpha=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu-\alpha=p</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha}=v-(\psi x_{\alpha+1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha+p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha}=v-(\psi x_{\alpha+1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha+p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\alpha+1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha+p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\alpha+1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha+p}</annotation></semantics></math> are algebraic functions of
<math id="S1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, given any
<math id="S1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{\prime},\dotsc,x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{\prime},\dotsc,x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}+\psi x_{\alpha+1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha+p}=v^{\prime}-(\psi x_{1}^{\prime\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}^{\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}+\psi x_{\alpha+1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha+p}=v^{\prime}-(\psi x_{1}^{\prime\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.3" class="ltx_p">Subtract this formula from the one above, and set <math id="S1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V:=v-v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:=v-v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The result
is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha}-\psi x_{1}^{\prime}-\cdots-\psi x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}=V+\psi x_{1}^{\prime\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime\prime}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x_{1}+\cdots+\psi x_{\alpha}-\psi x_{1}^{\prime}-\cdots-\psi x_{\alpha^{\prime}}^{\prime}=V+\psi x_{1}^{\prime\prime}+\cdots+\psi x_{p}^{\prime\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p21.4" class="ltx_p">namely, the addition theorem with <math id="S1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>. This result is
essentially Abel’s main theorem on hyperelliptic integrals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">In his Paris manuscript, Abel addressed the two intermediate questions
for an arbitrary <math id="S1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. However, his computations are more involved, and
his results, less definitive. He found constancy holds when <math id="S1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x</annotation></semantics></math> is
of the form</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{h(x,y)}{\partial f/\partial y}\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi x:=\int^{x}_{x_{0}}\frac{h(x,y)}{\partial f/\partial y}\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p22.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg h\leq\deg f-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg h\leq\deg f-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, <math id="S1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy certain linear
conditions; namely, <math id="S1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> must vanish suitably everywhere <math id="S1.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f/\partial y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f/\partial y</annotation></semantics></math> does on the curve <math id="S1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, at finite distance and at
infinity. Abel took the maximum number of independent <math id="S1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> as the genus
<math id="S1.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, Abel found that there exists an <math id="S1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu-\alpha=p-i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu-\alpha=p-i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p23.2" class="ltx_p">This equation does not contradict (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) as the two
<math id="S1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>’s differ; in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), the linear system of
intersections is incomplete, whereas in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>), the
system is complete.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Abel’s ideas have been clarified and completed over the course of time
through the efforts of many. Doubtless, Riemann made the greatest
contribution in his revolutionary 1857 paper on Abelian functions. In
his thesis of 1851, he had developed a way of extending complex analysis
to a multivalued function <math id="S1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> of a single variable <math id="S1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by viewing <math id="S1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as
a single-valued function on an abstract multisheeted covering of the
<math id="S1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-plane, the “Riemann surface” of <math id="S1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1857, he treated the case
where the surface is compact, and showed this case is precisely the case
where <math id="S1.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Riemann defined the genus <math id="S1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> topologically, essentially as half the
first Betti number of the surface. However, the term “genus” is not
Riemann’s, but Clebsch’s. Clebsch introduced it in 1865 to signal his
aim of using <math id="S1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in order to classify algebraic curves. And he showed
that every curve of genus 0 is birationally equivalent to a line, and
every curve of genus 1, to a nonsingular plane cubic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Also in 1865, Clebsch gave an algebro-geometric formula for the genus
<math id="S1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of a plane curve: if the curve has degree <math id="S1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and, at worst,
<math id="S1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and <math id="S1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> cusps, then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(d-1)(d-2)/2-\delta-\kappa." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(d-1)(d-2)/2-\delta-\kappa.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p26.2" class="ltx_p">The next year, Clebsch and Gordan employed this formula to prove the
birational invariance of <math id="S1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>; they determined how <math id="S1.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S1.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Plainly, birationally equivalent curves have homeomorphic Riemann
surfaces, and so the same genus <math id="S1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. But Clebsch was no longer
satisfied in just showing the consequences of Riemann’s work. He now
wanted to establish the theory of Abelian integrals on the basis of the
algebraic theory of curves as developed by Cayley, Salmon, and
Sylvester. At the time, Riemann’s theory was strange and suspect; there
was, as yet, no theory of manifolds, and no proof of the Dirichlet
principle. Clebsch’s efforts led to a sea change in algebraic geometry,
which turned toward the study of birational invariants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Riemann defined an integral to be of the “first kind” if it is finite
everywhere. He proved these integrals form a vector space of dimension <math id="S1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, each can be expressed in the form (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>)
provided the curve <math id="S1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> has, at worst, double points; if so, the linear
conditions on <math id="S1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> just require <math id="S1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> to vanish at these double points. In
1874, Brill and M. Noether generalized this result to ordinary <math id="S1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-fold
points: <math id="S1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> must vanish to order <math id="S1.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math>. They termed such <math id="S1.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>
“adjoints.” Meanwhile, starting with Kronecker in 1858 and Noether in
1871 and continuing through Muhly and Zariski in 1938, many algebraic
geometers developed corresponding ways of reducing the singularities of
a given plane curve by means of birational transformations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Euler noted the integral <math id="S1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{x}dx/\sqrt{1-x^{4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{x}dx/\sqrt{1-x^{4}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a “modulus of
multivaluedness” like that of the inverse trigonometric functions.
Abel noted an arbitrary Abelian integral has a similar ambiguity, but
viewed it as a sort of constant of integration, and avoided it by
keeping the domain small. Riemann clarified the issue completely. He
proved every integral of the first kind has <math id="S1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> “periods,” which are
numbers that generate all possible changes in the value of the integral
arising from changes in the path of integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Riemann, in effect, did as follows. He fixed a basis
<math id="S1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}x,\dotsc,\psi_{p}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}x,\dotsc,\psi_{p}x</annotation></semantics></math> of the integrals of the first kind, and he
fixed a homology basis of <math id="S1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> paths. Then, inside the vector space
<math id="S1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{\,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{\,p}</annotation></semantics></math>, he formed the lattice <math id="S1.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the
<math id="S1.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding <math id="S1.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors of periods. And he proved the quotient
is a <math id="S1.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional complex torus</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p30.2" class="ltx_p">Later <math id="S1.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> was termed the “Jacobian” to honor Jacobi’s work on
inversion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be the curve <math id="S1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, or better, the associated Riemann surface.
Let <math id="S1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(\mu)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(\mu)}</annotation></semantics></math> be its <math id="S1.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-fold symmetric product. Riemann, in
effect, formed the following map:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}\colon C^{(\mu)}\to J\mbox{ given by }\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})=\biggl(\sum_{i=1}^{\mu}\psi_{1}x_{i},\dots,\sum_{i=1}^{\mu}\psi_{p}x_{i}\biggr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> given by </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}\colon C^{(\mu)}\to J\mbox{ given by }\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})=\biggl(\sum_{i=1}^{\mu}\psi_{1}x_{i},\dots,\sum_{i=1}^{\mu}\psi_{p}x_{i}\biggr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p31.2" class="ltx_p">This map <math id="S1.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is rather important. It has been called the
“Abel–Jacobi map” and the “Abel map.” The latter name is
historically more correct and shorter, so better.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Riemann, in effect, studied the fibers of the Abel map <math id="S1.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>.
He proved that, if two divisors <math id="S1.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{\prime}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{\prime}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly equivalent, then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})=\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1}^{\prime},\dotsc,x_{\mu}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})=\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1}^{\prime},\dotsc,x_{\mu}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p32.2" class="ltx_p">Riemann called this result “Abel’s Addition Theorem,” and cited
Jacobi’s 1832 proof of it in the hyperelliptic case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p33.1" class="ltx_p">The converse of this result holds too. But Abel did not recognize
it, and it lies, at best, between the lines of Riemann’s paper. The
converse was first explicitly stated by Clebsch in 1864, and first
proved in full generality some time later by Weierstrass. In 1913, in
Weyl’s celebrated book on Riemann surfaces, Weyl combined the result and
its converse under the heading of Abel’s Theorem. Ever since then, most
mathematicians have done the same, even though Weyl explained it is not
historically correct to do so.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Together, the above result and its converse imply the fiber
<math id="S1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}^{-1}\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}^{-1}\Psi_{\mu}(x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete linear system
determined by <math id="S1.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}+\dotsb+x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its dimension is just Abel’s
<math id="S1.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of <math id="S1.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that can vary independently in the system.
Furthermore, in effect, Riemann rediscovered Abel’s formula
(<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>), and in 1864, Roch identified <math id="S1.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of
independent adjoints vanishing on <math id="S1.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1874, Brill
and Noether, inspired by Clebsch, gave the first algebro-geometric
treatment of the formula, whose statement they named the “Riemann–Roch
Theorem.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, Riemann treated the inversion problem. In effect, he proved
that the Abel map <math id="S1.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic on a certain saturated
Zariski open subset <math id="S1.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset C^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset C^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>; namely, <math id="S1.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of
the image of <math id="S1.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(p-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(p-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> under the map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{2},\dotsc,x_{p})\mapsto(x_{0},x_{2},\dotsc,x_{p})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{2},\dotsc,x_{p})\mapsto(x_{0},x_{2},\dotsc,x_{p}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p35.2" class="ltx_p">The inverse map <math id="S1.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{p}U\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{p}U\to U</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed using the coordinate
functions on <math id="S1.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, so in terms of functions on <math id="S1.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{p}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{p}U</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S1.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, these functions can be lifted to an
open subset of <math id="S1.p35.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{\,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{\,p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then continued to meromorphic
functions on <math id="S1.p35.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{\,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{\,p}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p35.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> periods. Riemann termed these
special functions “Abelian functions.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Two years later, in 1859, Riemann proved that every meromorphic function
<math id="S1.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> variables has at most <math id="S1.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> independent period vectors. Those
<math id="S1.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> with exactly <math id="S1.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math> soon became known as “Abelian functions.” They were
studied by many, including Weierstrass, Frobenius, and Poincaré. In
particular, in 1869, Weierstrass observed that not every <math id="S1.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> comes from a
curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Form the set <math id="S1.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of all “Abelian functions” whose group of periods
contains a given lattice <math id="S1.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{\,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{\,p}</annotation></semantics></math>
of rank <math id="S1.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p</annotation></semantics></math>. It turns out <math id="S1.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>
is a field of transcendence degree <math id="S1.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S1.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is
the field of rational functions on a <math id="S1.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional projective
algebraic variety <math id="S1.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, which is parameterized on a Zariski open set by
<math id="S1.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of them. There are many such <math id="S1.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, and all were called “Abelian
varieties” at first. In 1919, Lefschetz proved there is a
distinguished <math id="S1.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, whose underlying set can be identified with
<math id="S1.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{\,p}/\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in a natural way, and he restricted the
term “Abelian variety” to it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Not only do the points of an Abelian variety <math id="S1.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> form a group, but the
operations of adding and of inverting are given by polynomials. Thus
<math id="S1.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete algebraic group, or an “Abelian variety” in Weil’s
sense of 1948. Weil proved each such abstract Abelian variety is
commutative. Earlier, in 1889, Picard had, in effect, proved this
commutativity in the case of a surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Every connected projective algebraic group is parameterized globally by
Abelian functions with a common lattice of periods. This fact was
proved by Picard for surfaces in 1889 and, assuming the group is
commutative, in any dimension in 1895. His proof was completed at
certain points of analysis in 1903 by Painlevé. Thus the two
definitions of Abelian variety agree, Lefschetz’s and Weil’s; however,
Weil worked in arbitrary characteristic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p40.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <math id="S1.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> above, its Jacobian <math id="S1.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is thus an Abelian variety.
Moreover, <math id="S1.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient of <math id="S1.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(\mu)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(\mu)}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> by
linear equivalence. So <math id="S1.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined by integrals, but
given by polynomials! And addition on <math id="S1.p40.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to addition of
divisors. Therefore, <math id="S1.p40.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p40.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> can be constructed
algebro-geometrically just by forming the quotient. Severi attributed
this construction to Castelnuovo.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p41.1" class="ltx_p">In 1905, Castelnuovo generalized the construction to surfaces. To set
the stage, he reviewed the case of curves, calling it very well known
(notissimo). His work is a milestone in the history of irregular
surfaces, which began in 1868.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p42.1" class="ltx_p">In 1868, Clebsch generalized Abel’s formula (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) to a
surface <math id="S1.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y,z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math> with ordinary singularities and no point at
infinity on the <math id="S1.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-axis; in other words, <math id="S1.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> is a general projection
of a smooth surface. Clebsch showed that every double integral of the
first kind is of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\!\!\int\frac{h(x,y,z)}{\partial f/\partial z}\,dx\,dy" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.355em;" width="0.355em"><mo>∫</mo></mpadded><mo>∫</mo></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\!\!\int\frac{h(x,y,z)}{\partial f/\partial z}\,dx\,dy</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p42.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg h=\deg f-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg h=\deg f-4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes when <math id="S1.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f/\partial z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f/\partial z</annotation></semantics></math> does. The number of independent integrals became known as the
“geometric genus” and denoted by <math id="S1.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p43.1" class="ltx_p">In 1870, M. Noether found an algebro-geometric proof that <math id="S1.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
birational invariant, as conjectured by Clebsch. In 1871, Cayley found
a formula for the expected number of independent <math id="S1.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, later called the
“arithmetic genus” and denoted by <math id="S1.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. He observed that, if <math id="S1.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>
is a ruled surface over a base curve of genus <math id="S1.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}=-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}=-p</annotation></semantics></math>, but
<math id="S1.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Later in 1871, Zeuthen used Cayley’s formula to give an
algebro-geometric proof that <math id="S1.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> too is a birational invariant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p44.1" class="ltx_p">In 1875, Noether explained the unexpected discrepancy between <math id="S1.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>: the vanishing conditions on <math id="S1.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> need not be independent; in any
case, <math id="S1.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}\geq p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}\geq p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. He noted that, if the surface <math id="S1.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> in 3-space is
smooth or rational, then <math id="S1.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}=p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}=p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. It was expected that equality
usually holds; so when it did, <math id="S1.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> was termed “regular.” The
difference <math id="S1.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a quantitative measure of the failure of
<math id="S1.p44.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> to be regular; so <math id="S1.p44.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> was termed its “irregularity.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p45.1" class="ltx_p">In 1884, Picard studied, on the surface <math id="S1.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, simple integrals</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int P(x,y,z)\,dx+\int Q(x,y,z)\,dy" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int P(x,y,z)\,dx+\int Q(x,y,z)\,dy</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p45.2" class="ltx_p">that are closed, or <math id="S1.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial P/\partial y=\partial Q/\partial x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial P/\partial y=\partial Q/\partial x</annotation></semantics></math>;
they became known as “Picard integrals.” And <math id="S1.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> was used to denote
the number of independent Picard integrals of the first kind. Picard
noted that, if <math id="S1.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, then <math id="S1.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1894, Humbert proved
that, if <math id="S1.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then every algebraic system of curves is contained in a
linear system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by Humbert’s result, in 1896, Castelnuovo proved that, if
<math id="S1.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then every algebraic system of curves on <math id="S1.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math> is contained
in a linear system under a certain restriction. In 1899, Enriques
removed the restriction. For a modern version of this result and of its
converse, which together characterize regular surfaces, see
Exercises <a href="#S5.Thmthm16" title="Exercise 5.16. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm17" title="Exercise 5.17. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.17</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p47.1" class="ltx_p">In 1897, Castelnuovo fixed a linear system of curves on the surface
<math id="S1.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and studied the “characteristic” linear system cut out on a
general member by the other members. Let <math id="S1.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> be the amount, termed
the “deficiency,” by which the dimension of the characteristic system
falls short of the dimension of its complete linear system. Castelnuovo
proved that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq p_{g}-p_{a}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq p_{g}-p_{a},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p47.2" class="ltx_p">and equality holds for the system cut out by the surfaces of suitably
high degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p48.1" class="ltx_p">In February 1904, Severi extended Castelnuovo’s work. Severi took a
complete algebraic system of curves, without repetition, on the surface
<math id="S1.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>, say with parameter space <math id="S1.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S1.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a general point, and <math id="S1.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding
curve. Say <math id="S1.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> moves in a complete linear system of dimension
<math id="S1.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, to each tangent direction at <math id="S1.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, Severi
associated, in an injective fashion, a member of the complete
characteristic system on <math id="S1.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus he got an <math id="S1.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
“characteristic” linear subsystem. The complete system has dimension
<math id="S1.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\leq r+p_{g}-p_{a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\leq r+p_{g}-p_{a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p49.1" class="ltx_p">A few months later, Enriques and, shortly afterward, Severi gave proofs
that, if <math id="S1.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently positive, then equality holds above;
in other words, then the characteristic system of <math id="S1.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is complete.
Both proofs turned out to have serious gaps, as Severi himself observed
in 1921. Meanwhile, in 1910, Poincaré gave an analytic construction
of a family with <math id="S1.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=p_{g}-p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=p_{g}-p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows formally, by means
of the Riemann–Roch Theorem for surfaces, that whenever <math id="S1.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is
sufficiently positive, the characteristic system is complete. After
Severi’s criticism, it became a major open problem to find a purely
algebro-geometric treatment of this issue. But see
Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5.5. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> and Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm18" title="Remark 5.18. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.18</span></a>: the solution
finally came forty years later with Grothendieck’s systematic use of
nilpotents!</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p50.1" class="ltx_p">In mid January 1905, Severi proved that <math id="S1.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}\geq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}\geq q</annotation></semantics></math> and that
<math id="S1.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}=b-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}=b-q</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of independent Picard integrals of
the second kind, which is equal to the first Betti number.
Simultaneously and independently, Picard too proved that <math id="S1.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}=b-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}=b-q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p51.1" class="ltx_p">A week later and more fully that May and June, Castelnuovo took the last
step in this direction. He fixed <math id="S1.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently positive, and
formed the quotient, <math id="S1.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> say, of <math id="S1.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> modulo linear equivalence. So
<math id="S1.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, and
<math id="S1.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=p_{g}-p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=p_{g}-p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, since two sufficiently positive curves sum to a third, it
follows that <math id="S1.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choice of <math id="S1.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is a
commutative group variety. Hence <math id="S1.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian variety by the
general theorem of Picard, completed by Painlevé, mentioned above.
Hence <math id="S1.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is parameterized by <math id="S1.p51.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P</annotation></semantics></math> Abelian functions. Castelnuovo
proved they induce independent Picard integrals on <math id="S1.p51.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,
<math id="S1.p51.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}-p_{a}\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}-p_{a}\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus Castelnuovo obtained the Fundamental Theorem of
Irregular Surfaces:</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=p_{g}-p_{a}=q=b/2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=p_{g}-p_{a}=q=b/2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p51.2" class="ltx_p">For a modern discussion of the result, see Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm15" title="Remark 5.15. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.15</span></a> and
Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm16" title="Exercise 5.16. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>. In 1905, the term “Abelian variety” was not
yet in use, and so, naturally enough, Castelnuovo termed <math id="S1.p51.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> the
“Picard variety” of the surface <math id="S1.p51.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p52.1" class="ltx_p">Picard also studied Picard integrals of the third kind on the surface
<math id="S1.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1901, he proved that there is a smallest integer <math id="S1.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho</annotation></semantics></math>
such that any <math id="S1.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϱ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho+1</annotation></semantics></math> curves are the logarithmic curves of some such
integral. On the basis this result, in 1905, Severi proved, in effect,
that <math id="S1.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank of the group of all curves modulo algebraic
equivalence. In 1952, Néron proved the result in arbitrary
characteristic. So the group is now called the “Néron–Severi
group,” and <math id="S1.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho</annotation></semantics></math> is called the “Picard number.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p53.1" class="ltx_p">In 1908 and 1910, Severi studied, in effect, the torsion subgroup of the
Néron–Severi group, notably proving it is finite. In 1957, Matsusaka
proved this finiteness in arbitrary characteristic. However, there is
no special name for this subgroup or for its order. For more about them
and <math id="S1.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho</annotation></semantics></math>, see Corollary (<a href="#S6.Thmthm17" title="Corollary 6.17. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.17</span></a>) and Remark
(<a href="#S6.Thmthm19" title="Remark 6.19. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.19</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p54.1" class="ltx_p">The impetus to work in arbitrary characteristic came from developments
in number theory. In 1921, E. Artin developed, in his thesis, an
analogue of the Riemann Hypothesis, in effect, for a hyperelliptic curve
over a prime field of odd characteristic. In 1929, F. K. Schmidt
generalized Artin’s work to all curves over all finite fields, and recast
it in the geometric style of Dedekind and Weber. In 1882, they had
viewed a curve as the set of discrete valuation rings in a finitely
generated field of transcendence degree 1 over <math id="S1.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and they had
given an abstract algebraic treatment of the Riemann–Roch Theorem.
Schmidt observed that their treatment works with little change in
arbitrary characteristic, and he used the Riemann–Roch Theorem to prove
that Artin’s zeta function satisfies a natural functional equation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p55.1" class="ltx_p">In 1936, Hasse proved Artin’s Riemann hypothesis in genus 1 using an
analogue of the theory of elliptic functions. Then he and Deuring noted
that to extend the proof to higher genus would require developing a
theory of correspondences between curves analogous to that developed by
Hurwitz and others. This work inspired Weil to study the fixed points
of the Frobenius correspondence, and led to his announcement in 1940 and
to his two great proofs in 1948 of Artin’s Riemann hypothesis for the
zeta function of an arbitrary curve and also to his proof of the
integrality of his analogue of Artin’s <math id="S1.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-functions of 1923 and 1930.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p56.1" class="ltx_p">First, in 1946, Weil carefully rebuilt the foundation of algebraic
geometry from scratch. Following in the footsteps of E. Noether, van
der Waerden, and Schmidt, Weil took a variable coefficient field of
arbitrary characteristic inside a fixed algebraically closed coordinate
field of infinite transcendence degree. Then he formed “abstract”
varieties by patching pieces of projective varieties, and said when
these varieties are “complete.” Finally, he developed a calculus of
cycles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p57" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p57.1" class="ltx_p">In 1948, Weil published two exciting monographs. In the first, he
reproved the Riemann–Roch theorem for (smooth complete) curves, a
theorem he regarded as fundamental (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">We79</a>, I, p. 562, top; II,
p. 541, top]</cite>). Then he developed an elementary theory of
correspondences between curves, which included Castelnuovo’s theorem of
1906 on the positive definiteness of the equivalence defect of a
correspondence. Of course, Castelnuovo’s proof was set over <math id="S1.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, but “its translation into abstract terms was essentially a routine
matter once the necessary techniques had been created,” as Weil put it
in his 1954 ICM talk. Finally, Weil derived the Riemann hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p58.1" class="ltx_p">In the second monograph, Weil established the abstract theory of Abelian
varieties. He constructed the Jacobian <math id="S1.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> of a curve <math id="S1.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>
by patching together copies of an open subset of the symmetric product
<math id="S1.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then taking a prime <math id="S1.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> different from the characteristic,
he constructed, out of the points on <math id="S1.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S1.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
an <math id="S1.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-adic representation of the ring of correspondences, equivalent to
the representation on the first cohomology group of <math id="S1.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, he
proved the positive definiteness of the trace of this representation,
reproved the Riemann hypothesis for the zeta function, and completed the
proof of his analogue of Artin’s conjectured integrality for
<math id="S1.p58.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-functions of number fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p59" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p59.1" class="ltx_p">Weil left open two questions: Do a curve and its Jacobian have the same
coefficient field? Is every Abelian variety projective? Both questions
were soon answered in the affirmative by Chow and Matsusaka. However,
there has remained some general interest in constructing nonprojective
varieties and in finding criteria for projectivity. Furthermore, Weil
was led in 1956 to study the general question of descent of the
coefficient field, and this work in turn inspired Grothendieck’s general
descent theory, which he sketched in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, no. 236]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p60" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p60.1" class="ltx_p">In 1949, Weil published his celebrated conjectures about the zeta
function of a variety of arbitrary dimension. Weil did not explicitly
explain these conjectures in terms of a hypothetical cohomology theory,
but such an explanation lies between the lines of his paper.
Furthermore, it was credited to him explicitly in Serre’s 1956 “Mexico
paper” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sr56</a>, p. 24]</cite> and in Grothendieck’s 1958 ICM talk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p61.1" class="ltx_p">In his talk, Grothendieck announced that he had found a new approach to
developing the desired “Weil cohomology.” He wrote: “it was
suggested to me by the connections between sheaf-theoretic cohomology
and cohomology of Galois groups on the one hand, and the classification
of unramified coverings of a variety on the other …, and by Serre’s
idea that a ‘reasonable’ algebraic principal fiber space …, if it is
not locally trivial, should become locally trivial on some covering
unramified over a given point.” This is the announcement of
Grothendieck topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p62.1" class="ltx_p">In 1960, Grothendieck and Dieudonné <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA I</a>, p. 6]</cite> listed the
titles of the chapters they planned to write. The last one, Chapter
XIII, is entitled “Cohomologie de Weil.” The next-to-last is
entitled “Schémas abéliens et schémas de Picard.” Earlier, at
the end of his 1958 ICM talk, Grothendieck had listed five open
problems; the fifth is to construct the Picard scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p63.1" class="ltx_p">In 1950, Weil published a remarkable note on Abelian varieties. For
each complete normal variety <math id="S1.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of any dimension in any characteristic,
he said there ought to be two associated Abelian varieties, the
“Picard” variety <math id="S1.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and the “Albanese” variety <math id="S1.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, with certain
properties, discussed just below. He explained he had complete proofs
for smooth complex <math id="S1.p63.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and “sketches” in general. Soon all was
proved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p64.1" class="ltx_p">Weil’s sketches rest on two criteria for linear equivalence, developed
in 1906 by Severi and reformulated in the 1950 note by Weil. He
announced proofs of them in 1952, and published the details in 1954.
For some more information, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za35</a>, p. 120]</cite> and
Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Remark 5.8. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>. In Weil’s commentaries on his ’54 paper, he
wrote: “Ever since 1949, I considered the construction of an algebraic
theory of the Picard variety as the task of greatest urgency in abstract
algebraic geometry.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p65.1" class="ltx_p">The properties are these. First, <math id="S1.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizes the linear
equivalence classes of divisors on <math id="S1.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. And there exists a map <math id="S1.p65.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to A</annotation></semantics></math>
that is “universal” in the sense that every map from <math id="S1.p65.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to an Abelian
variety factors through it. In his commentaries on the note, Weil
explained that <math id="S1.p65.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> had been introduced and named by Castelnuovo; so
historically speaking, it would be justified to name <math id="S1.p65.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> after him, but
it was better not to tamper with common usage. By contrast, Weil chose
to name <math id="S1.p65.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> after Albanese in order to honor his work in 1934 viewing
<math id="S1.p65.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of a symmetric power of <math id="S1.p65.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, although <math id="S1.p65.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> had been
introduced and studied in 1913 by Severi.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p66.1" class="ltx_p">Second, if <math id="S1.p66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian variety, then <math id="S1.p66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the Picard
variety of <math id="S1.p66.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>; so each of <math id="S1.p66.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p66.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the “dual” of the other. If
<math id="S1.p66.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary, then <math id="S1.p66.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p66.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are dual Abelian varieties; in fact,
the universal map <math id="S1.p66.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to A</annotation></semantics></math> induces the canonical isomorphism from the
Picard variety of <math id="S1.p66.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S1.p66.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p66.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve, then both <math id="S1.p66.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p66.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> coincide with the Jacobian of <math id="S1.p66.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and the universal map <math id="S1.p66.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to A</annotation></semantics></math> is
just the Abel map; in other words, the Jacobian is “autodual.” This
autoduality can be viewed as an algebro-geometric statement of Abel’s
theorem and its converse for integrals of the first kind. For some more
information, see Remarks <a href="#S5.Thmthm24" title="Remark 5.24. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.24</span></a>–<a href="#S5.Thmthm26" title="Remark 5.26. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.26</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p67" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p67.1" class="ltx_p">In 1951, Matsusaka gave the first algebraic construction of <math id="S1.p67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, he had to extend the ground field because he applied Weil’s
results: one of the equivalence criteria, and the construction of the
Jacobian. Both applications involve the “generic curve,” which is the
section of <math id="S1.p67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by a generic linear space of complementary dimension. In
1952, Matsusaka gave a different construction; it does not require
extending the ground field, but requires <math id="S1.p67.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be smooth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p68.1" class="ltx_p">Both constructions are like Castelnuovo’s in that they begin by
constructing a complete algebraic system of sufficiently positive
divisors, and then form the quotient modulo linear equivalence. To
parameterize the divisors, Matsusaka used the theory of “Chow
coordinates,” which was developed by Chow and van der Waerden in 1938
and refined by Chow contemporaneously. In 1952, Matsusaka also used
this theory to form the quotient. In the same paper, he gave the first
construction of <math id="S1.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, again using the Jacobian of the generic curve, but
he did not relate <math id="S1.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p69.1" class="ltx_p">In 1954, Chow published a construction of the Jacobian similar to
Matsusaka’s second construction of <math id="S1.p69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Chow had announced it in 1949,
and both Weil and Matsusaka had referred to it in the meantime. In
1955, Chow constructed <math id="S1.p69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p69.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> by a new procedure; he took the
“image” and the “trace” of the Jacobian of a generic curve.
Moreover, he showed that the universal map <math id="S1.p69.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to A</annotation></semantics></math> induces an
isomorphism from the Picard variety of <math id="S1.p69.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S1.p69.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p70" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p70.1" class="ltx_p">In a course at the University of Chicago, 1954–55, Weil gave a more
complete and elegant treatment, based on the “see-saw principle,” which
he adapted from Severi, and on his own Theorem of the Square and Theorem
of the cube. This treatment became the core of Lang’s 1959 book,
“Abelian.Varieties.” The idea is to construct <math id="S1.p70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> first using the
generic curve, and then to construct <math id="S1.p70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of <math id="S1.p70.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> modulo a
finite subgroup; thus there is no need for Chow coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p71" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p71.1" class="ltx_p">In 1959 and 1960, Nishi and Cartier independently established the
duality between <math id="S1.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in full generality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p72" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p72.1" class="ltx_p">Between 1952 and 1957, Rosenlicht published a remarkable series of
papers, which grew out of his 1950 Harvard thesis. It was supervised by
Zariski, who had studied Abelian functions and algebraic geometry with
Castelnuovo, Enriques, and Severi in Rome from 1921 to 1927. Notably
Rosenlicht generalized to a curve with arbitrary singularities the
notions of linear equivalence and differentials of the first kind. Then
he constructed a “generalized Jacobian” over <math id="S1.p72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> by
integrating and in arbitrary characteristic by patching. It is not an
Abelian variety, but an extension of the Jacobian of the normalized
curve by an affine algebraic group.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p73" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p73.1" class="ltx_p">Rosenlicht cited Severi’s 1947 monograph, “Funzioni Quasi Abeliane,”
where the generalized Jacobian was discussed for the first time, but
only for curves with double points. In turn, Severi traced the history
of the corresponding theory of quasi-Abelian functions back to Klein,
Picard, Poincaré, and Lefschetz.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p74.1" class="ltx_p">In 1956, Igusa established the compatibility of specializing a curve
with specializing its generalized Jacobian in arbitrary characteristic
when the general curve is smooth and the special curve has at most one
node. Igusa explained that, in 1952, Néron had studied the total
space of such a family of Jacobians, but had not explicitly analyzed the
special fiber. Igusa’s approach is, in spirit, like Castelnuovo’s,
Chow’s, and Matsusaka’s before him and Grothendieck’s after him. But
Grothendieck went considerably further: he proved compatibility with
specialization for a family of varieties of arbitrary dimension with
arbitrary singularities, both in equicharacteristic and in mixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p75" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p75.1" class="ltx_p">In 1960, Chevalley constructed a Picard variety for any normal variety
<math id="S1.p75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> using locally principal divisorial cycles. Cartier had already
focused on these cycles in his 1958 Paris thesis. But Chevalley said he
would call them simply “divisors,” and we follow suit, although they
are now commonly called “Cartier divisors.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p76" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p76.1" class="ltx_p">First, Chevalley constructed a “strict” Albanese variety; it is
universal for regular maps (morphisms) into Abelian varieties. Then he
took its Picard variety to be that of <math id="S1.p76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. He noted his Picard and
Albanese varieties need not be equal to those of a desingularization of
<math id="S1.p76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By contrast, Weil’s <math id="S1.p76.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p76.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> are birational invariants, and his
universal map <math id="S1.p76.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to A</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational map, which is defined wherever <math id="S1.p76.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is smooth. In 1962, Seshadri generalized Chevalley’s construction to a
variety with arbitrary singularities, thus recovering Rosenlicht’s
generalized Jacobian.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p77" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p77.1" class="ltx_p">Back in 1924, van der Waerden initiated the project of rebuilding the
whole foundation of algebraic geometry on the basis of commutative
algebra. His goal was to develop a rigorous theory of Schubert’s
enumerative geometry, as called for by Hilbert’s fifteenth problem. Van
der Waerden drew on Elimination Theory, Ideal Theory, and Field Theory
as developed in the schools of Kronecker, of Dedekind, and of Hilbert.
Van der Waerden originated, notably, the algebraic notion of
specialization as a replacement for the topological notion of
continuity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p78" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p78.1" class="ltx_p">In 1934, as Zariski wrote his book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za35</a>]</cite>, he lost confidence in
the clarity, precision, and completeness of the algebraic geometry of
his Italian teachers. He spent a couple of years studying the algebra
of E. Noether and Krull, and aimed to reduce singularities rigorously.
He introduced three algebraic tools: normalization, valuation theory,
and completion. He worked extensively with the rings obtained by
localizing affine coordinate domains at arbitrary primes over arbitrary
fields. And, in 1944, he put a topology on the set of all valuation
rings in a field of algebraic functions, and used the property that any
open covering has a finite subcovering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p79" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p79.1" class="ltx_p">In 1949, Weil saw that the “Zariski topology” can be put on his
abstract varieties, simplifying the old exposition and suggesting the
construction of new objects, such as locally trivial fiber spaces. In
his paper of 1950 on Abelian varieties, he noted that line bundles
correspond to linear equivalence classes of divisors, and predicted that
line bundles would play a role in the theory of quasi-Abelian functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p80" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p80.1" class="ltx_p">In 1955, Serre provided abstract algebraic geometry with a very powerful
new tool: sheaf cohomology. Given a variety equipped with the Zariski
topology, he assembled the local rings into the stalks of a
“structure” sheaf. Then he developed a cohomology theory of coherent
sheaves, analogous to the one that he and Cartan and Kodaira and Spencer
had just developed in complex analytic geometry, and had so successfully
applied to establish and to generalize previous work on complex
algebraic varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p81" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p81.1" class="ltx_p">About the same time, a general theory of abstract algebraic geometry was
developed by Chevalley. He did not use sheaves and cohomology, but did
work with what he called “schemes,” obtained by patching “affine
schemes”; an affine scheme is the set of rings obtained by localizing a
finitely generated domain over an arbitrary field. Nevertheless, he
soon returned to a more traditional theory of “varieties” when he
worked on the theory of algebraic groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p82" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p82.1" class="ltx_p">In January 1954, Chevalley lectured on schemes at Kyoto University. His
lectures inspired Nagata in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na56</a>]</cite> to generalize the theory by
replacing the coefficient field by a Dedekind domain. But Nagata used
Zariski’s term “model,” not Chevalley’s term “scheme.” Earlier, at
the 1950 ICM, Weil had recalled Kronecker’s dream of an algebraic
geometry over the integers; however, Nagata did not cite Weil’s talk,
and likely was not motivated by it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p83" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p83.1" class="ltx_p">In the fall of 1955, Chevalley lectured on schemes over fields at the
Séminaire Cartan–Chevalley, and Grothendieck was there. By February
1956 (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">CS01</a>, p. 32]</cite>), he was patching the spectra of arbitrary
Noetherian rings, and studying the cohomology of Cartier’s
“quasi-coherent” sheaves. There is good reason for the added generality:
nilpotents allow better handling of higher-order infinitesimal
deformations, of inseparability in positive characteristic, and of
passage to the completion; quasi-coherent sheaves have the technical
convenience of coherent sheaves without their cumbersome finiteness. By
October 1958 (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">CS01</a>, p. 63]</cite>), Grothendieck and Dieudonné had
begun the gigantic program of writing EGA — rebuilding once again
the foundation of algebraic geometry in order to provide a more flexible
framework, more powerful methods, and a more refined theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p84" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p84.1" class="ltx_p">Also in 1958, there appeared two other papers, which discuss objects
similar to Chevalley’s schemes: Kähler published a 400 page
foundational monograph, which introduced general base changes, and Chow
and Igusa published a four-page note, which proved the Künneth Formula
for coherent sheaves. The two works are mentioned briefly in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">CS01</a>, p. 101]</cite>, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA I</a>, p. 8]</cite> and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, p. 6]</cite>;
however, they seem to have had little or no influence on Grothendieck.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p85" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p85.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in 1961–1962, Grothendieck constructed the Hilbert scheme
and the Picard scheme. The construction is a technical masterpiece,
showing the tremendous power of Grothendieck’s new tools. In
particular, a central role is played by the theory of flatness. It was
introduced by Serre in 1956 as a formal device for use in comparing
algebraic functions and analytic functions. Then Grothendieck developed
the theory extensively, for he recognized that flatness is the technical
condition that best expresses continuity across a family.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p86" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p86.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 221-1]</cite> saw Hilbert schemes as “destined to
replace” Chow coordinates. However, he <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 221-7]</cite> had to
appeal to the theory of Chow coordinates for a key finiteness result: in
projective space, the subvarieties of given degree form a bounded
family. A few years later, Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Lects. 14, 15]</cite> gave a
simple direct proof of this finiteness; his proof introduced an
important new tool, now known as “Castelnuovo–Mumford” regularity.
In a slightly modified form, this tool plays a central role in the
proofs of the finiteness theorems for the Picard scheme, which are
addressed in Chapter 6 below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p87" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p87.1" class="ltx_p">In spirit, Grothendieck’s construction of the Picard scheme is
like Castelnuovo’s and Matsusaka’s. He began with the component
<math id="S1.p87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hilbert scheme determined by a sufficiently positive
divisor. Then he formed the quotient; in fact, he did so twice for
diversity. First, he used “quasi-sections”; second, and more
elegantly, he used the Hilbert scheme of <math id="S1.p87.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p88" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p88.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck’s definition of the two schemes is yet a greater
contribution than his construction of them. He defined them by their
functors of points. These schemes are universal parameter spaces; so
they receive a map from a scheme <math id="S1.p88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> just when <math id="S1.p88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizes a
family of subschemes or of invertible sheaves, respectively, and this
map is unique.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p89" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p89.1" class="ltx_p">What is a universal family? The answer seems obvious when we use
schemes. But Chow coordinates parameterize positive cycles, not
subschemes. And even in the analytic theory of the Picard variety <math id="S1.p89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
there was some question about the sense in which <math id="S1.p89.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizes
divisor classes. Indeed, the American Journal of Math., <span id="S1.p89.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">74</span>
(1952), contains three papers on <math id="S1.p89.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. First, Igusa constructed <math id="S1.p89.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, but
left universality unsettled. Then Weil and Chow settled it with
different arguments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p90" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p90.1" class="ltx_p">A functor of points, or “representable functor,” is not an arbitrary
contravariant functor from schemes to sets. It is determined locally,
so is a sheaf. But it suffices to represent a sheaf locally, as the
patching is determined. Thus to construct the Hilbert scheme, the first
step is to check that the Hilbert functor is, in fact, a sheaf for the
Zariski topology, that is, a “Zariski sheaf.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p91" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p91.1" class="ltx_p">The naive Picard functor is not a Zariski sheaf. So the first step is
to localize it, or form the associated sheaf. This time, the Zariski
topology is not fine enough. However, a representable functor is an
fpqc sheaf by a main theorem of Grothendieck’s descent theory. In
practice, it is enough to localize for the étale Grothendieck topology
or for the fppf, and these localizations are more convenient to work
with. The localizations of the Picard functor are discussed in Chapter
2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p92" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p92.1" class="ltx_p">The next step is to cover the localized Picard functor by representable
Zariski open subfunctors. This step is elementary. But it is
technically involved, more so than any other argument in these notes.
It is carried out in the proof of the main result,
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Each subfunctor is represented by a quotient of
an open subscheme of the Hilbert scheme. Thus the Picard scheme is
constructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p93" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p93.1" class="ltx_p">In sum, Grothendieck’s method of representable functors is like
Descartes’s method of coordinate axes: simple, yet powerful. Here is
one hallmark of genius!</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p94" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p94.1" class="ltx_p">In the notes that follow, our primary aim is to develop in detail most
of Grothendieck’s original theory of the Picard scheme basically by
filling in his sketch in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>]</cite>. Our secondary aim is to review in
brief much of the rest of the theory developed by Grothendieck and by
others. We review the secondary material in a series of scattered
remarks. The remarks refer to each other and to the primary discussion,
but the primary discussion never refers to the remarks. So the remarks
may be safely ignored in a first reading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p95" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p95.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, the primary discussion does not develop Grothendieck’s
method of “relative representability.” Indeed, the details would take
us too far afield. On the other hand, were we to use the method, we
could obtain certain existence theorems and finiteness theorems in
greater generality by reducing to the cases that we do handle.
Consequently, in Sections 4–6, a number of results just assume the
Picard scheme exists, rather than assume hypotheses guaranteeing it
does. However, we do discuss the method and its applications in
Remark <a href="#S4.Thmthm18" title="Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18</span></a> and in other remarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p96" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p96.1" class="ltx_p">These notes also contain many exercises, which call for working
examples and constructing proofs. Unlike the remarks, these exercises
are an integral part of the primary discussion, which not only is
enhanced by them, but also is based in part on them. Furthermore, the
exercises are designed to foster comprehension. The answers involve no
new concepts or techniques. The exercises are meant to be easy; if a
part seems to be hard, then some review and reflection may be in
order. However, all the answers are worked out in detail in
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Answers to all the exercises ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p97" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p97.1" class="ltx_p">These notes assume familiarity with the basic algebraic geometry
developed in Chapters II and III of Hartshorne’s popular
textbook <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>]</cite>, and assume familiarity, but to a lesser extent,
with the foundational material developed in Grothendieck and
Dieudonné’s monumental reference books <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA I</a>]</cite> to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>]</cite>. In
addition, these notes assume familiarity with basic Grothendieck
topology, descent theory, and Hilbert-scheme theory, such as that
explained on pp. 129–147, 199–201, and 215–221 in Bosch,
Lütkebohmert, and Raynaud’s welcome survey book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>]</cite>; this
material and more was introduced by Grothendieck in three Bourbaki
talks, nos. 190, 212, and 221, which were reprinted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>]</cite> and
are still worth reading. Of course, when specialized results are used
below, precise references are provided.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p98" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p98.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout these notes, we work only with locally Noetherian schemes,
just as Grothendieck did in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>]</cite>. Shortly afterward,
Grothendieck promoted the elimination of this restriction, through a
limiting process that reduces the general case to the Noetherian case.
Ever since, it has been common to make this reduction. However, the
process is elementary and straightforward. Using it here would only be
distracting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p99" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p99.1" class="ltx_p">Given two locally Noetherian schemes lying over a third, the (fibered)
product is not necessarily locally Noetherian. Consequently, there are
minor technical difficulties in working with the fpqc topology on the
category of locally Noetherian schemes. On the other hand, in practice,
there is no need to use the fpqc topology. Therefore, its use has been
eliminated from these notes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p100" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p100.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout, we work with a <span id="S1.p100.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fixed</span> separated map of finite type</p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p100.2" class="ltx_p">For convenience, when given an <math id="S1.p100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S1.p100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}:=X\times_{S}T" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}:=X\times_{S}T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p100.3" class="ltx_p">and denote the projection by <math id="S1.p100.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, when given a
<math id="S1.p100.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S1.p100.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and given quasi-coherent sheaves <math id="S1.p100.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p100.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p100.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p100.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the pullback sheaves by <math id="S1.p100.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}|T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}|T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p100.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S1.p100.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}|X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p100.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p101" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p101.1" class="ltx_p">Given an <math id="S1.p101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S1.p101.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we call an <math id="S1.p101.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S1.p101.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to P</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S1.p101.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S1.p101.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">point</span>
of <math id="S1.p101.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and we denote the set of all <math id="S1.p101.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points by <math id="S1.p101.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S1.p101.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
varies, the sets <math id="S1.p101.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T)</annotation></semantics></math> form a contravariant functor on the category
of <math id="S1.p101.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes, called the <span id="S1.p101.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functor of points</span> of <math id="S1.p101.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p102" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p102.1" class="ltx_p">Section 2 introduces and compares the four common relative Picard
functors, the likely candidates for the functor of points of the Picard
scheme. They are simply the functor <math id="S1.p102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\mapsto\Pic(X_{T})\big/\Pic(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\mapsto\Pic(X_{T})\big/\Pic(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and
its associated sheaves in the Zariski topology, the étale topology,
and the fppf topology. Section 3 treats relative effective (Cartier)
divisors on <math id="S1.p102.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> and the relation of linear equivalence. We prove
these divisors are parameterized by an open subscheme of the Hilbert
scheme of <math id="S1.p102.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we study the subscheme parameterizing
the divisors whose fibers are linearly equivalent, and prove it is of
the form <math id="S1.p102.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p102.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a certain coherent sheaf on <math id="S1.p102.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p103" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p103.1" class="ltx_p">Section 4 begins the study of the Picard scheme <math id="S1.p103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> itself.
Notably, we prove Grothendieck’s main theorem: <math id="S1.p103.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists when
<math id="S1.p103.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat and its geometric fibers are integral.
Then we work out Mumford’s example showing the necessity of the
integrality hypothesis. Section 5 studies <math id="S1.p103.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the
union of the connected components of the identity element of the fibers
of <math id="S1.p103.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we compute the tangent space at the
identity of each fiber. It is remarkable how much we can prove <span id="S1.p103.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">formally</span> about <math id="S1.p103.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Section 6 proves the two deeper
finiteness theorems. They concern <math id="S1.p103.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of the
points with a multiple in <math id="S1.p103.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p103.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose
points represent the invertible sheaves with a given Hilbert polynomial
<math id="S1.p103.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p104" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p104.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, there are two appendices. Appendix A contains detailed answers
to all the exercises. Appendix B develops basic divisorial intersection
theory, which is used freely throughout Section 6. The treatment is
short, simple, and elementary.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>The several Picard functors</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first job is to identify a likely candidate for the functor of
points of the Picard scheme. In fact, there are several reasonable such
Picard functors, and each one is more likely to be representable than
the preceding. In this section, they all are formally introduced and
compared.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">absolute Picard functor</span> <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the functor from the
category of (locally Noetherian) <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to the category of
abelian groups defined by the formula</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(T):=\Pic(X_{T})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(T):=\Pic(X_{T}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The absolute Picard functor is a “prepared presheaf” in this
sense: given any family of <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}\bigl(\textstyle\coprod T_{i}\bigr)=\prod\Pic_{X}(T_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∐</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∏</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}\bigl(\textstyle\coprod T_{i}\bigr)=\prod\Pic_{X}(T_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, given a covering family <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{T_{i}\to T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{T_{i}\to T\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Zariski topology,
the étale topology, or any other Grothendieck topology on the category
of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes, there is no harm, when we consider the sheaf associated
to <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, in working simply with the single map <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:=\coprod T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∐</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:=\coprod T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. And doing so lightens the notation, making for
easier reading. Therefore, we do so throughout, calling <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> simply
a <span id="S2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">covering</span> in the given topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The absolute Picard functor <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is never a separated presheaf in
the Zariski topology. Indeed, take an <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> that carries an
invertible sheaf <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial. (For
example, take <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=\mathbf{P}^{1}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=\mathbf{P}^{1}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the diagonal
map <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to X\times X</annotation></semantics></math> induces a section <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="gf_{T}=1_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">gf_{T}=1_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial.) Now, there exists a Zariski covering
<math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> such that the pullback <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}|T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}|T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial; here, <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
simply the disjoint union of the subsets in a suitable ordinary open
covering of <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. So the pullback <math id="S2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{T^{\prime}}\mathcal{N}|X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{T^{\prime}}\mathcal{N}|X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial too.
Thus <math id="S2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero element whose restriction is zero in
<math id="S2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">According to descent theory, every representable functor is a sheaf in
the Zariski topology  — in fact, in the étale and fppf
topologies as well. Therefore, the absolute Picard functor <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is never
representable. So, in the hope of obtaining a representable functor
that differs as little as possible from <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we now define a
sequence of three successively more promising relative functors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative Picard functor</span> <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(T):=\Pic(X_{T})\big/\Pic(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(T):=\Pic(X_{T})\big/\Pic(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Denote its associated sheaves in the Zariski, étale, and fppf
topologies by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar},\ \Pic_{(X/S)\et},\ \Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar},\ \Pic_{(X/S)\et},\ \Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now have a sequence of five Picard functors, and each one maps
naturally into the next. So each one maps naturally into any of its
successors. If the latter functor is one of the three just displayed,
then it is a sheaf in the indicated topology; in fact, it is the sheaf
associated to any one of its predecessors, and the map between them is
the natural map from a presheaf to its associated sheaf, as is easy to
check. In particular, the three displayed sheaves are the sheaves
associated to the absolute Picard functor <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as to the
relative Picard functor <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a priori a sheaf, it is remarkable that it is
representable so often in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, for every <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, each <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of
<math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, or element of <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fpqc}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fpqc</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fpqc}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, is
represented by an invertible sheaf <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
fppf-covering <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, there must
be an fppf-covering <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the two pullbacks of
<math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic  — or to put it informally, the
restrictions of <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must agree on a covering of the overlaps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, a second such sheaf <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the
same <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point if and only if there is an fppf-covering <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}^{\prime}\to T_{1}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}^{\prime}\to T_{1}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the pullbacks of <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T_{1}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T_{1}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are
isomorphic. Technically, this condition includes the preceding one,
which concerns the case where <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}=T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}=T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}=\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}=\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must represent the same <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point as itself. Of course, similar
considerations apply to the Picard functors for the Zariski and étale
topologies as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Spec(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Spec(A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a local
ring, show that the natural maps are isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{X/S}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{X/S}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A):=\Pic_{X}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A):=\Pic_{X}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(A):=\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(A):=\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so forth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin local with algebraically closed residue field. Show</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraically closed field <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Show</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(k)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(k)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Show that the natural map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\to\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\to\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">need not be an isomorphism. Specifically, take <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be the following
curve in the real projective plane:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:u^{2}+v^{2}+w^{2}=0\text{ in }\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{R}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:u^{2}+v^{2}+w^{2}=0\text{ in }\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-points, but over the complex numbers <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon X_{\mathbb{C}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon X_{\mathbb{C}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Show that <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> defines an element of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is not in the image of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this section is the following comparison theorem.
It identifies two useful conditions: the first guarantees that the first
three relative functors can be viewed as subfunctors of the fourth;
together, the two conditions guarantee that all four functors coincide.
The second condition has three successively weaker forms. Before we can
prove the theorem, we must develop some theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Comparison)<span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally; that is, for any
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the comorphism of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism, <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1.<span id="S2.Thmthm5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Then the natural maps are injections:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">2.<span id="S2.Thmthm5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> All three maps are isomorphisms if also <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a
section; the latter two maps are isomorphisms if also <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section
locally in the Zariski topology; and the last map is isomorphism if also
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section locally in the étale topology.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally. Using
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, show that its four functors have the same
geometric points; in other words, for every algebraically closed field
<math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> containing the residue class field of a point of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points
of all four functors are, in a natural way, the same. Show, in fact,
that these <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points are just the elements of <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">What if <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessarily hold
universally?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the functor <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\mapsto f^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\mapsto f^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully-faithful from the category <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of locally free
sheaves of finite rank on <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to that on <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The essential image is
formed by the sheaves <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that </span>(i)<span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the image
<math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and </span>(ii)<span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the natural map <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}f_{*}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}f_{*}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">(Compare with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 21.13.2]</cite>.) For any <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a string of three natural isomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}\otimes f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}\otimes f_{*}f^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}f^{*}\mathcal{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}\otimes f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}\otimes f_{*}f^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}f^{*}\mathcal{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.7.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">The first isomorphism arises by tensor product with the comorphism of
<math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>; this comorphism is an isomorphism by hypothesis. The second
isomorphism arises from the identification <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=f^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=f^{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
third arises from the projection formula.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, also <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence,
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7.1</span></a>) yields an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}f^{*}\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}f^{*}\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, since <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are locally free of finite rank, the
natural map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\to\SHom(f^{*}\mathcal{N},\,f^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\SHom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\to\SHom(f^{*}\mathcal{N},\,f^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">is an isomorphism locally, so globally. Hence there is an isomorphism
of groups</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Hom(f^{*}\mathcal{N},\,f^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathcal{N},\,\mathcal{N}^{\prime})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Hom(f^{*}\mathcal{N},\,f^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.4.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\mapsto f^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\mapsto f^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully-faithful.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, the essential image consists of the sheaves <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> that are
isomorphic to those of the form <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given such an <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an isomorphism <math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\mathcal{M}\simeq\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7.1</span></a>). Hence <math id="S2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}f_{*}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}f_{*}\mathcal{M}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism locally, so globally; thus
(i) and (ii) hold. Conversely, if (i) and (ii) hold, then <math id="S2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is,
by definition, in the essential image.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Part 1 of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, represent <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by an invertible
sheaf <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exist an fppf covering <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>
and an isomorphism <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible
sheaf <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> implies that
<math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, so
<math id="S2.p13.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S2.p13.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.p13.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
is invertible and the natural map <math id="S2.p13.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{*}_{T}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{T}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is
an isomorphism, as both statements hold after pullback via <math id="S2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is faithfully flat. Therefore, <math id="S2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The rest is formal. Indeed, take the last injection, and form the
associated sheaves in the Zariski topology. This operation is exact by
general (Grothendieck) topology, and <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> remains the
same, as it is already a Zariski sheaf. Thus <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Alternatively, we can avoid the use of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> by starting
from the fact that <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sheaf associated to
<math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than to <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. This way, we may assume <math id="S2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p15.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\smash{T^{\prime}}*}p_{X}^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> because
<math id="S2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally. We now proceed
just as before.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="fg=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">fg=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme,
and <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> a sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigidification</span> of <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the choice of an isomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>,
assuming one exists.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Form the
group of isomorphism classes of pairs <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L},u)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
invertible sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidification of <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then this group is carried isomorphically onto <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> by
the homomorphism <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S2.Thmthm9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mathcal{L},u):=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathcal{L},u):=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, represent <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by an
invertible sheaf <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{M}\otimes(f_{T}^{*}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{M})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{M}\otimes(f_{T}^{*}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{M})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> too represents <math id="S2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Also
<math id="S2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}=g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}\otimes g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}=g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}\otimes g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now,
<math id="S2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="fg=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">fg=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the natural isomorphism <math id="S2.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}\otimes(g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M})^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{\mathcal{}}O_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}\otimes(g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M})^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{\mathcal{}}O_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a <math id="S2.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidification of
<math id="S2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, let <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L},u)</annotation></semantics></math> represent an element of
its kernel. Then there exist an invertible sheaf <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and an
isomorphism <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:=g_{T}^{*}v\circ u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:=g_{T}^{*}v\circ u</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, a map of pairs is just a
map <math id="S2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of the first components such that <math id="S2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}_{T}w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}_{T}w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with
the two <math id="S2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidifications. So <math id="S2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon(\mathcal{L},u)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}(f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon(\mathcal{L},u)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}(f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N},w)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{T}w\colon(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}},1)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}(f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{T}w\colon(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}},1)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}(f^{*}_{T}\mathcal{N},w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S2.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds universally. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> an invertible
sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidification of <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every
automorphism of the pair <math id="S2.Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L},u)</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">An automorphism of <math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L},u)</annotation></semantics></math> is just an automorphism <math id="S2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}v\circ u\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}v\circ u\colon\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}_{T}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="S2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. But then <math id="S2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}v=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Now,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\Hom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L})=\textup{H}^{0}(\SHom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L}))=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}})=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{T});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\Hom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L})=\textup{H}^{0}(\SHom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L}))=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}})=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{T});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">the middle equation holds since the natural map <math id="S2.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\!X_{T}}\to\SHom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\!X_{T}}\to\SHom(\mathcal{L},\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally an isomorphism, so globally one, and the
last equation holds since <math id="S2.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. But
<math id="S2.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}v=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Part 2 of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Owing to Part 1, it suffices to prove
that every <math id="S2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Represent <math id="S2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by a <math id="S2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}\in\Pic_{X/S}(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}\in\Pic_{X/S}(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is an fppf covering. Then there is an fppf covering <math id="S2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the two pullbacks of <math id="S2.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are
equal. We may assume <math id="S2.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S2.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated
for the fppf topology, again owing to Part 1.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Owing to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, we may represent <math id="S2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by a pair
<math id="S2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an invertible sheaf on <math id="S2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="S2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidification of <math id="S2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, on <math id="S2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there
is an isomorphism <math id="S2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the pullback of <math id="S2.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> via the first
projection onto the pullback via the second.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the three projections <math id="S2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}\to X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}\to X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pullback of <math id="S2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> via the projection to the <math id="S2.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th
and <math id="S2.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th factors. Then <math id="S2.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{13}^{\prime-1}v^{\prime}_{23}v^{\prime}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>13</mn><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>23</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{13}^{\prime-1}v^{\prime}_{23}v^{\prime}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
automorphism of the pullback of <math id="S2.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> via the first projection
<math id="S2.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}\to X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}\times T^{\prime}}\to X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, owing to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, this
automorphism is trivial. Hence <math id="S2.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}^{\prime},u^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> descends to a pair
<math id="S2.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L},u)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S2.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">The rest is formal. Indeed, suppose that there is a Zariski covering
<math id="S2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a section. Then, by the above,
<math id="S2.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}|T^{\prime}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}|T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}|T^{\prime}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}|T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by general
(Grothendieck) topology, <math id="S2.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>
since both source and target are sheaves in the Zariski topology and a map
of sheaves is an isomorphism if it is so locally. Similarly,
<math id="S2.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section locally
in the étale topology.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is another way to prove Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. This way is more
sophisticated, and yields more information, which we won’t need.
Here is the idea.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Ex. III, 4.5, p. 224]</cite> that, for any ringed space <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a natural isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.E1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(R)=\textup{H}^{1}(R,\mathcal{O}_{\!R}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(R)=\textup{H}^{1}(R,\mathcal{O}_{\!R}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Now, given any <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, form the presheaf <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\mapsto\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T^{\prime}},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime}}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\mapsto\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T^{\prime}},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime}}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Its associated sheaf is
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. III, 8.1, p. 250]</cite> simply <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>R</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(T)=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(T)=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the Leray spectral sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gd58</a>, Thm. II, 4.17.1, p. 201]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1g.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{E}_{2}^{pq}:=\textup{H}^{p}(T,\textup{R}^{q}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\Longrightarrow\textup{H}^{p+q}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>E</mtext><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>q</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{E}_{2}^{pq}:=\textup{H}^{p}(T,\textup{R}^{q}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\Longrightarrow\textup{H}^{p+q}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and form its exact sequence of terms of low degree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gd58</a>, Thm. I, 4.5.1,
p. 82]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\\
\to\textup{H}^{2}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{2}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>R</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\\
\to\textup{H}^{2}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{2}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally, then <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the beginning
of (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.3</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If also <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> induces, for each <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, a left
inverse of the map <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{H}^{p}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{p}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{p}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{p}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. So the latter is injective. Hence,
<math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>
is surjective. Thus <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding argument works for the étale and fppf topologies
too with little change. First of all, consider the functor</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T):=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T):=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be an indeterminate. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by the
<math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}:=\Spec(\mathcal{O}_{\!S}[u,u^{-1}])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}:=\Spec(\mathcal{O}_{\!S}[u,u^{-1}]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Indeed, giving an <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as giving an
<math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(S,\mathcal{O}_{\!S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(S,\mathcal{O}_{\!S})</annotation></semantics></math>-homomorphism from <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(S,\mathcal{O}_{\!S})[u,u^{-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(S,\mathcal{O}_{\!S})[u,u^{-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T})</annotation></semantics></math>, and giving such a
homomorphism is the same as assigning to <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> a unit in <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(T,\mathcal{O}_{\!T})</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, since <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is representable, it is a sheaf for the
étale and fppf topologies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck’s generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 190-16]</cite> of Hilbert’s Theorem
90 asserts the formula</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(T)=\textup{H}^{1}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(T)=\textup{H}^{1}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in either the étale or fppf
topology. The proof is simple, and similar to the proof of
(<a href="#S2.E1a" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.1</span></a>). The <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed as a Čech group. And,
essentially by definition, for a covering <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>, a Čech cocycle
with values in <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> amounts to descent data on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The data is effective by descent theory, and the resulting
sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible since the covering is faithfully flat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In the present context, the exact sequence (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.3</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\\
\to\textup{H}^{2}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{2}(X_{T},\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}).$$" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>R</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>H</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\textup{H}^{1}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{1}(X_{T},\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\\
\to\textup{H}^{2}(T,f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})\to\textup{H}^{2}(X_{T},\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}).$$</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.11.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the proof of (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.2</span></a>) yields, for example in the fppf
topology,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)=\textup{H}^{0}(T,\textup{R}^{1}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally, then it follows from
the definitions that <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}=\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\smash{T}*}\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}=\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the beginning
of (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.4</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> if also <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section. As before, the rest of
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> follows formally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The étale group <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})</annotation></semantics></math> was studied extensively by Grothendieck
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dix</a>, pp. 46–188]</cite>. He showed that it gives one of two significant
generalizations of the Brauer group of central simple algebras over a
field. The other is the group of Azumaya algebras on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. He denoted
the latter by <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Br(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Br(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and the former by <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Br^{\prime}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Br^{\prime}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dix</a>, pp. 127–128]</cite>, if <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds
universally, then (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.4</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T)\to\Br^{\prime}(T)\to\Br^{\prime}(X_{T});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\Pic(T)\to\Pic(X_{T})\to\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T)\to\Br^{\prime}(T)\to\Br^{\prime}(X_{T});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.2" class="ltx_p">in particular, the obstruction to representing an element of
<math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> by an invertible sheaf on <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by an
element of <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Br^{\prime}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Br^{\prime}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps to <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Br^{\prime}(X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="undefined">\Br</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Br^{\prime}(X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Using the smoothness of <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, Grothendieck
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dix</a>, p. 180]</cite> proved the natural homomorphisms are
isomorphisms from the étale groups <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{p}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{p}(T,\mathbb{G}_{\textup{m}})</annotation></semantics></math> to the
corresponding fppf groups. Hence, if <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds universally, then it follows from (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Remark 2.11. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11.4</span></a>) via the Five Lemma
that, whether <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section locally in the étale topology or not,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm11.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm11.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, when a discussion is set in the greatest possible
generality, it is common to work with <math id="S2.Thmthm11.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> and call it
<span id="S2.Thmthm11.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> Picard functor.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Relative effective divisors</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck constructed the Picard scheme by taking a suitable family
of effective divisors and forming the quotient modulo linear
equivalence. This section develops the basic theory of these notions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effective divisors.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A closed subscheme <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is called an <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">effective (Cartier)
divisor</span> if its ideal <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Given an <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(nD):=\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{I}^{\otimes-n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(nD):=\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{I}^{\otimes-n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. So the inclusion <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> yields, via tensor product with <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, an injection
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, which, in turn, corresponds to a
global section of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. This section is not arbitrary since
it corresponds to an injection. Sections corresponding to injections
are termed <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regular</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, given an arbitrary invertible sheaf <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subset of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting
of the regular sections, those corresponding to injections <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. And let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of effective
divisors <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is, in some way, isomorphic to <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. For historical reasons, <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complete
linear system</span> associated to <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> (but <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math> needn’t be a <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> isn’t integral).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the conditions of (<a href="#S3.Thmthm1" title="3.1Effective divisors. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), establish a canonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}|\mathcal{L}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}|\mathcal{L}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative effective divisor</span> on <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor
<math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> that is <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subscheme, <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in D</annotation></semantics></math> a point, and <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>
its image. Then the following statements are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The subscheme </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a relative effective divisor at
</span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (that is, in a neighborhood of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The schemes </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-flat at </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and the fiber </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
is an effective divisor on </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> at </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The scheme </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-flat at </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and the subscheme </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is cut out at </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by one element that is regular (a nonzerodivisor)
on the fiber </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For convenience, set <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\mathcal{O}_{S,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\mathcal{O}_{S,s}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote its residue field by
<math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, set <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X,x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:=\mathcal{O}_{D,x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:=\mathcal{O}_{D,x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes_{A}k=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes_{A}k=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s},x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (i), and let’s prove (ii). By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective
divisor at <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. So there is a regular element <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math> that generates
the ideal of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiplication by <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> defines a short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to B\to B\to C\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to B\to B\to C\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">In turn, this sequence induces the following exact sequence:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)\to B\otimes k\to B\otimes k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)\to B\otimes k\to B\otimes k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat at <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k\to B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k\to B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. Its image is the ideal of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is
surjective. This map is given by multiplication by <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> lies in
the maximal ideal of <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely
generated <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Hence, <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Nakayama’s lemma.
Therefore, by the local criterion of flatness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 1</a>, Thm. 5.6,
p. 98]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">OB72</a>, Thm. 6.1, p. 73]</cite>, <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat; in other
words, <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat at <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (ii) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (ii). To prove (iii), denote the ideal of <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and
that of <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Take an element <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in I</annotation></semantics></math> whose image <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; such a <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> exists because <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
effective divisor at <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis. For the same reason, <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
regular. It remains to prove <math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to I\to B\to C\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to I\to B\to C\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat. Hence the map <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\otimes k\to B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\otimes k\to B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> is
injective. Its image is <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is generated by <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So the image
of <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> generates it. Hence, by Nakayama’s lemma, <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>
generates <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (iii) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (iii). To prove (i), again denote the ideal of <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by an element <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> whose image <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> is regular. We have to prove <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is regular and <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The exact sequence <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to I\to B\to C\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to I\to B\to C\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> yields this one:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)\to I\otimes k\to B\otimes k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)\to\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)\to I\otimes k\to B\otimes k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">The last map is injective for the following reason. Since <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=Bb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=Bb</annotation></semantics></math>,
multiplication by <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> induces a surjection <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to I</annotation></semantics></math>, so a surjection
<math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k\to I\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k\to I\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the composition</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k\to I\otimes k\to B\otimes k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k\to I\otimes k\to B\otimes k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">It is given by multiplication by <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, so is injective because <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
regular. Hence <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}I\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}I\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\otimes k\to B\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\otimes k\to B\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math> is
injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By hypothesis, <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat. So <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(B,k)=0.</annotation></semantics></math> Hence the
exactness of (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4.1</span></a>) implies <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Tor</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tor^{A}_{1}(C,k)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by the
local criterion, <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat and <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to I\to B\to C\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to I\to B\to C\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, also
<math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by the exact sequence <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to K\to B\to I\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to K\to B\to I\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to K\otimes k\to B\otimes k\to I\otimes k\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to K\otimes k\to B\otimes k\to I\otimes k\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">is exact since <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-flat. But <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\otimes k\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}I\otimes k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\otimes k\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}I\otimes k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\otimes k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\otimes k=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Nakayama’s lemma. But <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the kernel of
multiplication by <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is regular. Thus (i) holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be relative effective divisors on <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math>
their sum. Show <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative effective divisor too.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define a functor <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Div_{X/S}(T):=\{\,\text{relative effective divisors $D$
on $X_{T}/T$}\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>relative effective divisors </mtext><mi>D</mi><mtext> on </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}(T):=\{\,\text{relative effective divisors $D$
on $X_{T}/T$}\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Note <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed a functor. Namely, given a relative
effective divisor <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> and an arbitrary <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have to see the <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-flat closed subscheme <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{T^{\prime}}\subset X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{T^{\prime}}\subset X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor. So let <math id="S3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ideal of
<math id="S3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-flat, <math id="S3.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X_{T}}^{*}\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X_{T}}^{*}\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the ideal of
<math id="S3.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. But, since <math id="S3.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, so is <math id="S3.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X_{T}}^{*}\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X_{T}}^{*}\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S3.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (relative) effective divisor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable by an open subscheme <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Hilbert scheme
<math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=\IHilb_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=\IHilb_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset X\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset X\times H</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal
(closed) subscheme, and <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon W\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon W\to H</annotation></semantics></math> the projection. Let <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
set of points <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W</annotation></semantics></math> at which <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor. Plainly <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>
is open in <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=q(W-V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=q(W-V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is closed because <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper. Set <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U:=H-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U:=H-Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open, and <math id="S3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective
divisor in <math id="S3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times U</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, since <math id="S3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, <math id="S3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative
effective divisor in <math id="S3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times U/U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times U/U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to show that <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. So let <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an
<math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> a relative effective divisor. By the
universal property of the pair <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique map
<math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon T\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon T\to H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{X}^{-1}W=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{X}^{-1}W=D</annotation></semantics></math>. We have to show that <math id="S3.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> factors
through <math id="S3.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, the fiber <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor since it is
obtained by base change (or owing to Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). But
<math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}=W_{g(t)}\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}=W_{g(t)}\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the residue field of <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> too is a divisor, as a field extension is faithfully flat.
Hence, since <math id="S3.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-flat, <math id="S3.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is, by
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, a relative effective divisor along the fiber over
<math id="S3.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. So, since <math id="S3.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is open, <math id="S3.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> factors
through <math id="S3.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat and is projective Zariski locally over <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume its fibers are curves, that is, of <span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pure</span> dimension 1. Given
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the functor whose <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are the
relative effective divisors <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> with fibers <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Show the <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are representable by open and closed subschemes
of finite type <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}\subset\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}\subset\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are disjoint and
cover.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be the subscheme where <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. Show
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-fold <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-fibered product. Show there is a natural
<math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon X_{0}^{m}\to\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon X_{0}^{m}\to\IDiv_{X/S}^{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which is given on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points
by <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\Gamma_{1},\dotsc,\Gamma_{m})=\sum\Gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\Gamma_{1},\dotsc,\Gamma_{m})=\sum\Gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the map <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Exercise 3.8. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. Plainly <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
is compatible with permuting the factors of <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
factors through the symmetric product <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
induces an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}^{(m)}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\IDiv_{X_{0}/S}^{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}^{(m)}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\IDiv_{X_{0}/S}^{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This isomorphism is treated in detail in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">De73</a>, Prp. 6.3.9,
p. 437]</cite> and in outline in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>, Prp. 3, p. 254]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The module <math id="S3.Thmthm10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, and let <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a coherent <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>-module flat over <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.7.6]</cite> that there
exist a coherent <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and an isomorphism of functors
in the quasi-coherent <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon\SHom(\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{N})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon\SHom(\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{N})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.10.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The pair <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{Q},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{Q},q)</annotation></semantics></math> is unique, up to unique isomorphism, and by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.7.9]</cite>, forming it commutes with changing the base, in
particular, with localizing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and assume <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Spec(\mathcal{O}_{S,s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Spec(\mathcal{O}_{S,s})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the following
conditions are equivalent:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is free (or equivalently,
projective);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the functor <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\mapsto f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\mapsto f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})</annotation></semantics></math> is right
exact;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the natural map is an isomorphism, <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}(\mathcal{F})\otimes\mathcal{N}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}(\mathcal{F})\otimes\mathcal{N}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the natural map is a surjection,
<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{F})\otimes k_{s}\twoheadrightarrow\textup{H}^{0}(X_{s},\mathcal{F}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{F})\otimes k_{s}\twoheadrightarrow\textup{H}^{0}(X_{s},\mathcal{F}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, the equivalence of (i)–(iii) is elementary and straightforward.
Moreover, (iv) is a special case of (iii). Conversely, (iv) implies
(iii) by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.7.10]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">OB72</a>, Cor. 5.1 p. 72]</cite>; this
useful implication is known as the “property of exchange.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, (i)–(iv) are implied by the following condition:</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the first cohomology group of the fiber vanishes, <math id="S3.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X_{s},\mathcal{F}_{s})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X_{s},\mathcal{F}_{s})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmthm10.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, (v) implies that <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{1}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{1}f_{*}(\mathcal{F}\otimes f^{*}\mathcal{N})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmthm10.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.5.3]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">OB72</a>, Cor. 2.1 p. 68]</cite>; in turn, this
vanishing implies (ii) owing to the long exact sequence of higher direct
images.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat, and its geometric fibers are
reduced and connected. Show <math id="S3.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds
universally.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf on <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a subfunctor
<math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula</p>
<table id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T):=\{\,\text{relative effective divisors $D$
on $X_{T}/T$ such that}\\
\text{$\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}$
for some invertible sheaf $\mathcal{N}$ on }T\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>relative effective divisors </mtext><mi>D</mi><mtext> on </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mtext> such that</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext> for some invertible sheaf </mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mtext> on </mtext></mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T):=\{\,\text{relative effective divisors $D$
on $X_{T}/T$ such that}\\
\text{$\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}$
for some invertible sheaf $\mathcal{N}$ on }T\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Notice the similarity of this definition with that,
Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, of <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>: both definitions work with
isomorphism classes of invertible sheaves on <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, in
the hope of producing a representable functor. Here, this hope is
fulfilled under suitable hypotheses on <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, according to the next
theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat, and its
geometric fibers are integral. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf on
<math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module associated in
Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> to <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
represents <math id="S3.Thmthm13.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined up to isomorphism.
Indeed, let <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a second choice. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">So <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
since <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Hence <math id="S3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, which applies to <math id="S3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally by Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{T},\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{T},\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, forming <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with changing the base to <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and so
(<a href="#S3.E1a" title="In 3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10.1</span></a>) becomes</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1b.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\SHom(\mathcal{Q}_{T},\mathcal{N})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T}*}(\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SHom(\mathcal{Q}_{T},\mathcal{N})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{\smash{T}*}(\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.13.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a map <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative effective divisor on <math id="S3.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math>, its
fiber <math id="S3.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor on <math id="S3.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Since <math id="S3.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined by <math id="S3.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, necessarily <math id="S3.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S3.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S3.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, <math id="S3.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, so <math id="S3.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space of
dimension 1. So <math id="S3.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Hence, by Nakayama’s lemma,
<math id="S3.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective at <math id="S3.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S3.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary. So <math id="S3.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective
everywhere.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T\to L</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA II</a>, 4.2.3]</cite>. Since <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{N},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{N},u)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined up to
isomorphism, a second choice yields the same <math id="S3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, this construction is functorial in <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we obtain a map
of functors,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\colon\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)\to L(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\colon\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)\to L(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let us prove <math id="S3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in L(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in L(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T\to L</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S3.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map. Then <math id="S3.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> arises from a
surjection <math id="S3.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\mathcal{Q}_{T}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>; namely, <math id="S3.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=p^{*}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=p^{*}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S3.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon\mathcal{Q}_{L}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon\mathcal{Q}_{L}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological map. Moreover,
there is only one such pair <math id="S3.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{N},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{N},u)</annotation></semantics></math> up to isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Via the isomorphism in (<a href="#S3.E1b" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13.1</span></a>), the surjection <math id="S3.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponds to a global section <math id="S3.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{T},\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{T},\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, so <math id="S3.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
But <math id="S3.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S3.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\in\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S3.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is integral since the geometric fibers of
<math id="S3.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> are integral by hypothesis. Hence <math id="S3.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">The section <math id="S3.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map <math id="S3.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})^{-1}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N})^{-1}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image is the ideal of a closed subscheme <math id="S3.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is cut out locally by one element; moreover, on the fiber <math id="S3.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this element corresponds to <math id="S3.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, so is regular. Hence <math id="S3.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a
relative effective divisor on <math id="S3.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. In fact,
<math id="S3.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, <math id="S3.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the only such divisor
corresponding to <math id="S3.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{N},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{N},u)</annotation></semantics></math>, so mapping to <math id="S3.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S3.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S3.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. In other words, <math id="S3.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> represents
<math id="S3.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>, show that there exists a
natural relative effective divisor <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{L}\big/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{L}\big/L</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1g.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{L}}(W)=\mathcal{L}_{L}\otimes f_{L}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{L}}(W)=\mathcal{L}_{L}\otimes f_{L}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> possesses the following universal property: given any
<math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and any relative effective divisor <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
invertible sheaf <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist a unique <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\colon T\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\colon T\to L</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times w)^{-1}W=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times w)^{-1}W=D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Picard scheme</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section proves Grothendieck’s main theorem about the Picard scheme,
which asserts its existence if <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat and its
geometric fibers are integral; in fact, the functor <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable. The proof involves Grothendieck’s method of using
functors to prescribe patching. The basic theory is developed in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, Ch. 0, Sct. 4.5, pp. 102–107]</cite>, and is applied in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, Ch. 1, Sct. 9, pp. 354–401]</cite> to the construction of
Grassmannians and related parameter schemes. The present construction
involves the basic theory, but is more sophisticated and more
complicated because it works not simply with the Zariski topology, but
also with the étale topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If any of the four relative Picard functors of Definition <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>
is representable, then the representing scheme is called the <span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Picard
scheme</span> and denoted by <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we say simply that the
Picard scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that, although there are four relative Picard functors, there is
at most one Picard scheme. Of course, if any functor is representable,
then the representing scheme is uniquely determined, up to a unique
isomorphism that preserves the identification of the given functor
with the functor of points of the representing scheme. But here, there
is more to the story.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, for example, say <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by
<math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by descent theory, <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is already a
sheaf in the fppf topology; so it is equal to its associated sheaf
<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, it too is representable by <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> may or may not be representable;
however, if it is, then it, as well, must be representable by <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds universally. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> an invertible
sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Show that there exist a subscheme <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> and an
invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> with these three properties: first, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{N}\simeq f_{N}^{*}{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{N}\simeq f_{N}^{*}{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>; second, given any <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}{\mathcal{N}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}{\mathcal{N}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, necessarily <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}\simeq t^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}\simeq t^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>; and third, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subscheme if <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
separated. Show also that the first two properties determine <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely
and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> up to isomorphism.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. An invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">universal sheaf</span>, or <span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poincaré
sheaf</span>, if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> possesses the following property: given any
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and any invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a
unique <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for some invertible sheaf
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\simeq(1\times h)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\simeq(1\times h)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Show that a universal sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists if and only if <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
represents <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally. Show that, if
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, then it is unique up to tensor product with the pullback
of a unique invertible sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Show that, if also <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section, then a universal sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>
exists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Find an example where no universal sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and let <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme.
Show that <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists too, and in fact, that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}=\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}=\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus forming the Picard scheme commutes with changing the base.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Find an example where <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
but <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> does not represent <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and either it represents
<math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds
universally. Show the scheme points of <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond, in a
natural bijective fashion, to the classes of invertible sheaves <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
on the fibers of <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>. A class is, by definition, represented by an
<math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on an <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a field containing the residue field
<math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> of a (scheme) point <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>; an <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on an <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
represents the same class if and only if there is a third field <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
containing the other two such that <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Abel map</span> is the natural map of functors</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{X/S}(T)\colon\Div_{X/S}(T)\to\Pic_{X/S}(T)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{X/S}(T)\colon\Div_{X/S}(T)\to\Pic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">defined by sending a relative effective divisor <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> to the
sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. The target <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> may be replaced by any
of its associated sheaves. If <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists,
then the term <span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Abel map</span> may refer to the corresponding map of
schemes</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat with integral geometric fibers. Assume
<math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and denote it by <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. View <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
<math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme via the Abel map. Assume a universal sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists,
and let <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. Show <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Main)<span id="S4.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski locally over <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and is flat
with integral geometric fibers.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1)<span id="S4.Thmthm8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Then <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, is separated and locally of finite
type over <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and represents <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2)<span id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If also <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian and <math id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, then
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of open
subschemes, each an increasing union of open quasi-projective
<math id="S4.Thmthm8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, (0, 4.5.5), p. 106]</cite>, it is a local matter on <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to
represent a Zariski sheaf on the category of <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. Moreover, it
is also a local matter on <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to prove that an <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme is separated
and locally of finite type. Hence, in order to prove (1), we may assume
<math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian and <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly an <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme is separated if it is a disjoint union of
separated open subschemes, or if it is an increasing union of
separated open subschemes. Hence (1) follows from (2).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove (2), owing to Yoneda’s lemma, we may view the category of
schemes as a full subcategory of the category of functors by identifying
a scheme <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with its functor of points. Denote this functor too by <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
in order to lighten the notation. And say that the functor is a scheme,
as well as that it is representable. Also, set</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\Pic_{(X/S)\et}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\Pic_{(X/S)\et}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T)=\Hom(T,P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T)=\Hom(T,P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a polynomial <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be the
étale subsheaf associated to the presheaf whose <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are
represented by the invertible sheaves <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(X_{t},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t}(n))=\phi(n)\text{ for all $t\in T$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(X_{t},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t}(n))=\phi(n)\text{ for all $t\in T$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.8.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that this presheaf is well defined, because (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.1</span></a>)
remains valid after any base change <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>; indeed, for any <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{p(t^{\prime})},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{p(t^{\prime})}(n)\bigr)\otimes_{k_{t}}k_{t^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{p(t^{\prime})},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{p(t^{\prime})}(n)\bigr)\otimes_{k_{t}}k_{t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">because cohomology commutes with flat base change by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. III,
9.3, p. 255]</cite>. Hence <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix a map <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to P</annotation></semantics></math>, and represent it by means of an étale covering
<math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and an invertible sheaf <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the
subset <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\subset T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\subset T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}:=\{\,t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}\mid\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\phi(n)\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}:=\{\,t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}\mid\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\phi(n)\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is open by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.11]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}:=p({T^{\prime}}^{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}:=p({T^{\prime}}^{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> is
open as <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\subset T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\subset T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is open and <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is étale.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}=p^{-1}(T^{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}=p^{-1}(T^{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, let <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in p^{-1}(T^{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in p^{-1}(T^{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t^{\prime})=p(t^{\prime}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t^{\prime})=p(t^{\prime}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}\in{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}\in{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now,
there is an étale covering <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the two
pullbacks of <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic. Let <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime\prime}\in T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime\prime}\in T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
have image <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the first map <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and have the image
<math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}\in T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}\in T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the second map. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}_{1}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}_{1}}(n)\bigr)=\phi(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime\prime}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\chi\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}_{1}},\mathcal{L}^{-1}_{t^{\prime}_{1}}(n)\bigr)=\phi(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\supset p^{-1}(T^{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}\supset p^{-1}(T^{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}=p^{-1}(T^{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}=p^{-1}(T^{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is (represents) the fiber product of functors
<math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}\times_{P}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}\times_{P}T</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, to see they have the same <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-points, let
<math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\colon R\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\colon R\to T</annotation></semantics></math> be a map; form <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}:=R\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}:=R\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}\colon R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}\colon R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
<math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}\to P</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> essentially by definition.
Now, <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}\to R</annotation></semantics></math> is an étale covering. Hence <math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to T\to P</annotation></semantics></math> factors
through <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is an étale sheaf.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to T\to P</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to P</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by means of an étale covering <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime\prime}\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime\prime}\to R</annotation></semantics></math> and an
invertible sheaf <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(X_{u},\mathcal{\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}}^{-1}_{u}(n))=\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(X_{u},\mathcal{\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}}^{-1}_{u}(n))=\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in R^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in R^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since both <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> define <math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to P</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an étale covering <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime\prime\prime}\to R^{\prime\prime}\times_{R}R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></msub><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime\prime\prime}\to R^{\prime\prime}\times_{R}R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the pullbacks of <math id="S4.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S4.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R^{\prime\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">′′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R^{\prime\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic. Hence the image of <math id="S4.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}\colon R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}\colon R^{\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in
<math id="S4.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. But the latter is open. Hence <math id="S4.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through it.
Therefore, <math id="S4.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\colon R\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\colon R\to T</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S4.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}\times_{P}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}\times_{P}T</annotation></semantics></math> have the same <math id="S4.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-points.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> vary. Plainly the <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T^{\prime}}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and cover <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So the <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and cover <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by a general
result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, (0, 4.5.4), p. 103]</cite>, if the <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are
(representable by) schemes, then <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is their disjoint union. Thus it
remains to represent each <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> by an increasing union of open
quasi-projective <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}\subset P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}\subset P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
étale subsheaf associated to the presheaf whose <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are
represented by the <math id="S4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, in addition to
(<a href="#S4.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.1</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}(n)=0\text{ for all }i\geq 1\text{ and }n\geq m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> for all </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> and </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{L}(n)=0\text{ for all }i\geq 1\text{ and }n\geq m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.8.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that this presheaf is well defined, because (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>)
remains valid after any base change <math id="S4.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>, as is shown next.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, let’s see that (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>) is equivalent to the following
condition:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))=0\text{ for all }i\geq 1,\text{ all }n\geq m,\text{ and all }t\in T." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))=0\text{ for all }i\geq 1,\text{ all }n\geq m,\text{ and all }t\in T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.8.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.3</span></a>) implies (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>); in fact, for any given
<math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all
quasi-coherent <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.5.3]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">OB72</a>, Cor. 2.1
p. 68]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, assume (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>). Fix <math id="S4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s proceed by
descending induction on <math id="S4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to prove <math id="S4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes.
It vanishes for <math id="S4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math> by Serre’s Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 2.2.2]</cite>.
Suppose it vanishes for some <math id="S4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p16.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all
quasi-coherent <math id="S4.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, as just noted. So <math id="S4.p16.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is right
exact in <math id="S4.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the long exact sequence of higher direct
images. Therefore, by general principles, there is a natural
isomorphism of functors</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}(\mathcal{L}(n))_{t}\otimes\mathcal{N}_{t}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}(\mathcal{L}(n))_{t}\otimes\mathcal{N}_{t}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{R}^{i-1}f_{\smash{T}*}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(n)\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\bigr)_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>) holds, both source and target vanish.
Taking <math id="S4.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the vanishing of <math id="S4.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i-1}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i-1}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>) implies (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, for any <math id="S4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="S4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{p(t^{\prime})},\mathcal{L}_{p(t^{\prime})}(n)\bigr)\otimes_{k_{t}}k_{t^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{t^{\prime}},\mathcal{L}_{t^{\prime}}(n)\bigr)=\textup{H}^{i}\bigl(X_{p(t^{\prime})},\mathcal{L}_{p(t^{\prime})}(n)\bigr)\otimes_{k_{t}}k_{t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">because cohomology commutes with flat base change. So (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.3</span></a>)
remains valid after the base change <math id="S4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>; whence, (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>)
does too. Thus the presheaf is well defined, and so <math id="S4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Arguing much as we did for <math id="S4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}\times_{P}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}\times_{P}T</annotation></semantics></math>, we find, given a map <math id="S4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to P^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to P^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, that, as <math id="S4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> varies, the products <math id="S4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}\times_{P^{\phi}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msup></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}\times_{P^{\phi}}T</annotation></semantics></math> form a
nested sequence of open subschemes of <math id="S4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, whose union is <math id="S4.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. In the
argument, the key change is in proving openness. In place of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.11]</cite>, we use the following part of Serre’s
Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 2.2.2]</cite>: given a coherent sheaf <math id="S4.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on a
projective scheme over a Noetherian ring <math id="S4.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, there are only finitely
many <math id="S4.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero, and all
these nonzero <math id="S4.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules are finitely generated. Hence, if there is a
prime <math id="S4.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf p}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))_{\bf p}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝐩</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))_{\bf p}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S4.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is an <math id="S4.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\notin{\bf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\notin{\bf p}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))_{a}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(\mathcal{F}(n))_{a}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, again by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, (0, 4.5.4), p. 103]</cite>, it suffices to
represent each <math id="S4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> by a quasi-projective <math id="S4.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S4.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}(n):=\phi(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}(n):=\phi(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is an
isomorphism of functors <math id="S4.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined
as follows. First, define an endomorphism <math id="S4.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
by sending an invertible sheaf <math id="S4.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on an <math id="S4.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Plainly <math id="S4.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is an automorphism. So <math id="S4.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> induces an
automorphism <math id="S4.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of the associated sheaf <math id="S4.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly
<math id="S4.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> carries <math id="S4.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S4.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus it
suffices to represent <math id="S4.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\phi_{0}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by a quasi-projective <math id="S4.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">The function <math id="S4.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto\chi(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto\chi(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> is locally constant on
<math id="S4.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.11]</cite>. Hence we may assume it is constant by
replacing <math id="S4.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by an open and closed subset. Set <math id="S4.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(n):=\chi(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(n):=\chi(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the Abel map <math id="S4.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{X/S}\colon\Div_{X/S}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{X/S}\colon\Div_{X/S}\to P</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that
<math id="S4.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme, in fact, an open subscheme of the Hilbert
scheme <math id="S4.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>. Form the product
<math id="S4.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}\times_{P}\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}\times_{P}\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a scheme <math id="S4.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, in fact, an
open subscheme of <math id="S4.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, by what was proved above. Set
<math id="S4.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(n):=\psi(n)-\phi_{0}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(n):=\psi(n)-\phi_{0}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="S4.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> lies in
<math id="S4.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X/S}^{\theta}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X/S}^{\theta}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is projective over <math id="S4.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s now prove the projection <math id="S4.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a surjection of étale sheaves. In other words, given a <math id="S4.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and a
<math id="S4.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have to find an étale
covering <math id="S4.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S4.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\in Z(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\in Z(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\lambda_{1})\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\lambda_{1})\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the image of
<math id="S4.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Represent <math id="S4.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by means of an étale covering <math id="S4.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and an
invertible sheaf <math id="S4.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Virtually by definition, the
product <math id="S4.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\times_{\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\times_{\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}}Z</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="S4.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}^{\prime}/X_{T^{\prime}}/T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}^{\prime}/X_{T^{\prime}}/T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>, this product is equal
to <math id="S4.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module associated to
<math id="S4.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. Now, <math id="S4.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.3</span></a>). Since (v) implies
(i) in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>, therefore <math id="S4.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="S4.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="S4.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So there exist an étale
covering <math id="S4.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S4.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S4.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 17.16.3 (ii)]</cite>. Then the composition <math id="S4.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})\to Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})\to Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the composition <math id="S4.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to T^{\prime}\to T\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to T^{\prime}\to T\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the map <math id="S4.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="S4.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\in Z(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\in Z(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(\lambda_{1})\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(\lambda_{1})\in\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}(T_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the image of <math id="S4.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
the composition <math id="S4.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\to T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\to T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is an étale covering, <math id="S4.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is
thus a surjection of étale sheaves.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, the map <math id="S4.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon Z\to\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the
invertible sheaf associated to the universal relative effective divisor
on <math id="S4.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{Z}/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{Z}/Z</annotation></semantics></math>. So taking <math id="S4.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=Z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:=T</annotation></semantics></math> above, we conclude that the
product <math id="S4.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\times_{\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\times_{\smash{P_{0}^{\phi_{0}}}}Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme and that the first
projection is smooth and projective. Therefore, the theorem now results
from the following general lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon Z\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon Z\to P</annotation></semantics></math> be a map of étale sheaves, and set <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:=Z\times_{P}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=Z\times_{P}Z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjection, <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by a
quasi-projective <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by an <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and
the first projection <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by a smooth and proper
map. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by a quasi-projective <math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and
<math id="S4.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by a smooth map.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">To lighten the notation, let <math id="S4.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
corresponding schemes as well. Since the structure map <math id="S4.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective, it is separated; whence, the first projection
<math id="S4.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\times_{S}Z\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\times_{S}Z\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is too. But the first projection <math id="S4.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, and
factors naturally through a map <math id="S4.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\colon R\to Z\times_{S}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon R\to Z\times_{S}Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is proper.
But <math id="S4.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is a monomorphism; that is, <math id="S4.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is injective on <math id="S4.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points.
Therefore, <math id="S4.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed embedding by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 8.11.5]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, for each <math id="S4.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the subset <math id="S4.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)\subset Z(T)\times_{S(T)}Z(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)\subset Z(T)\times_{S(T)}Z(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph of an equivalence relation. Also, the
map of schemes <math id="S4.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is flat and proper, and <math id="S4.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a
quasi-projective <math id="S4.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. It follows that there exist a
quasi-projective <math id="S4.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and a faithfully flat and projective map
<math id="S4.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=Z\times_{Q}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=Z\times_{Q}Z</annotation></semantics></math>. (In other words, a flat and proper
equivalence relation on a quasi-projective scheme is effective.) In
fact, <math id="S4.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map from <math id="S4.p28.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> to the Hilbert scheme <math id="S4.p28.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{Z/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{Z/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
its graph lies in the universal scheme as a closed subscheme, which
descends to <math id="S4.p28.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset\IHilb_{Z/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset\IHilb_{Z/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; for more details, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK80</a>, Thm. (2.9),
p. 70]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Q</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, it is smooth if (and only if) its fibers are
smooth. But these fibers are, up to extension of the ground field, the
same as those of <math id="S4.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="S4.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth by hypothesis. Thus
<math id="S4.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Q</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to see that <math id="S4.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S4.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. First, observe that <math id="S4.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Q</annotation></semantics></math>
is (represents) a surjection of étale sheaves. Indeed, given an
element of <math id="S4.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S4.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=Z\times_{Q}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=Z\times_{Q}T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. So there
exist an étale covering <math id="S4.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S4.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S4.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to A</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 17.16.3 (ii)]</cite>. Then <math id="S4.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to A\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to A\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> is an element of
<math id="S4.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps to the element of <math id="S4.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the given
element of <math id="S4.p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\to Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjection of étale sheaves, <math id="S4.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is, in the
category of étale sheaves, the coequalizer of the pair of maps
<math id="S4.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightrightarrows Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightrightarrows Z</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 4.10. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a> below, which is an
elementary exercise in general Grothendieck topology. By the same
exercise, <math id="S4.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> too is the coequalizer of this pair of maps. But, in any
category, the coequalizer is unique up to unique isomorphism. Thus <math id="S4.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>
represents <math id="S4.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a map of étale sheaves <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G</annotation></semantics></math>, show it is a surjection if
and only if <math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the coequalizer of the pair of maps
<math id="S4.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat, its geometric fibers are integral,
and <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a subscheme of
finite type. Show <math id="S4.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski locally over <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and is flat
with integral geometric fibers. First, show that, if a universal sheaf
<math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, then the Abel map <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski locally over <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, show that, in general, <math id="S4.Thmthm12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is proper. Proceed by reducing this case to the first: just use
<math id="S4.Thmthm12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> itself to change the base.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat and projective Zariski locally over <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume its geometric fibers <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are integral curves of arithmetic
genus <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> at least 1. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be the
open subscheme where <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. Show there is a natural closed
embedding <math id="S4.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon X_{0}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon X_{0}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_eg">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, the geometric fibers of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to be
integral. This condition is needed not only for the proof to work, but
also for the statement to hold. The matter is clarified by the following
example, which is attributed to Mumford and is described in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 236-1]</cite> and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>, p. 210]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[\![t]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[\![t]\!]</annotation></semantics></math> be the ring of formal power series, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> its
spectrum. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbf{P}^{2}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbf{P}^{2}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subscheme with inhomogeneous
equation <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}+y^{2}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}+y^{2}=t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> the structure map. The generic
fiber <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate conic. The special fiber <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
pair of conjugate lines; <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible and geometrically
connected. So <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is flat by the implication (iii)<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>(i) of
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> with <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=X</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds universally by Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>. However, as we’ll see,
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> does <span id="S4.Thmthm14.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> exist!</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}:=\mathbb{C}[\![t]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}:=\mathbb{C}[\![t]\!]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}:=X\times S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}:=X\times S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has sections: for example, one section <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
setting</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x:=\sqrt{-1}\text{ and }y:=\sqrt{1+t}=1+(1/2)t-(1/8)t^{2}+\dotsb." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x:=\sqrt{-1}\text{ and }y:=\sqrt{1+t}=1+(1/2)t-(1/8)t^{2}+\dotsb.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Hence all four relative Picard functors of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal by the
Comparison Theorem, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. Furthermore, as we’ll see,
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, but is <span id="S4.Thmthm14.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> separated!</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of isomorphic open
nonseparated subschemes <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained
from <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by repeating the origin infinitely often; more precisely, to
form <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, take a copy <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for each pair <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b=n</annotation></semantics></math>, and glue the <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> together off their closed
points. Each <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizes a different degeneration of the
invertible sheaf of degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on the generic fiber <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>; the
degenerate sheaf has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> on the first line, and degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> on the
second. Also, complex conjugation interchanges the two lines, so
interchanges <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{b,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{b,a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}=\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}=\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. Since the closed points of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{b,a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{b,a}</annotation></semantics></math> are conjugate, they map to the same point of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This point lies in an affine open subscheme <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. So the two closed
points lie in the preimage <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
affine since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are affine. However, if <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq b</annotation></semantics></math>, then
the two closed points are distinct, and so lie in no common affine <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have a contradiction. Thus <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot exist.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, let’s prove <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, note <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular; in fact, in <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{C}}\times\mathbf{A}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{C}}\times\mathbf{A}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the equation <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}+y^{2}=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}+y^{2}=t</annotation></semantics></math> defines a smooth surface. So on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> every
reduced curve is an effective divisor. In particular, consider these
three: the line <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:x=\sqrt{-1}y,\,t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:x=\sqrt{-1}y,\,t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the line
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:x=-\sqrt{-1}y,\,t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:x=-\sqrt{-1}y,\,t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the image <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of the section <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(bL+nA)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(bL+nA)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=a+b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=a+b</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction to
the generic fiber <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\cap X^{\prime}_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\cap X^{\prime}_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of a section. And <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|M</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cap M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cap M</annotation></semantics></math> is empty and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\cap M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\cap M</annotation></semantics></math> is a
reduced <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-rational point. And <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|L</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>
since, in addition, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(L)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(M)\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(L)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(M)\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> as
the ideal of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L+M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L+M</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly, every invertible sheaf
on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial; fix a <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-rigidification <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{S^{\prime}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{S^{\prime}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and use it to identify the two sheaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.1" class="ltx_p">On <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X^{\prime}\times_{S^{\prime}}\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\times_{S^{\prime}}\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, form an invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by placing
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>; plainly, the <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> patch
together. It now suffices to show this: given any <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and
any invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist a unique <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-map
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\colon T\to\mathbb{\amalg}S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon T\to\mathbb{\amalg}S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and some invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Replace <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{L}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{L}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, if <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> exist, then
necessarily <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=q^{*}g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=q^{*}g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}_{S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{O}_{S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Plainly, we may assume <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Then the function
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto\chi(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto\chi(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.5]</cite>. Set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\chi(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\chi(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b=n</annotation></semantics></math>. Set</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2b.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}\text{ and }\mathcal{N}:=f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mtext> and </mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}\text{ and }\mathcal{N}:=f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.2" class="ltx_p">Plainly <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the natural map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> be the generic fiber of the structure map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\colon T\to S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\colon T\to S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\sigma}\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\sigma}\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> is open. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate plane conic with a rational point <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}\simeq\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}\simeq\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}(nA_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}(nA_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by Serre’s explicit computation
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 2.1.12]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible at <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and forming <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes
with passing to <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, owing to the theory in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.
So forming <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with passing to <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> too.
But <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective along
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by Nakayama’s lemma. But both source and target of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> are
invertible along <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism along <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
over <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary. Now, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}|T_{\sigma}=\mathcal{O}_{T_{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}|T_{\sigma}=\mathcal{O}_{T_{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{\sigma}}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{T_{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{\sigma}}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{T_{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a,b}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a,b}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(t)\in S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(t)\in S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
closed point and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|M_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|M_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T_{a,b}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T_{a,b}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}|L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree 0, so <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}|L_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{L_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|L_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{L_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}|M_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{M_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|M_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{M_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the natural short exact sequence</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{L_{t}}(-1)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}\to\mathcal{O}_{M_{t}}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{L_{t}}(-1)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}\to\mathcal{O}_{M_{t}}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.2" class="ltx_p">Twist by <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and take cohomology. Thus <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}\to\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}_{t}}\to\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This map is surjective as it is surjective after
restriction to <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and to <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. So this map is an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.1" class="ltx_p">As above, we conclude <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism on an open neighborhood <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}:=f_{T^{\prime}}(X^{\prime}_{T}-V^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}:=f_{T^{\prime}}(X^{\prime}_{T}-V^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
open. But <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}W^{\prime}\subset V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}W^{\prime}\subset V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism over <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\!X^{\prime}_{t^{\prime}}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\!X^{\prime}_{t^{\prime}}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\in W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\in W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}\subset T_{a,b}^{\prime}\cup T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}\subset T_{a,b}^{\prime}\cup T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a,b}:=T_{a,b}^{\prime}\cup T_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a,b}:=T_{a,b}^{\prime}\cup T_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a,b}\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a,b}\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> is open
as it contains a neighborhood of each of its points. Furthermore, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>
is an isomorphism over <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}|T_{\sigma}=\mathcal{O}_{T_{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}|T_{\sigma}=\mathcal{O}_{T_{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, again since <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{T}^{*}f_{T}^{*}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}_{a,b}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm14.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{a,b}\colon T_{a,b}\to\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{a,b}\colon T_{a,b}\to\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the composition of the
structure map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\colon T\to S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\colon T\to S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the inclusion of <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q_{a,b})^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q_{a,b})^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{L}|X^{\prime}_{T_{a,b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Plainly, as <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vary, the <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> patch to a map
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\colon T\to\mathbb{\amalg}S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon T\to\mathbb{\amalg}S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly
this map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the only <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaf <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\amalg S^{\prime}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm14.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a universal
sheaf.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.15</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally free sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and is
everywhere of rank at least 2. Show <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and
represents <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; in fact, <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}=\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}=\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the disjoint union of copies of <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S4.Thmthm15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 4.16</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the curve <math id="S4.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 2.4. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. Show
<math id="S4.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}=\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}=\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.17</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S4.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S4.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S4.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Owing to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 8.14.2]</cite>, we just
need to check the following condition. For every filtered inverse system
of affine <math id="S4.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S4.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the natural map is a bijection:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varinjlim P(T_{i})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P\bigl(\varprojlim T_{i}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>→</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>←</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varinjlim P(T_{i})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P\bigl(\varprojlim T_{i}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">To check it is injective, set <math id="S4.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=\varprojlim T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>←</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=\varprojlim T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S4.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S4.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\in P(T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\in P(T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Represent <math id="S4.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by a sheaf <math id="S4.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S4.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T_{i}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T_{i}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}^{\prime}\to T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}^{\prime}\to T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an fppf covering. Set
<math id="S4.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}:=T_{i}^{\prime}\times_{T_{i}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}:=T_{i}^{\prime}\times_{T_{i}}T</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S4.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}:=\mathcal{L}_{i}|X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}:=\mathcal{L}_{i}|X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be
the image of <math id="S4.p33.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p33.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p33.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S4.p33.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0\in P(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0\in P(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists an fppf covering <math id="S4.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}\to T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{T^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{T^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 8.8.2, 8.10.5(vi), 11.2.6, 8.5.2(i), and 8.5.2.4]</cite> that
there exist a <math id="S4.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq i</annotation></semantics></math> and an fppf covering <math id="S4.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}^{\prime\prime}\to T_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}^{\prime\prime}\to T_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S4.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}^{\prime}:=T_{i}^{\prime}\times_{T_{j}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}^{\prime}:=T_{i}^{\prime}\times_{T_{j}}T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{T_{j}^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T_{j}^{\prime\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{T_{j}^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T_{j}^{\prime\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S4.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="S4.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in P(T_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in P(T_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the map
is injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">Surjectivity can be proved similarly. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.18</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are three important existence theorems for <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
refine Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. They were proved soon after it, and each
involves new ideas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, Mumford proved the following generalization of
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, and it fits in nicely with
Example <a href="#S4.Thmthm14" title="Example 4.14. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbsthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Theorem 4.18.1</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat, and its geometric fibers are
reduced and connected; assume the irreducible components of its ordinary
fibers are geometrically irreducible. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mumford stated this theorem at the bottom of Page viii in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>]</cite>.
He said the proof is a generalization of that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Lects. 19–21]</cite>
in the case where <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field
and <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth surface. That proof is based on his theory of
independent 0-cycles. This theory is further developed in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK79</a>, pp. 23–28]</cite> and used to prove <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK79</a>, Thm. (3.1)]</cite>, which
asserts the existence of a natural compactification of <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
when <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat and locally projective with integral geometric
fibers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 236-1]</cite> attributed to
Mumford a slightly different theorem, in which neither the geometric
fibers nor the ordinary fibers are assumed connected (see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>, p. 210]</cite> also). Grothendieck said the proof is based on a
refinement of Mumford’s construction of quotients, and referred to the
forthcoming notes of a Harvard seminar of Mumford and Tate’s, held in
the spring of 1962.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Mumford was kind enough, in November 2003, to mail the present author
his personal folder of handwritten notes from the seminar; the folder is
labeled, “Groth–Mumford–Tate,” and contains notes from talks by each
of the three, and notes written by each of them. Virtually all the content
has appeared elsewhere; Mumford’s contributions appeared in Mumford’s
books <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm65</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>]</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm70</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The notes contain, in Mumford’s hand, a precise statement of the theorem
and a rough sketch of the proof. This statement too is slightly
different from that of Theorem  <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm1" title="Theorem 4.18.1. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.1</span></a>: he crossed out the
hypothesis that the geometric fibers are connected; and he made the
weaker assumption that the ordinary fibers are connected. The proof is
broadly like his proof in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Lects. 19–21]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Second, Grothendieck proved this theorem of “generic
representability.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbsthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Theorem 4.18.2</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, and <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is integral. Then there exists a
nonempty open subset <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{V}/V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{V}/V}</annotation></semantics></math> exists,
represents <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is a disjoint union of open
quasi-projective subschemes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A particularly important special case is covered by the
following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbscor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Corollary 4.18.3</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is complete. Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, represents <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is a disjoint
union of open quasi-projective subschemes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Before Grothendieck discovered Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" title="Theorem 4.18.2. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.2</span></a>, he
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Cor. 6.6, p. 232-17]</cite> proved Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a>
assuming <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is projective. To do so, he developed a method of
“relative representability,” by which Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> implies
the existence of the Picard scheme in other cases. The method
incorporates a “dévissage” of Oort’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oo62</a>, §6]</cite>; the latter
yields the Picard scheme as an extension of a group subscheme of
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> by an affine group scheme.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Rem. 6.6, p. 232-17]</cite>, Grothendieck said it is “extremely
plausible” that Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a> holds in general, and can be
proved by extending the method of relative representability, so that it
covers the case of a surjective map <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> projective, such
as the map provided by Chow’s lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA II</a>, Thm. 5.6.1]</cite>.
Furthermore, he conjectured, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Rem. 6.6, p. 232-17]</cite>, that,
for any surjective map <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> between proper schemes over a field,
the induced map on Picard schemes is affine. He said he was led to the
conjecture by considerations of “nonflat descent,” a version of
descent theory where the maps are not required to be flat, but the
objects are.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Thanking Grothendieck for help, Murre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mr63</a>, (II.15)]</cite> gave the
first proof of the heart of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a>: if <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper, then <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and
is locally of finite type. Murre did not use the method of relative
representability. Rather, he identified seven conditions
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mr63</a>, (I.2.1)]</cite> that are necessary and sufficient for the
representability of a functor from schemes over a field to Abelian
groups. Then he showed the seven are satisfied by the Picard functor
localized in the fpqc topology. To handle the last two conditions, he
used Chow’s lemma and Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In the meantime, Grothendieck had proved Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" title="Theorem 4.18.2. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.2</span></a>,
according to Murre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mr63</a>, p. 5]</cite>. Later, the proof appeared in two
parts. The first part established a key intermediate result, the
following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbsthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Theorem 4.18.4</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a coherent sheaf on <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathcal{F}}\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathcal{F}}\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> the subfunctor of all <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-flat. Then <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by an
unramified <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Murre sketched Grothendieck’s proof of this theorem in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mr65</a>, Cor. 1,
p. 294-11]</cite>. The proof involves identifying and checking eight
conditions that are necessary and sufficient for the representability
of a functor by a separated and unramified <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme locally of finite
type. As in Murre’s proof of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a>, a key step is
to show the functor is “pro-representable”: there exist certain
natural topological rings, and if the functor is representable by <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
then these rings are the completions of the local rings at the points
of <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> that are closed in their fibers over <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In the second part of Grothendieck’s proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" title="Theorem 4.18.2. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.2</span></a>,
the main result is the following theorem of relative representability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbsthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Theorem 4.18.5</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is integral. Let <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a surjective map of
proper <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. Then there is a nonempty open subset <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> such that the map</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}\to\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}\to\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is representable by quasi-affine maps of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, for every <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and map
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, the fibered product
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}\times_{\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}\times_{\Pic_{(X^{\prime}_{V}/V)\fppf}}T</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable and its projection to <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-affine map of finite
type between schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Raynaud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ra71</a>, Thm. 1.1]</cite> gave Grothendieck’s proof of
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5" title="Theorem 4.18.5. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.5</span></a>; the main ingredients are, indeed, Oort
dévissage and nonflat descent. As a first consequence, Raynaud
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ra71</a>, Cor. 1.2]</cite> derived Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" title="Theorem 4.18.2. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.2</span></a>. In order to show
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of open quasi-projective subschemes,
he used a finiteness theorem for <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which Grothendieck had stated
under (v) in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. C-08]</cite>, and which is proved below as
Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a>. As a second consequence of
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5" title="Theorem 4.18.5. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.5</span></a>, Raynaud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ra71</a>, Cors. 1.5]</cite> established
Grothendieck’s conjecture that, over a field, a surjective map of proper
schemes induces an affine map on Picard schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The third important existence theorem for <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is due to
M. Artin, whose work greatly clarifies the situation. Artin proved
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists when it should, but not as a scheme. Rather, it
exists as a more general object, called an “algebraic space,” which is
closer in nature to a (complex) analytic space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Rem. 5.2, p. 232-13]</cite> had said: “it is not
ruled out that <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists whenever <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat
and such that <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally. At least,
this statement is proved in the context of analytic spaces when <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>
is, in addition, projective.” Mumford’s example,
Example <a href="#S4.Thmthm14" title="Example 4.14. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>, shows the statement is false for schemes;
Artin’s theorem shows the statement holds for algebraic spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Algebraic spaces were introduced by Artin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ar68</a>, §1]</cite>, and the
theory developed by himself and his student Knutson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kn71</a>]</cite>. The
spaces are constructed by gluing together schemes along open subsets
that are isomorphic locally in the étale topology. Over <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, these
open sets are locally analytically isomorphic; so an algebraic space is
a kind of complex analytic space. In general and more formally, an
algebraic space is the quotient in the category of étale sheaves of a
scheme divided by an étale equivalence relation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Artin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ar67</a>, Thm. 3.4, p. 35]</cite>, proceeding in the spirit of
Grothendieck and Murre, identified five conditions on a functor that are
necessary and sufficient for it to be a locally separated algebraic
space that is locally of finite type over a field or over an excellent
Dedekind domain. In the proof of necessity, a key new ingredient is
Artin’s approximation theorem; it implies that the topological rings
given by pro-representability can be algebraized. The resulting schemes
are then glued together via an étale equivalence relation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p21.1" class="ltx_p">By checking that the five conditions hold, Artin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ar67</a>, Thm. 7.3, p. 67]</cite>
proved the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_sbsthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem">Theorem 4.18.6</span>.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be a flat, proper, and finitely presented map of
algebraic spaces. Assume forming <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with changing
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is “cohomologically flat in dimension zero”). Then
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists as an algebraic space, which is locally of finite
presentation over <math id="S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is finitely presented, the statement can be reduced
to the case where <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally of finite type over a field or over an
excellent Dedekind domain. Artin’s proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6" title="Theorem 4.18.6. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.6</span></a> is
direct: it involves no reduction whatsoever to Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p23.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, it is not known, in general, whether
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6" title="Theorem 4.18.6. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.6</span></a> implies Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, which asserts
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme. However, more is known over a <span id="S4.Thmthm18.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">field</span>.
Indeed, given an algebraic space that is locally of finite type over a
field, Artin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ar67</a>, Lem. 4.2, p. 43]</cite> proved this: if the space is a
sheaf of groups, then it is a group scheme. Thus Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6" title="Theorem 4.18.6. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.6</span></a>
implies the heart of Corollary  <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm18.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm6" title="Theorem 4.18.6. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.6</span></a>, the fibers over <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of the algebraic
space <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are schemes; they are the Picard schemes of the
fibers <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, though the <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be schemes. In particular, if
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are schemes — so the <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are too — then the
<math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are schemes. Furthermore, then they form a family;
its total space <math id="S4.Thmthm18.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic space, but not necessarily
a scheme.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>The connected component of the identity</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having treated the existence of the Picard scheme <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, we now
turn to its structure. In this section, we study the union
<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of the connected components of the identity element,
<math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. We establish a number of basic
properties, especially when <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is remarkable how much we can prove about <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> formally, or
nearly so, from general principles. Notably, we can do without the
finiteness theorems proved in the next section. In order to emphasize
the formal nature and corresponding generality of the arguments,
most of the results are stated with the general hypothesis that
<math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists instead of with specific hypotheses that imply it
exists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a group scheme locally of finite
type. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the connected component of the identity element <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1)<span id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Then <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is separated.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2)<span id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Then <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth if it has a geometrically
reduced open subscheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(3)<span id="S5.Thmthm1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Then <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open and closed group subscheme of finite
type; it is geometrically irreducible; and forming it commutes with
extending <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point, it is closed. Define a map <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon G\times G\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon G\times G\to G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points by <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(g,h):=gh^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(g,h):=gh^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}e\subset G\times G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}e\subset G\times G</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed subscheme. Its <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are just the pairs <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,g)</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G(T)</annotation></semantics></math>; so <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}e</annotation></semantics></math> is the diagonal. Thus (1) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has a geometrically reduced open subscheme <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. To prove
<math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, we may replace <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by its algebraic closure. Then <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a nonempty smooth open subscheme <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, given any
two closed points <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,\,h\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,\,h\in G</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an automorphism of <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that
carries <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, multiplication by <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}h</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in W</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth at <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (2) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider (3). By definition, <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest connected subspace
containing <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. But the closure of a connected subspace is plainly
connected; so <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA I</a>, 6.1.9]</cite>, in any locally
Noetherian topological space, the connected components are open. Thus
<math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is open too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and has a <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point, <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically
connected by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 4.5.14]</cite>. Thus forming <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with
extending <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}\times G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}\times G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 4.5.8]</cite>.
So <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(G^{0}\times G^{0})\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(G^{0}\times G^{0})\subset G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, and contains <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>,
so lies in <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically irreducible and quasi-compact, we may
replace <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by its algebraic closure. Then <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}_{\red}\times G^{0}_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}_{\red}\times G^{0}_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> is
reduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 4.6.1]</cite>. Hence <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map from
<math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}_{\red}\times G^{0}_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}_{\red}\times G^{0}_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
subgroup. Thus we may replace <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced and <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed, <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a
nonempty smooth affine open subscheme <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Take arbitrary <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points
<math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hg^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hg^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and open, and
contains <math id="S5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible locally at <math id="S5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is
arbitrary, and <math id="S5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. So <math id="S5.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA I</a>, 6.1.10]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, its open subschemes <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hU</annotation></semantics></math> meet. So
their intersection contains a <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically
closed. Then <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=hh_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=hh_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some a <math id="S5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S5.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=g_{1}h_{1}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=g_{1}h_{1}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S5.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary. Hence <math id="S5.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(U\times U)=G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(U\times U)=G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S5.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times U</annotation></semantics></math> is affine, so quasi-compact. Hence <math id="S5.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact. By hypothesis, <math id="S5.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is locally of finite type. Hence
<math id="S5.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type. Thus (3) holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group scheme of finite type over a field, <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{{\red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{{\red}}</annotation></semantics></math> its
reduction. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{{\red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{{\red}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be a group subscheme, because
<math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{{\red}}\times G_{{\red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{{\red}}\times G_{{\red}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be reduced. Waterhouse
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wa79</a>, p. 53]</cite> gives two conditions equivalent to reducedness when <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
is finite in Exercise 9, and he gives a counterexample in Exercise 10.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
exists and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
separated, and it is smooth if it has a geometrically reduced open
subscheme. Furthermore, the connected component of the identity
<math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open and closed group subscheme of finite type; it
is geometrically irreducible; and forming it commutes with extending
<math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">This result follows formally from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 4.17. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a>
and Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is projective
and geometrically integral. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is
quasi-projective. If also <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically normal, then
<math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> implies <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents
<math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and is of finite type by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> (in fact, here
Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 4.17. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a> is logically unnecessary since <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is locally of finite type by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>). So <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is quasi-projective by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is also geometrically normal. Since <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective, to prove it is projective, it suffices to prove it is
proper. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, forming <math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes
with extending <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. And by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.7.1(vii)]</cite>, a <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme is
complete if (and only if) it is after extending <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. So we may, and do,
assume <math id="S5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall the structure theorem of Chevalley and Rosenlicht for algebraic
groups, or reduced connected group schemes of finite type over <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Co02</a>, Thm. 1.1, p. 3]</cite>. The theorem says that every algebraic
group is an extension of an Abelian variety (or complete algebraic
group) by a linear (or affine) algebraic group. Recall also that every
solvable linear algebraic <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-group is triangularizable (the
Lie–Kolchin theorem); so, if it’s nontrivial, then it contains a copy
of the multiplicative group or of the additive group; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo69</a>, (10.5)
and (10.2)]</cite>. Now, <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative, so solvable.
Hence it suffices to show that, if <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the
affine line minus the origin, then every <math id="S5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T\to(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k})_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T\to(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k})_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed, <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> has a section. So <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
arises from an invertible sheaf <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> by the Comparison
Theorem, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. Since <math id="S5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, there is a
divisor <math id="S5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D)=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D)=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Ex. II, 6.15,
p. 145]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Form the projection <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon X\times T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon X\times T\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Restrict <math id="S5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to its generic
fiber. This restriction is trivial as <math id="S5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subset of the
line. So there is a rational function <math id="S5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi)+D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi)+D</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to the trivial divisor. Let <math id="S5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\colon X\to X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\colon X\to X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> be a
section. Set <math id="S5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=s^{*}((\phi)+D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=s^{*}((\phi)+D)</annotation></semantics></math>; then <math id="S5.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-defined divisor on
<math id="S5.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, <math id="S5.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi)+D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi)+D</annotation></semantics></math> coincide as cycles; whence, they
coincide as divisors by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK70</a>, Prp. (3.10), p. 139]</cite>, since <math id="S5.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times T</annotation></semantics></math>
is normal. Therefore, <math id="S5.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=p^{*}\mathcal{O}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=p^{*}\mathcal{O}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T\to\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T\to\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is constant.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and integral. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction to <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of a Poincaré sheaf. Then a
</span>Poincaré family<span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> exists; by definition, <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative
effective divisor on <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}/P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}/P</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{P}}\bigl(W-(W_{0}\times P)\bigr)\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{P}}\bigl(W-(W_{0}\times P)\bigr)\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fiber over <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in P</annotation></semantics></math> and where <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
invertible sheaf on <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is quasi-projective by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>
and that <math id="S5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists by Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> and Exercise
<a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Since <math id="S5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, Serre’s Theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 2.2.1]</cite> implies there is an <math id="S5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.p17.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{i}f_{\smash{P}*}\mathcal{P}(n)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}f_{\smash{P}*}\mathcal{P}(n)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that
(<a href="#S4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.2</span></a>) implies (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8.3</span></a>); similarly, <math id="S5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{P}_{t}(n))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{P}_{t}(n))=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in P</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix an <math id="S5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq N</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(n))&gt;\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(n))&gt;\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say <math id="S5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the
automorphism of <math id="S5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> of multiplication by <math id="S5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly,
<math id="S5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is carried onto the connected component, <math id="S5.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> say, of <math id="S5.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon P\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon P\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the induced isomorphism. Let <math id="S5.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
restriction to <math id="S5.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a Poincaré sheaf. Plainly, <math id="S5.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{P}(n)\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{P}(n)\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaf <math id="S5.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 4.7. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, there is a coherent sheaf
<math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free of rank <math id="S5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> owing to
Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. So <math id="S5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is of rank at least <math id="S5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, there is an <math id="S5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that the sheaf <math id="S5.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SHom(\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime},\,\mathcal{O}_{P})(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SHom(\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime},\,\mathcal{O}_{P})(m)</annotation></semantics></math>
is generated by finitely many global sections; so a general linear
combination of them vanishes nowhere by a well-known lemma
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, p. 148]</cite> attributed to Serre. Hence
there is a surjection <math id="S5.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{P}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{P}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Correspondingly, there is a
<math id="S5.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="S5.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}\colon P^{\prime}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}\colon P^{\prime}\to\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}|P^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>; in other words, <math id="S5.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a section
of the restriction over <math id="S5.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Abel map <math id="S5.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}\colon\IDiv_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}\subset X_{P^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}\subset X_{P^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the pullback under <math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of the universal
relative effective divisor. Then <math id="S5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{P^{\prime}}}(W^{\prime})=\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{P^{\prime}}}(W^{\prime})=\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a section of <math id="S5.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}|P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}|P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, in particular, <math id="S5.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}(W^{\prime}_{\lambda})=\mathcal{O}_{X}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}(W^{\prime}_{\lambda})=\mathcal{O}_{X}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W:=(1\times q)^{-1}W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W:=(1\times q)^{-1}W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, <math id="S5.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is
a Poincaré family.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> holds whenever <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is proper,
whether <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is integral or not. The proof is essential the same, but
uses Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a> in place of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. In
fact, the proof of the quasi-projectivity assertion is easier, and does
not require Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, since <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is given as
contained in a quasi-projective scheme. On the other hand, the proof of
the projectivity assertion requires an additional step: the reduction,
when <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed, to the case where <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.
Here is the idea: since <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of its
irreducible components <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and plainly
<math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}=\prod\IPic_{X_{i}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}=\prod\IPic_{X_{i}/k}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, if the <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{i}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{i}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> are
complete, so is <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Chow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ch57</a>, Thm. p. 128]</cite> proved every algebraic group  — indeed,
every homogeneous variety  — is quasi-projective. Hence, in
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> , if <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced, then <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective. In characteristic 0, remarkably <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, so
reduced; this result is generally attributed to Cartier, and is proved
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, p. 167]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It follows that Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> holds in characteristic 0
whenever <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper algebraic <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-space. Indeed, the
quasi-projectivity assertion holds in view of the preceding discussion
and of the discussion at the end of Remark <a href="#S4.Thmthm18" title="Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18</span></a>. The proof of
the projectivity assertion has one more complication: it is necessary to
work with an étale covering <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to X</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme and with an
invertible sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times T</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the proof shows that, in
arbitrary characteristic, if <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper and normal algebraic
<math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-space, then <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and smooth, its geometric fibers are
irreducible, and <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Using the Valuative Criterion
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 4.7, p. 101]</cite> rather than the Chevalley–Rosenlicht
structure theorem, prove that a closed subscheme <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective over <math id="S5.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if it is of finite type.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are three interesting alternative proofs of the second assertion
of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>. The first alternative uses
Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. It was sketched by Grothendieck
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 236-12]</cite>, and runs basically as follows. Proceed by
induction on the dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, so trivially projective. If <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective by Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the proof of the theorem, reduce to the case
where <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a general hyperplane
section of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> too is normal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Se50</a>, Thm. <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>7</mn><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, p. 376]</cite>.
Plainly the inclusion <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon Y\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon Y\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}:\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}:\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By induction, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective. So <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective too if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to handle <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, Grothendieck suggested using a version of
the “known equivalence criteria.” In this connection, he
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 236-02]</cite> announced that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 2</a>]</cite> contains some key
preliminary results, which must be combined with the existence theorems
for the Picard scheme. In fact, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 2</a>, Cor. 3.6, p. 153]</cite> does
directly imply <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective for <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
generically finite. So <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite since it is homogeneous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl73</a>, Lem. 3.11, p. 639, and Rem. 3.12, p. 640]</cite>
assert <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and unipotent, so trivial in characteristic
0; however, the proofs in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl73</a>]</cite> require the compactness of
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. It would be good to have a direct proof of this
assertion also when <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>, a proof in the spirit of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0, Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm67</a>, p. 99]</cite> proved as follows
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes. Suppose not. First of all, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
reduced by Cartier’s theorem. So <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a point <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
of order <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> defines an unramified Galois cover <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}/X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}/X</annotation></semantics></math>
with group <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/n</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}:=Y\times_{X}X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}:=Y\times_{X}X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint
union of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other
hand, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is ample since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is; hence, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected (by
Corollary <a href="#A2.Thmthm29" title="Corollary B.29. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.29</span></a> for example). We have a contradiction. Thus
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0, the injectivity of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> also follows from the
Kodaira Vanishing Theorem. Indeed, as just noted, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
reduced. So <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective if and only if its differential is
zero. Now, this differential is, owing to Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>
below, equal to the natural map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Its kernel is equal to <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-1))</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the long exact
sequence of cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0, if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-1))</annotation></semantics></math>
vanishes by the Kodaira Vanishing Theorem. If also <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then the dual
group <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes by the theorem on the
regularity of the adjoint system, which was proved by Picard in 1906
using Abelian integrals and by Severi in 1908 using algebro-geometric
methods. For more information, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za35</a>, pp. 181, 204–206]</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm67</a>, pp. 94–97]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there are, as Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 236-12]</cite>
suggested, finitely many smooth irreducible curves <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the induced map is injective:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}^{0}\to\textstyle\prod\IPic_{Y_{i}/k}^{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}^{0}\to\textstyle\prod\IPic_{Y_{i}/k}^{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.2" class="ltx_p">So, again, since the <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{Y_{i}/k}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{Y_{i}/k}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are projective, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is projective too.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To find the <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, use the final version of the “equivalence criteria”
proved by Weil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">We54</a>, Cor. 2, p. 159]</cite>; it says in other words that,
if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W/T</annotation></semantics></math> is the family of smooth 1-dimensional linear-space sections of
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (or even a nonempty open subfamily), then the induced map is
injective:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}^{0}(k)\to\IPic_{W/T}^{0}(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}^{0}(k)\to\IPic_{W/T}^{0}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For each finite set <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be the kernel
of the map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}^{0}\to\textstyle\prod_{t\in F}\IPic_{W_{t}/k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}^{0}\to\textstyle\prod_{t\in F}\IPic_{W_{t}/k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed, we may assume, by Noetherian induction, that
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> contains no strictly smaller <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point. It yields a nonzero <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{W/T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{W/T}</annotation></semantics></math>, so a nonzero
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{W_{t}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{W_{t}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the
union of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly smaller than <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Take the <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0 or if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>, another way to finish is to take a
desingularization <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, a divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is principal if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta^{*}D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta^{*}D</annotation></semantics></math> is principal; hence, the induced map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta^{*}:\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta^{*}:\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.2" class="ltx_p">is injective. But <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective by
Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. So <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective too.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The second alternative proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> is similar to the
proof in Answer <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. (It too may be due to
Grothendieck — see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 21.14.4, iv]</cite> — but was
indicated to the author by Mumford in a private communication in 1974).
Again we reduce to the case where <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. Then,
using a more refined form of the Valuative Criterion (obtained modifying
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Ex. 4.11, p. 107]</cite> slightly), we need only check this
statement: given a <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Spec(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Spec(C)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is
a complete discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue
field <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and with fraction field <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> say, and given a divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, its closure <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor on <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.1" class="ltx_p">This statement follows from the Ramanujam–Samuel Theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 21.14.1]</cite>, a result in commutative algebra. Apply it
taking <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to be the local ring of a closed point of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to be the
local ring of the image point in <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The hypotheses hold because
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> share the residue field <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The completion <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
normal domain by a theorem of Zariski’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZS60</a>, Thm. 32, p. 320]</cite>.
And <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\to B</annotation></semantics></math> is formally smooth because <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a formal power series
ring over <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by a theorem of Cohen’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZS60</a>, Thm. 32, p. 320]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The third alternative proof is somewhat like the second, but involves
some geometry instead of the Ramanujam–Samuel Theorem; see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK74</a>, Thm. 19, p. 138]</cite>. Moreover, <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> need not be complete, just
discrete, and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be algebraically closed. Here is the idea.
Let <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset X_{k_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset X_{k_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the closed fiber of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}/T</annotation></semantics></math>. In
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X_{L}/L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X_{L}/L}</annotation></semantics></math>, form the sets <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> parameterizing the
divisorial cycles linearly equivalent to those of the form <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{L}+H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{L}+H</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{L}+H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{L}+H</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over the divisors whose associated sheaves are
algebraically equivalent to <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{L}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{L}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for a suitably large <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">It can be shown that <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are dense open subsets of the same
irreducible component of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X_{L}/L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X_{L}/L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence they have a common
point. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the ideals of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there are
invertible sheaves <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{L}\otimes\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{L}\otimes\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{L}\otimes\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{L}\otimes\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic. Since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, so is
<math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence so is <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor, as desired.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p16.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily projective, but is simply
complete and normal, then <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is still complete, whether <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is a scheme or algebraic space. Indeed, the original proof and its
second alternative work without essential change. The first and third
alternatives require <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be projective. However, it is easy to see
as follows
that this case implies the general case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm8.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, we may assume <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. By Chow’s lemma,
there is a projective variety <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and a birational map <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are normal, a divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the divisor of a
function <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}D</annotation></semantics></math> is the divisor of <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}h</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence the induced map <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. It
follows, as above, that <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is complete since <math id="S5.Thmthm8.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{Y/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be
invertible sheaves on <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">algebraically
equivalent to</span> <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for some <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exist a connected <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme of finite type <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, geometric
points <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i},\,t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i},\,t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same field, and an invertible sheaf
<math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{1,s_{1}},\ \mathcal{M}_{1,t_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{2,s_{2}},\ \dotsc,\ \mathcal{M}_{n-1,t_{n-1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{n,s_{n}},\ \mathcal{M}_{n,t_{n}}\simeq\mathcal{N}_{t_{n}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{1,s_{1}},\ \mathcal{M}_{1,t_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{2,s_{2}},\ \dotsc,\ \mathcal{M}_{n-1,t_{n-1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{n,s_{n}},\ \mathcal{M}_{n,t_{n}}\simeq\mathcal{N}_{t_{n}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf
on <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding point. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically equivalent to <math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S5.Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically equivalent to <math id="S5.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and use the
notation of Definition <a href="#S5.Thmthm9" title="Definition 5.9. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>. Then <math id="S5.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map
<math id="S5.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}\colon T_{i}\to\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}\colon T_{i}\to\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S5.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{n,t_{n}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t_{n}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{n,t_{n}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t_{n}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S5.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{n}(t_{n})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{n}(t_{n})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
<math id="S5.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}(t_{i})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}(t_{i})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}(T_{i})\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}(T_{i})\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
since <math id="S5.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. So <math id="S5.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}(s_{i})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}(s_{i})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S5.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i,s_{i}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{i-1,t_{i-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i,s_{i}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{i-1,t_{i-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="S5.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i-1}(t_{i-1})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i-1}(t_{i-1})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Descending induction yields
<math id="S5.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}(s_{1})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}(s_{1})\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S5.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,s_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{L}_{s_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,s_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{L}_{s_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S5.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="S5.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inclusion
<math id="S5.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by an invertible sheaf <math id="S5.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some fppf covering <math id="S5.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\,t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\,t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
be geometric points of <math id="S5.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> lying over <math id="S5.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\,0\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\,0\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},\,T_{2}\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},\,T_{2}\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> be irreducible components containing <math id="S5.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\,t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\,t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S5.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}^{\prime},\,T_{2}^{\prime}\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}^{\prime},\,T_{2}^{\prime}\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> their images. The latter contain
open subsets because an fppf map is open by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.4.6]</cite>. Since
<math id="S5.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> , these open
subsets contain a common point. Say it is the image of geometric points
<math id="S5.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},\,s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},\,s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},\,T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},\,T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,t_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{2,s_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,t_{1}}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{2,s_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Exercise 2.6. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. Set <math id="S5.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}:=\mathcal{M}|T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}:=\mathcal{M}|T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraically equivalent to <math id="S5.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
tangent space at <math id="S5.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}=\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}=\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be any <math id="S5.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme locally of finite type, <math id="S5.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in P</annotation></semantics></math> a rational point, <math id="S5.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> its local ring, and <math id="S5.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math> its maximal ideal.
Usually, by the “tangent space” <math id="S5.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{e}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{e}P</annotation></semantics></math> is meant the Zariski
tangent space <math id="S5.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{m}^{2},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{m}^{2},k)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as in differential
geometry, <math id="S5.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{e}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{e}P</annotation></semantics></math> may be viewed as the vector space of
<math id="S5.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-derivations <math id="S5.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\colon A\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\colon A\to k</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, <math id="S5.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\mathbf{m}^{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\mathbf{m}^{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math>; so
<math id="S5.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> induces a linear map <math id="S5.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{m}^{2}\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{m}^{2}\to k</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, every
such linear map arises from a <math id="S5.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is unique because
<math id="S5.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ring of “dual numbers,” the ring obtained from <math id="S5.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
by adjoining an element <math id="S5.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then any (fixed)
derivation <math id="S5.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> induces a local homomorphism of <math id="S5.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras
<math id="S5.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon A\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon A\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(a):=\overline{a}+\delta(a)\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(a):=\overline{a}+\delta(a)\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{a}\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{a}\in k</annotation></semantics></math> is the residue of <math id="S5.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, every such <math id="S5.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> arises from a
unique <math id="S5.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, to give a <math id="S5.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as to give a <math id="S5.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-map
<math id="S5.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> from the “free tangent vector” <math id="S5.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(k_{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(k_{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the image of <math id="S5.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> has support at <math id="S5.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the set of <math id="S5.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, as sets,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}P=P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}P=P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.11.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">The vector space structure on <math id="S5.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}P</annotation></semantics></math> transfers as follows. Given <math id="S5.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in k</annotation></semantics></math>, define a <math id="S5.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra homomorphism <math id="S5.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{a}\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{a}\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{a}\varepsilon:=a\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{a}\varepsilon:=a\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, let <math id="S5.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> be a derivation, and <math id="S5.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> the
corresponding homomorphism. Then <math id="S5.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\delta</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S5.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{a}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{a}u</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus multiplication by <math id="S5.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> transfers as <math id="S5.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mu_{a})\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mu_{a})\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ring obtained from <math id="S5.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining
an element <math id="S5.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\varepsilon^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\varepsilon^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Define a
homomorphism <math id="S5.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\mapsto\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\mapsto\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Define another <math id="S5.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\mapsto 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\mapsto 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S5.p27.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> induce a map of sets</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon P(k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\times P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon P(k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\times P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p27.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p27.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p27.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}})_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of maps with image supported at
<math id="S5.p27.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="S5.p27.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is bijective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define a third homomorphism <math id="S5.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\mapsto\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\mapsto\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{\prime}\mapsto\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Given two derivations <math id="S5.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\,\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\,\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, define a homomorphism <math id="S5.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon A\to k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon A\to k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(a):=\overline{a}+\delta(a)\varepsilon+\delta^{\prime}(a)\varepsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(a):=\overline{a}+\delta(a)\varepsilon+\delta^{\prime}(a)\varepsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta+\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta+\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to
<math id="S5.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma v</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, addition on <math id="S5.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{e}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{e}P</annotation></semantics></math> transfers as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex3a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\times P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\text{ where }\alpha:=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\times P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\to P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}\text{ where }\alpha:=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S5.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a group scheme, <math id="S5.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in P</annotation></semantics></math> the identity. The natural ring
homomorphism <math id="S5.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k</annotation></semantics></math> induces a group homomorphism
<math id="S5.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\rho)\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})\to P(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\rho)\colon P(k_{\varepsilon})\to P(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly</span></p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}=\ker P(\rho)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}=\ker P(\rho).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.11.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p29.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p29.2.1" class="ltx_text">The left side <math id="S5.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{0}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{0}P</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector space; the right side is a group.
Does addition on the left match multiplication on the right? Yes,
indeed! We know <math id="S5.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the addition map. We must show <math id="S5.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is
also the multiplication map. Let us do so.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> arise from ring homomorphisms, both are
group homomorphisms. Now <math id="S5.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S5.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a group homomorphism too. So</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(m,n)=\alpha(m,e)\cdot\alpha(e,n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(m,n)=\alpha(m,e)\cdot\alpha(e,n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p30.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p30.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity. So we have to show
<math id="S5.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(m,e)=m\text{ and }\alpha(e,n)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(m,e)=m\text{ and }\alpha(e,n)=n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the inclusion <math id="S5.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly
<math id="S5.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S5.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S5.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\sigma_{2})P(\iota)(m)=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\sigma_{2})P(\iota)(m)=e</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in P(k_{\varepsilon})_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to
Formula (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.2</span></a>). On the other hand, <math id="S5.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is the
identity of <math id="S5.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi P(\iota)(m)=(m,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi P(\iota)(m)=(m,e)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly <math id="S5.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\iota\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is also the identity of <math id="S5.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S5.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\sigma)P(\iota)(m)=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\sigma)P(\iota)(m)=m</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(m,e)=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}\pi P(\iota)(m)=m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(m,e)=P(\sigma)\pi^{-1}\pi P(\iota)(m)=m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p32.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p32.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly <math id="S5.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(e,n)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(e,n)=n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the multiplication map.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="S5.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then Formulas (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.1</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.2</span></a>) yield</span></p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}=\ker\bigl(\IPic_{X/k}(k_{\varepsilon})\to\IPic_{X/k}(k)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}=\ker\bigl(\IPic_{X/k}(k_{\varepsilon})\to\IPic_{X/k}(k)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.11.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">To compute this kernel, set <math id="S5.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\varepsilon}:=X\otimes_{k}k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\varepsilon}:=X\otimes_{k}k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, and form the truncated
exponential sequence of sheaves of Abelian groups:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex6a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to 1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p34.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p34.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the first map takes a local section <math id="S5.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+b\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+b\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This
sequence is split by the map <math id="S5.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
<math id="S5.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\mapsto a+0\cdot\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\mapsto a+0\cdot\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence taking cohomology yields this split exact
sequence of Abelian groups:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\to 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\to 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">However, <math id="S5.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S5.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the sheaf
associated to the presheaf <math id="S5.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\mapsto\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\mapsto\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. So
there is a natural commutative square of groups</span></p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<span id="S5.E4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S5.E4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.E4.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 18.92523pt\kern-18.92523pt\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 18.92523pt\kern-18.92523pt\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{\varepsilon}}^{*})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S5.E4.1.2" class="ltx_p">   <span id="S5.E4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.E4.m1.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>      <span id="S5.E4.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.E4.m1.m2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S5.E4.1.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.E4.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 18.92523pt\kern-16.46967pt\IPic_{X/k}(k_{\varepsilon})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to19.5578pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\IPic_{X/k}(k)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext class="undefined"><span class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\IPic</span></mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 18.92523pt\kern-16.46967pt\IPic_{X/k}(k_{\varepsilon})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to19.5578pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\IPic_{X/k}(k).</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.11.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p35.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p35.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, there is an induced homomorphism between the horizontal kernels.
Owing to Formula (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.3</span></a>), this homomorphism is an additive
map</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in k</annotation></semantics></math>. On <math id="S5.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, scalar multiplication by <math id="S5.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is,
owing to the discussion after Formula (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.1</span></a>), the map
induced by <math id="S5.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{a}\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{a}\colon k_{\varepsilon}\to k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S5.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> induces an
endomorphism of the above square. At the top, it arises from the map of
sheaves of groups <math id="S5.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S5.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\mapsto a\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\mapsto a\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. So the induced endomorphism of <math id="S5.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math> is scalar
multiplication by <math id="S5.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p36.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a map of <math id="S5.p36.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text">Square (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.4</span></a>) maps to the corresponding square obtained by
making a field extension <math id="S5.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the kernels are vector spaces,
there is an induced square</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<span id="S5.Ex9.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S5.Ex9.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 29.05283pt\kern-29.05283pt\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes_{k}K\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{K}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 29.05283pt\kern-29.05283pt\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes_{k}K\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{K}})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S5.Ex9.1.2" class="ltx_p">    <span id="S5.Ex9.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle v\otimes_{k}K$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">K</mi></mpadded><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle v\otimes_{k}K$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>       <span id="S5.Ex9.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1.m2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded><mo mathsize="1.600em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mpadded style="width:0.0pt;" width="0.0pt"></mpadded></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{\hss$\scriptstyle$}}\Big\downarrow\vbox{\hbox to0.0pt{$\scriptstyle$\hss}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S5.Ex9.1.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 29.05283pt\kern-24.4886pt\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}\otimes_{k}K\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.74388pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X_{K}/K}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>T</mtext></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 29.05283pt\kern-24.4886pt\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}\otimes_{k}K\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.74388pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X_{K}/K}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p37.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p37.2.1" class="ltx_text">The two horizontal maps are isomorphisms. Hence, if the right-hand
map is an isomorphism, so is <math id="S5.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we may assume <math id="S5.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraically closed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">In Square (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11.4</span></a>), the two vertical maps are isomorphisms by
Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> since <math id="S5.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S5.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. Therefore, <math id="S5.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
too is an isomorphism, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a relative version of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>. Namely,
assume <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, represents <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is
locally of finite type, but let <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{1}f_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{1}f_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the normal sheaf of <math id="S5.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> along the identity
section, its “Lie algebra”; the latter is simply the dual of the
restriction to this section of the sheaf of relative differentials. For
more information, see the recent treatment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLR</a>, § 1]</cite> and the
references it cites.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field
<math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\IPic_{X/k}\leq\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\IPic_{X/k}\leq\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equality holds if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth at <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0;</annotation></semantics></math> if so, then
<math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm13.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math>
everywhere.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly we may assume <math id="S5.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. Then,
given any closed point <math id="S5.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an automorphism
of <math id="S5.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> that carries 0 to <math id="S5.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, “multiplication”
by <math id="S5.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S5.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same dimension at <math id="S5.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as
at 0, and <math id="S5.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth at <math id="S5.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it is
smooth at 0.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">By general principles, <math id="S5.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{0}\IPic_{X/k}\leq\dim\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{0}\IPic_{X/k}\leq\dim\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and equality holds if and only if <math id="S5.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular at <math id="S5.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S5.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular at <math id="S5.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it is smooth
at <math id="S5.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed. Therefore, the corollary
results from Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is of characteristic <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is of characteristic <math id="S5.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, any group scheme
locally of finite type over <math id="S5.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth by Cartier’s theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Thm. 1, p. 167]</cite>. So the assertion follows from
Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over a field <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of positive characteristic, <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be
smooth, even when <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected smooth projective surface.
Examples were constructed by Igusa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ig55</a>]</cite> and Serre
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sr56</a>, n<sup id="S5.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">∘</sup> 20]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Lect. 27, pp. 193–198]</cite> proved that
<math id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth if and only if all of Serre’s Bockstein
operations <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish; here</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}:\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\text{ and }\beta_{i}\colon\ker\beta_{i-1}\to\cok\beta_{i-1}\text{ for }i\geq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\cok</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}:\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\to\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\text{ and }\beta_{i}\colon\ker\beta_{i-1}\to\cok\beta_{i-1}\text{ for }i\geq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In fact, the tangent space to <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subspace of <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}=\bigcap\ker\beta_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{0}\IPic_{X_{\red}/k}=\bigcap\ker\beta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, here <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> need not be smooth or 2-dimensional.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The examples illustrate further pathologies. Set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:=\dim\IPic_{X/k},\ h^{0,1}:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}),\text{ and }h^{1,0}:=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{1}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:=\dim\IPic_{X/k},\ h^{0,1}:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}),\text{ and }h^{1,0}:=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{1}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In Igusa’s example, <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0,1}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0,1}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1,0}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1,0}=2</annotation></semantics></math>; in Serre’s,
<math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0,1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0,1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1,0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1,0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, Igusa had just proved
that, in any event, <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq h^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq h^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm15.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, in characteristic 0, Serre’s Comparison Theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 2.1, p. 440]</cite> says that <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be
computed by viewing <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as a complex analytic manifold. Hence Hodge
Theory yields</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0,1}=h^{1,0}\text{ and }h^{0,1}+h^{1,0}=b" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0,1}=h^{1,0}\text{ and }h^{0,1}+h^{1,0}=b</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.Thmthm15.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the first Betti number; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za35</a>, p. 200]</cite>.
Therefore, the following exercise now yields the Fundamental Theorem of
Irregular Surfaces (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 5.16</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective,
smooth, and geometrically irreducible surface. According to the
original definitions as stated in modern terms, the “geometric genus”
of <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the number <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{g}:=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{g}:=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math>; its
“arithmetic genus” is the number <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}:=\phi(0)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}:=\phi(0)-1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is
the polynomial such that <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n)=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n)=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>; and its “irregularity” <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the difference between the two
genera, <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:=p_{g}-p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:=p_{g}-p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Show <math id="S5.Thmthm16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\IPic_{X/k}\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\IPic_{X/k}\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>, with equality in characteristic 0.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 5.17</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and integral. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Show <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if every algebraic system of curves is
“contained completely in a linear system.” The latter condition means
just that, given any relative effective divisor <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
is a connected <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, there exist invertible sheaves <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition may be put more geometrically: in the notation of
Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Exercise 3.14. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>, it means there is a map, necessarily unique,
<math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\colon T\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\colon T\to L</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times w)^{-1}W=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times w)^{-1}W=D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm17.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm17.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In characteristic 0, show <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> if the condition
holds for all smooth such <math id="S5.Thmthm17.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.18</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/k</annotation></semantics></math> is projective
and geometrically integral. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective
divisor, and <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> its normal sheaf. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> be
the point representing <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> the point
representing <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then the tangent space at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the formula</p>
<table id="S5.E1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.18.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which respects the vector space structure of each side. This formula
can be proved with a simple elementary computation; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Cor.,
p. 154]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Form the fundamental exact sequence of sheaves</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\to\mathcal{N}_{D}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\to\mathcal{N}_{D}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and consider its associated long exact sequence of cohomology groups</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Ex2c.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 2.5pt\kern-2.5pt0\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.56119pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 2.5pt\kern-2.5pt0\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.56119pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Ex2c.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom">
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.Ex2c.1.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Ex2c.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kern 2.5pt\kern-2.5pt0\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{\partial^{0}}$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.56119pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{u}$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{\partial^{1}}$}\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mover><mpadded style="width:24.0pt;" width="24.0pt"><mtext> → </mtext></mpadded><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>0</mn></msup></mover><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mtext> → </mtext><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mover><mpadded style="width:20.6pt;" width="20.6pt"><mtext> → </mtext></mpadded><mi>u</mi></mover><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mover><mpadded style="width:24.0pt;" width="24.0pt"><mtext> → </mtext></mpadded><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msup></mover><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kern 2.5pt\kern-2.5pt0\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{\partial^{0}}$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to15.83313pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to20.56119pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{u}$}\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D})\hbox{$\mathop{\hbox to24.00644pt{\kern 3.75pt\rightarrowfill\kern 3.75pt}}\limits^{\partial^{1}}$}\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Another elementary computation shows that the boundary map <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
is equal to the tangent map of the Abel map, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{T}_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}\to T_{\lambda}\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>T</mtext><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{T}_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}\to T_{\lambda}\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Prp., p. 165]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be “semiregular” if the boundary map
<math id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. Plainly, it is equivalent that <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=0</annotation></semantics></math>. So it
is equivalent that <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D})=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}_{D})=R</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))-1-\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))-1-\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Semiregularity was recognized in 1944 by Severi as precisely the right
positivity condition for the old (1904) theorem of the completeness of
the characteristic system, although Severi formulated the condition in
an equivalent dual manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In its modern formulation, the theorem of the completeness of the
characteristic system asserts that <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension
<math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is semiregular, provided the
characteristic is 0, or more generally, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.
Indeed, Formula (<a href="#S5.E1a" title="In Remark 5.18. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.18.1</span></a>) says that the characteristic
system of <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is always equal to the complete linear
system of the invertible sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> can have a
nilpotent or a singularity at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. So, in effect, Enriques and
Severi had simply sought conditions guaranteeing <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth
of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The first purely algebraic discussion of the theorem was made by
Grothendieck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Sects. 221-5.4 to 5.6]</cite>, and he proved it in the
two most important cases. Specifically, he noted that, if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Abel map is smooth; see the end of the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth; furthermore, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth in characteristic zero by Cartier’s theorem. Grothendieck
pointed out that this case had been treated with transcendental means by
Kodaira in 1956.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Grothendieck also observed <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{N}_{D})</annotation></semantics></math> houses the obstruction to
deforming <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, if this group vanishes, then <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is smooth at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in any characteristic. Mumford
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, pp. 157–159]</cite> explicitly worked out the obstruction and its
image under <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is semiregular,
then <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth if this image vanishes. Inspired by work
of Kodaira and Spencer in 1959, Mumford used the exponential in
characteristic 0 and proved the image vanishes. Mumford did not use
Cartier’s theorem; so the latter results from taking a <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A purely algebraic proof of the full completeness theorem is given in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl73</a>, Thm., p. 307]</cite>. This proof was inspired by Kempf’s
(unpublished) thesis. The proof does not use obstruction theory, but
only simple formal properties of a scheme of the form <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> arises from an invertible sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. This proof works, more generally, if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
arbitrary and if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat and has integral geometric
fibers. Here <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the divisor on the geometric fiber through
<math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then provided <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth of relative dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is
semiregular.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.1" class="ltx_p">There is a celebrated example, valid over an algebraically closed field <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
of any characteristic, where <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonreduced at <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The example was discovered by Severi and Zappa in the 1940s, and is
explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, pp. 155–156]</cite>. Here is the idea. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be
an elliptic curve, and <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}\to\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> the
nontrivial extension; set <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm18.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the section of <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/C</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{D}=\mathcal{O}_{\!C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{D}=\mathcal{O}_{\!C}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. 2.8, p. 372]</cite>; so <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim T_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim T_{\delta}\IDiv_{X/k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is an isolated point.
Otherwise, the connected component of <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> contains a second closed
point. And it represents a curve <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> algebraically equivalent to <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!D}(D^{\prime})=\deg\mathcal{N}_{D}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!D}(D^{\prime})=\deg\mathcal{N}_{D}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are
disjoint. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a fiber. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!F}(D)=\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!F}(D^{\prime})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!F}(D)=\deg\mathcal{O}_{\!F}(D^{\prime})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a second section. Therefore, <math id="S5.Thmthm18.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is
decomposable, a contradiction.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.19</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> be a point such that <math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there
exists an open neighborhood of <math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> over which <math id="S5.Thmthm19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the Semicontinuity Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.7.5-I)]</cite>, there exists an
open neighborhood <math id="S5.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. Replace <math id="S5.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, <span id="S5.p43.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span>-10.3.1, p. 20]</cite>, there is a flat local homomorphism
from <math id="S5.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> into a Noetherian local ring <math id="S5.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> whose residue field is
algebraically closed. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 6.8.3]</cite>, if <math id="S5.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, then
<math id="S5.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth along <math id="S5.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Replace <math id="S5.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the Infinitesimal Criterion for Smoothness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 1</a>, p. 67]</cite>, it
suffices to show this: given any <math id="S5.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S5.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S5.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Spec(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Spec(A)</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an Artin local ring that is a finite <math id="S5.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra and
given any closed subscheme <math id="S5.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> whose ideal <math id="S5.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> has square
0, every <math id="S5.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.p44.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a <math id="S5.p44.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">The residue field of <math id="S5.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite extension of <math id="S5.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
algebraically closed; so the two fields are equal. Hence the <math id="S5.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point
is defined by an invertible sheaf on <math id="S5.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. So
we want to show invertible sheaves on <math id="S5.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> lift to <math id="S5.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we can form the truncated exponential sequence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1g.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{R}}^{*}\to 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{R}}^{*}\to 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p46.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p46.2.1" class="ltx_text">It yields the following exact sequence:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{R}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{R}}^{*})\to\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p46.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p46.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence it suffices to show <math id="S5.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p47.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is affine, <math id="S5.p47.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=H^{0}(\textup{R}^{2}f_{T*}f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=H^{0}(\textup{R}^{2}f_{T*}f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> owing to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. 8.5, p. 251]</cite>. But <math id="S5.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by the
Property of Exchange <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.7.5 II and 7.7.10]</cite>. Thus <math id="S5.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(f_{T}^{*}{\mathcal{I}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.20</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and represents <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, assume all the <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth of the same
dimension. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> has an open group subscheme
<math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type whose fibers are the <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, if <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced, then <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if all the <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are complete and if
<math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated over <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in
<math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> and proper over <math id="S5.Thmthm20.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text">First off, <math id="S5.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally of finite type by
Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 4.17. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a>. Now,
for every <math id="S5.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, the schemes <math id="S5.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. And the
<math id="S5.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth of the same dimension by hypothesis. So
the <math id="S5.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> form an open subscheme <math id="S5.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S5.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> and the structure map <math id="S5.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is
universally open by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 15.6.3 and 15.6.4]</cite>. Furthermore, if
<math id="S5.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced, then <math id="S5.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is flat by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 15.6.7]</cite>, so
smooth by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 17.5.1]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p49.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p49.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define a map <math id="S5.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\times_{S}\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\times_{S}\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S5.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(g,h):=gh^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(g,h):=gh^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> factors through the open
subscheme <math id="S5.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> because forming <math id="S5.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with passing
to the fibers. Hence <math id="S5.p49.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p50.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove <math id="S5.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type, we may work locally on <math id="S5.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so assume <math id="S5.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Since <math id="S5.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
locally of finite type, we need only prove <math id="S5.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonempty affine open subscheme, and
<math id="S5.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\sigma^{-1}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\sigma^{-1}V</annotation></semantics></math> another. Then <math id="S5.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma U\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma U\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> is open since
<math id="S5.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is an open map. Set <math id="S5.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}:=\sigma^{-1}\sigma U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}:=\sigma^{-1}\sigma U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
open, and <math id="S5.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to a map <math id="S5.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}\colon U\times_{V}U\to U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}\colon U\times_{V}U\to U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact,
<math id="S5.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective because its geometric fibers are surjective by
an argument at the end of the proof of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Now,
<math id="S5.p51.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\times_{V}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\times_{V}U</annotation></semantics></math> so quasi-compact. Hence <math id="S5.p51.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S5.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=S-\sigma U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=S-\sigma U</annotation></semantics></math>. By Noetherian induction, we may assume
<math id="S5.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-1}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-1}T</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact. Therefore, <math id="S5.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}\cup\sigma^{-1}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}\cup\sigma^{-1}T</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact. But it is equal to <math id="S5.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is quasi-compact, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, if all the <math id="S5.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are complete and if
<math id="S5.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated over <math id="S5.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper over
<math id="S5.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 15.7.11]</cite>. Finally, consider the inclusion map
<math id="S5.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is proper as <math id="S5.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper and <math id="S5.p53.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated; hence, <math id="S5.p53.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.21</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume the characteristic is 0 and <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper.
Then, in the last two propositions, more can be said, as Vistoli
explained to the author in early May 2004. These additions result from
Part (i) of Theorem (5.5) on p. 123 in Deligne’s article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">De68</a>]</cite>
(which uses Hodge theory on p. 121). Deligne’s theorem asserts that,
under the present conditions, all the sheaves <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{q}f_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{q}f_{*}\Omega^{p}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
are locally free of finite rank, and forming them commutes with changing
the base.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 5.19. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.19</span></a>, <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is smooth over the
connected component <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Indeed, Deligne’s theorem implies
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{2}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X/S}\big|S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{2}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{X/S}\big|S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes since it is locally free, so of
constant rank, and its formation commutes with passage to every fiber,
in particular, to that over <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 5.19. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.19</span></a>, as it stands, implies <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth
over <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a>, <span id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">there is no need to assume all the
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth of the same dimension.</span> Indeed, all the
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})}</annotation></semantics></math> by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>. But <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on each connected component of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> owing to
Deligne’s theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, if <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and projective Zariski
locally over <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth whether or not <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
reduced.</span> Indeed, we may assume <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type over <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; the
reduction is standard, and sketched by Deligne at the beginning of his
proof of his Theorem (5.5). By the Infinitesimal Criterion for
Smoothness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">SGA 1</a>, p. 67]</cite>, it suffices to show this: given any
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Spec(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Spec(A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an Artin local ring
that is a finite <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra and given any closed subscheme
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset T</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point.
But, <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by the above argument.
Hence it suffices to show every <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The residue field of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite extension of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; so the two
fields are equal. Hence the <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point is defined by an invertible sheaf
on <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. So we want to show every
invertible sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Serre’s
Comparison Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 2.1, p. 440]</cite>, it suffices to lift
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to an analytic invertible sheaf since <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective. So pass now to the analytic category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closed fiber of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and form the exponential
sequence:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1h" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1h.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathbb{Z}_{X_{0}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}^{*}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathbb{Z}_{X_{0}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}^{*}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Consider the class of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. It
maps to 0 in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathbb{Z}_{X_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathbb{Z}_{X_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> because this group is discrete and
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Form the exponential map <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}\to\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, form its kernel
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and form the natural map <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\colon\mathcal{Z}\to Z_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\colon\mathcal{Z}\to Z_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is bijective. Indeed, let <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be a local section of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+a+a^{2}/2+\dotsb=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+a+a^{2}/2+\dotsb=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:=1+a/2+\dotsb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:=1+a/2+\dotsb</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="au=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">au=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> maps to the local section 0 of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is
nilpotent. So <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is
injective. But <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is obviously surjective. Thus <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is
bijective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm21.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the class <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. It
follows that <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> maps to 0 in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
comes from a class <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Deligne’s
theorem, <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a class <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The image of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X_{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> gives the desired
lifting of <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmthm21.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_eg">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.22</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following example complements Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a>, and was
provided, in early May 2004, by Vistoli. The example shows
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">can be smooth and proper over <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and open and
closed in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, although <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> isn’t smooth.</span> In fact,
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and projective, its geometric fibers are integral,
and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve over an algebraically closed field <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of
arbitrary characteristic. Furthermore, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> has a component
that is a reduced <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point; so it is not smooth over <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, nor even
flat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To construct <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\mathbb{P}^{3}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\mathbb{P}^{3}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and fix <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}:=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{P}(d))^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}:=\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{P}(d))^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> where the ‘<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math>’ means dual. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> represents the functor (on <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) whose
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are the <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-flat closed subschemes of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fibers are
surfaces of degree <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm17" title="Exercise 5.17. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.17</span></a> and
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset P\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset P\times H</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal
subscheme. Its ideal <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P\times H}(-W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P\times H}(-W)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the tensor product
of the pullbacks of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P}(-d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P}(-d)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Exercise 3.14. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=\dim H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=\dim H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the Grassmannian of lines in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset P\times G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset P\times G</annotation></semantics></math> the
universal line. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon L\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon L\to G</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection. Form the exact
sequence of locally free sheaves</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1i" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1i.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{K}\to\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{P\times G}(d)\to\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(d)\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{K}\to\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{P\times G}(d)\to\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(d)\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which defines <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>; the right-hand map is surjective, since forming
it commutes with passing to the fibers, and on the fibers, it is plainly
surjective. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{K}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I:=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{K}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, irreducible,
and of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4+N-(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4+N-(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-d+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-d+3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{P\times G}(d)=\mathcal{Q}^{*}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{P\times G}(d)=\mathcal{Q}^{*}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. So there is a surjection
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{G}\to\mathcal{K}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{G}\to\mathcal{K}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it induces a closed embedding <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset G\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset G\times H</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, given a <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times H</annotation></semantics></math>, it lies in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> if and
only if <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{T}\subset W_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{T}\subset W_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the latter means the ideal of
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{T}\subset P_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{T}\subset P_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> maps to 0 in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>; in other words, the
composition</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{P_{T}}(-d)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{P_{T}}\to O_{P_{T}}\to\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{P_{T}}(-d)\otimes\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{P_{T}}\to O_{P_{T}}\to\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.2" class="ltx_p">vanishes. Equivalently, the composition</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3d.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{T}\to g_{T*}O_{P_{T}}(d)\to g_{T*}\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}(d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{T}\to g_{T*}O_{P_{T}}(d)\to g_{T*}\mathcal{O}_{L_{T}}(d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.3" class="ltx_p">vanishes. But the first map is equal to the natural map <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{T}\to\mathcal{Q}^{*}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{H}(-1)_{T}\to\mathcal{Q}^{*}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. So it is equivalent that this map factors
through <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, or that <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{O}_{H}(1)_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{T}\to\mathcal{O}_{H}(1)_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{*}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{*}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, the <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times H</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph of the incidence correspondence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Note <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\neq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\neq H</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim I=\dim H-d+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim I=\dim H-d+3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the open set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> over which <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap J</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty. Indeed, choose coordinates
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w,x,y,z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w,x,y,z</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap J</annotation></semantics></math> contains the point representing the
surface <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{w^{d}-x^{d}=y^{d}-z^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w^{d}-x^{d}=y^{d}-z^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\nmid d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\nmid d</annotation></semantics></math> or the surface
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{wx^{d-1}=y^{d-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{wx^{d-1}=y^{d-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mid d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mid d</annotation></semantics></math>, because, in either case, the
surface is smooth and contains a line, either <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w=x,\ y=z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w=x,\ y=z\}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w=0,\ y=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w=0,\ y=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in U\cap J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in U\cap J</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point. Take a line <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset H</annotation></semantics></math>
through <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and transverse to <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Replace <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\/" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\/</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap U</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> be the preimage of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> the induced map.
Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and projective, and its geometric fibers are
integral. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, is separated, and represents
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Moreover, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in S</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> implies
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{t}/k_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{t}/k_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced and discrete. In particular,
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{t}/k_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{t}/k_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, of dimension 0, and complete. Therefore,
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a> implies <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper
over <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and open and closed in <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in J</annotation></semantics></math>, the surface <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> contains a line <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. And
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth since <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in U</annotation></semantics></math>; hence <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor. Say
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> defines the <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. View <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> as
a reduced closed subscheme. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of its
fiber <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{s}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{s}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> because, as just noted, this fiber is reduced and
discrete. It remains to prove <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose not, and let’s find a contradiction. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be the connected
component of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ring of “dual
numbers,” and set <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=\Spec(k_{\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=\Spec(k_{\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a closed embedding
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> supported at <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> does not embed
into the fiber <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{s}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{s}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> because the latter is reduced and
discrete. Hence the structure map <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> embeds <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an invertible sheaf
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> since <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically
closed. Moreover, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}|X_{s}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}|X_{s}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> since the embedding
is supported at <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, by Serre duality
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Cor. 7.7, p. 244, and Cor. 7.12, p. 246]</cite>, it suffices to
show <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}(-M))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}(-M))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the sequence</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}(-M)\to\mathcal{\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}}_{\!X_{s}}\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}|M\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}(-M)\to\mathcal{\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}}_{\!X_{s}}\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{\!X_{s}}|M\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Now, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{X_{s}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{X_{s}}(d-4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{X_{s}}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{X_{s}}(d-4)</annotation></semantics></math> since
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{X_{s}}\simeq\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{P}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{X_{s}}(X_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{X_{s}}\simeq\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{P}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{X_{s}}(X_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. 8.20, p. 182]</cite> and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{P}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{P}(-4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}_{P}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{P}(-4)</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Eg. 8.20.1, p. 182]</cite>. But <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(d-4))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(d-4))=0</annotation></semantics></math>
because <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> is a hypersurface, and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(d-4))\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{M}(d-4))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(d-4))\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{M}(d-4))</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective because <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{P}(d-4))\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{M}(d-4))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{P}(d-4))\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{M}(d-4))</annotation></semantics></math> is. Thus <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm22.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{M})\otimes k\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{M})\otimes k\to\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M))</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective
by the implication (v)<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>(iv) of Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. So
the section of <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> defining <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a section of
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The extension defines a relative effective divisor on <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which restricts to <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, owing to the implication (iii)<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>(i)
of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. It follows that the embedding <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\hookrightarrow H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\hookrightarrow H</annotation></semantics></math> factors
through <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, so through <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> meet transversally at
<math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>; whence, <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> cannot embed into <math id="S5.Thmthm22.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\cap S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\cap S</annotation></semantics></math>. Here is the desired
contradiction. The discussion is now complete.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 5.23</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat, its geometric fibers are integral
curves of arithmetic genus <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Show the
“generalized Jacobians” <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> form a smooth
quasi-projective family of relative dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. And show this
family is projective if and only if <math id="S5.Thmthm23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.24</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme of relative dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>; that is,
<math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth and proper <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-group scheme with geometrically connected
fibers of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> needn’t be projective Zariski locally
over <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, according to Raynaud <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ra66</a>, Rem. 8.c, p. 1315]</cite>,
Grothendieck found two such examples: one where <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced and
1-dimensional, and another where <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of the ring of dual
numbers of a field of characteristic 0. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ra70</a>, Ch. XII]</cite>,
Raynaud gave detailed constructions of similar examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, and <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
exists, and is also a projective Abelian <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme of relative
dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, represents <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is
locally of finite type by the main theorem, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. For
<math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth and proper of dimension <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm70</a>, § 13]</cite>. Hence <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> exists by
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a>, and is projective by Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>.
Finally, <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth owing to a more sophisticated version of the
proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 5.19. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.19</span></a>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm65</a>, pp. 117–118]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm24.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a universal sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
since <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, the identity section. Normalize
<math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> by tensoring it with <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X^{*}}^{*}g_{X^{*}}^{*}\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X^{*}}^{*}g_{X^{*}}^{*}\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then its
restriction to <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times X^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times X^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map, which is a “duality”
isomorphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1j" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1j.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}X^{**}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}X^{**}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Indeed, forming <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with changing <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>’s
geometric fibers are isomorphisms by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm70</a>, Cor., p. 132]</cite>. But <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{**}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{**}</annotation></semantics></math> are proper over <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is flat. Therefore, <math id="S5.Thmthm24.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>
is an isomorphism by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 4.6.7]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.25</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is normal, integral, and projective. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible
and projective by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.
Set <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k})_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k})_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{P/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{P/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is
plainly an Abelian variety; whence, <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian variety too by
Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm24" title="Remark 5.24. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.24</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm25.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a point <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be an Abelian variety, and set
<math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{B/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{B/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian variety too, and there is
a canonical isomorphism <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}B^{**}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}B^{**}</annotation></semantics></math> by Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm24" title="Remark 5.24. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.24</span></a>.
Let <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\colon X\to X\times B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\colon X\to X\times B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map defined by <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in B^{*}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in B^{*}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
let <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\colon B^{*}\to X\times B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\colon B^{*}\to X\times B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map defined by <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm25.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a map <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\colon B^{*}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\colon B^{*}\to P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Comparison
Theorem, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an invertible sheaf
<math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{*}\mathcal{L}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Normalize
<math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by tensoring it with <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta^{*}\mathcal{L})_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta^{*}\mathcal{L})_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a
map <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon X\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon X\to B</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm25.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Reversing the preceding argument, we see that every such <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> arises from
a unique map <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\colon B^{*}\to\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\colon B^{*}\to\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is
integral, <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the maps <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\colon B^{*}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\colon B^{*}\to P</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon X\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon X\to B</annotation></semantics></math> are in bijective correspondence. Plainly, this
correspondence is compatible with maps <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}\colon B\to B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}\colon B\to B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a natural map <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon X\to A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon X\to A</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and every map <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\colon X\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\colon X\to B</annotation></semantics></math> factors uniquely
through <math id="S5.Thmthm25.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.26</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and smooth, its geometric fibers are
connected curves of genus <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Set
<math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; it exists and is a projective Abelian <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme by
Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm23" title="Exercise 5.23. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.23</span></a>. Set <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{J/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{*}:=\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{J/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; it exists, is a
projective Abelian scheme, and is “dual” to <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> by
Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm24" title="Remark 5.24. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.24</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm26.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has an invertible sheaf <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fibers <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are
of degree 1. Define an associated “Abel” map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1k" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1k.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathcal{L}}\colon X\to J" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathcal{L}}\colon X\to J</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.2" class="ltx_p">directly on <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points as follows. Given <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, its graph
subscheme <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{t}\subset X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{t}\subset X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative effective divisor; see
Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm13" title="Exercise 4.13. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>. Use <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(-\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(-\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> to define <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathcal{L}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathcal{L}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm26.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> induces, via pullback, an “auto-duality” isomorphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}\colon J^{*}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}J." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}\colon J^{*}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}J.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This isomorphism is independent of the choice of <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>; in fact, it
exists even if no <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> does. These facts are proved in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGK</a>, Thm. 2.1, p. 595]</cite>. In fact, a more general autoduality
result is proved: it applies to the natural compactification of <math id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>,
which parameterizes torsion-free sheaves, when the geometric fibers of
<math id="S5.Thmthm26.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are not necessarily smooth, but integral with double points at
worst. And the proof starts from scratch, recovering the original
case of a single smooth curve over a field.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm27.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.27</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm27.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and flat. Assume its geometric fibers are
curves, but not necessarily integral. Then there are two remarkable
theorems asserting the existence of <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> as an algebraic space
and of <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> as a separated <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. These theorems are
important in the theory of Néron models; so in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>, Sect. 9.4]</cite>,
their proofs are sketched, and the original papers, cited.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm27.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm27.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One theorem is due to Raynaud. He assumes, in addition, that <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the
spectrum of a discrete valuation ring, that <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, and that <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds. Furthermore, given any geometric
fiber of <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>, he measures the lengths of the local rings at the
generic points of its irreducible components, and he assumes their
greatest common divisor is 1. Then he proves the above existence
assertions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm27.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm27.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The other theorem is due to Deligne. Instead, he assumes, in addition,
<math id="S5.Thmthm27.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-stable; that is, its geometric fibers are reduced and
connected, and have, at worst, ordinary double points. Then he proves,
in addition, <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and quasi-projective; in fact, it
carries a canonical <math id="S5.Thmthm27.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-ample invertible sheaf.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>The torsion component of the identity</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section establishes the two main finiteness theorem for
<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and its geometric fibers are
integral. The first theorem asserts the finiteness of the torsion
component <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, an open and closed group subscheme. By
definition, it consists of the points with a multiple in the connected
component <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which was studied in the previous section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second theorem asserts the finiteness of a larger sort of subset
<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its points represent the invertible sheaves with a
given Hilbert polynomial <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. The section starts by developing
numerical characterizations of <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, or rather of the
corresponding invertible sheaves, when <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an
algebraically closed field. This development assumes some familiarity
with basic intersection theory, which is developed in Appendix B.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be
invertible sheaves on <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivalent to</span> <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for some nonzero <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> depending on <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>th power <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically
equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">numerically equivalent to</span> <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for every complete curve <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding
intersection numbers are equal:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y]=\int c_{1}\mathcal{N}\cdot[Y]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y]=\int c_{1}\mathcal{N}\cdot[Y].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It is sufficient, by additivity, to take <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to be complete and
integral. It is then equivalent that <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y}=\deg\mathcal{N}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y}=\deg\mathcal{N}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> or that
<math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y^{\prime}}=\deg\mathcal{N}_{Y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y^{\prime}}=\deg\mathcal{N}_{Y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalization of <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
because, in any event,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y]=\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y}=\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y]=\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y}=\deg\mathcal{L}_{Y^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of
invertible sheaves on <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bounded</span>
if there exist an <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type and an invertible sheaf
<math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, given <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a
geometric point <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is projective. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf on <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
following conditions are equivalent:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(a)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The sheaf <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(b)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The sheaf <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(c)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The family <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\mid p\in\mathbb{Z}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\mid p\in\mathbb{Z}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(d)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For every coherent sheaf <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L})=\chi(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L})=\chi(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">(e)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For every closed integral curve <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">(f)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For every integer <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the sheaf <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is ample.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S6.Thmthm3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is irreducible, then all the conditions above are
equivalent to the following one:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">(g)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For every pair of integers <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,\,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,\,n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is irreducible of dimension </span><math id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then all the
conditions above are equivalent to the following one:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">(h)<span id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Setting <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell h^{r-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell h^{r-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us proceed by establishing the following implications:</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex1a"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(a)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(e)}\Longleftrightarrow\textup{(b)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(f)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>(c)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(a)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(d)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(e)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mtext>(b)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(c)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(f)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(b)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(a)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(e)}\Longleftrightarrow\textup{(b)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(f)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex2a"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(g)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(h)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)};\text{ and }\textup{(g)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)}\text{ if }\dim X=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(d)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(g)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(h)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(b)</mtext></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mtext>(g)</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>(b)</mtext><mtext> if </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(g)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(h)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)};\text{ and }\textup{(g)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)}\text{ if }\dim X=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (c). Then, by definition, there exist an <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of
finite type and an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, given <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a geometric point <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\otimes p}=\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\otimes p}=\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Apply the Pigeonhole Principle: say <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the same connected component of
<math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\neq p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\neq p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=p_{1}-p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=p_{1}-p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraically equivalent to <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (a) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, for each <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>
is ample by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA II</a>, 4.5.8]</cite>. So <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> has a neighborhood <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{u}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{u}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is ample for every <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 4.7.1]</cite>.
Since <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact, <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is covered by finitely many of the <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the product of the corresponding <math id="S6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is
ample for every <math id="S6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is ample
for every <math id="S6.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S6.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes q}(1)\bigr)^{\otimes N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes q}(1)\bigr)^{\otimes N}</annotation></semantics></math> is
ample for every <math id="S6.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (f) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (a). The function <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
polynomial. To prove it is constant, we may replace <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> for any nonzero <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we may assume <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraically equivalent to <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. So let <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected
<math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> an invertible sheaf on <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for a fixed
<math id="S6.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S6.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> varies, the function <math id="S6.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> is constant by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.5]</cite>. It follows that (d)
holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (d). Taking <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get (e). Taking <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes(p+1)}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes(p+1)}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus whether or not <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, (g) holds,.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (g). Then <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, say of dimension <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\sum_{0\leq i,\,j\leq r}a_{ij}\binom{p+i}{i}\binom{n+j}{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\sum_{0\leq i,\,j\leq r}a_{ij}\binom{p+i}{i}\binom{n+j}{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}=\int\ell^{i}h^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}=\int\ell^{i}h^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j=r</annotation></semantics></math>. Then (g) implies <math id="S6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}=0</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then (h) follows. Suppose <math id="S6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="S6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> is the only closed integral curve
contained in <math id="S6.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of
<math id="S6.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell\cdot[X_{\red}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell\cdot[X_{\red}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (b) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conditions (e) and (b) are equivalent since <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y})</annotation></semantics></math> by Riemann’s Theorem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (f). Then for every closed integral curve <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y}^{\otimes p}(1))=p\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})+\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(1))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y}^{\otimes p}(1))=p\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})+\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(1))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">for every integer <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L}_{Y})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (b) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume (h). Then <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible of dimension <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. To prove
(b), plainly we may replace <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by its reduction. We proceed by
induction on <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then (b) holds by the Hodge Index Theorem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a complete integral curve <math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, take <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
so that the twisted ideal <math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{Y,X}(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{Y,X}(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by its global
sections. View these sections as sections of <math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then they
define a linear system that is free of base points on <math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>. So the
global sections of <math id="S6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{Y}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{Y}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> define a linear system that is very
ample on <math id="S6.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this system maps <math id="S6.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X-Y</annotation></semantics></math> onto a variety of
dimension at least 2; in other words, the system is not “composite with
a pencil.”</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence the generic member <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically irreducible by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za58</a>, Thm. I.6.3, p. 30]</cite>. In the first instance, we must apply
the cited theorem to the induced system on the normalization of <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
we conclude that the preimage of <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically irreducible.
But then <math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is too. Therefore, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 9.7.7]</cite>, a general
member <math id="S6.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!H}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!H}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by the
Projection Formula,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int\ell_{1}h_{1}^{r-2}=n\int\ell h^{r-1}=0\text{ and }\int\ell_{1}^{2}h_{1}^{r-3}=n\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int\ell_{1}h_{1}^{r-2}=n\int\ell h^{r-1}=0\text{ and }\int\ell_{1}^{2}h_{1}^{r-3}=n\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">So by induction, <math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S6.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> arises from a section of <math id="S6.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{Y}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{Y}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by the Projection Formula,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int\ell\cdot[Y]=\int\ell_{1}\cdot[Y]=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int\ell\cdot[Y]=\int\ell_{1}\cdot[Y]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p14.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus (b) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, assume (b). By Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> below, there is an <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such
that, if <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is an invertible sheaf on <math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to
<math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular. So <math id="S6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
by its global sections, and its higher cohomology groups vanish.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\phi(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\phi(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}(m))=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}(m))=M</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S6.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{N}(n))=\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{N}(n))=\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math> also by
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a> below. Set <math id="S6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-m)^{\oplus M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-m)^{\oplus M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S6.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient of <math id="S6.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=\IQuot^{\phi}_{\mathcal{F}/X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IQuot</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=\IQuot^{\phi}_{\mathcal{F}/X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type. Let <math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal quotient. Then there exists a <math id="S6.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S6.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S6.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> be the open set on
which <math id="S6.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Let <math id="S6.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of the
complement of <math id="S6.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. Replace <math id="S6.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-R</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by its restriction. Then <math id="S6.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> still. Thus the invertible
sheaves on <math id="S6.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> form a bounded
family. In particular, (c) holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the preceding paragraph, the last one in the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>. Using <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of
<math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IQuot^{\phi}_{\mathcal{F}/X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IQuot</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IQuot^{\phi}_{\mathcal{F}/X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, give another proof that the invertible
sheaves <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> form a
bounded family.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is projective of dimension <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a coherent sheaf on <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a number <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, if <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is any invertible
sheaf on <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n))\leq B_{\mathcal{F}}\tbinom{n+r}{r}\text{ for all }n\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n))\leq B_{\mathcal{F}}\tbinom{n+r}{r}\text{ for all }n\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S6.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n)=\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n)=\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. So we may take <math id="S6.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a short exact sequence <math id="S6.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime}\to\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime}\to\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n))\leq\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\prime}(n))+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}(n))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}(n))\leq\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\prime}(n))+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}(n)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">So given <math id="S6.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can take <math id="S6.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}}+B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime}}+B_{\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\Proj(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Proj</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\Proj(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\widetilde{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\widetilde{M}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a finitely generated
graded <math id="S6.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module. Then there is a filtration by graded submodules</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=:M_{q}\supset M_{q-1}\supset\cdots\supset M_{1}\supset M_{0}:=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊃</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊃</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=:M_{q}\supset M_{q-1}\supset\cdots\supset M_{1}\supset M_{0}:=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text">such that <math id="S6.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i+1}/M_{i}\simeq(A/P_{i})[p_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i+1}/M_{i}\simeq(A/P_{i})[p_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homogeneous
prime for each <math id="S6.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that we may assume <math id="S6.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is integral and
<math id="S6.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero section.
It defines a divisor <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly 0. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle 0\leq\int h^{r-1}[D]=\int h^{r-1}\ell+p\int h^{r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle 0\leq\int h^{r-1}[D]=\int h^{r-1}\ell+p\int h^{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">But <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int h^{r-1}\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int h^{r-1}\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a curve
since <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="S6.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int h^{r}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int h^{r}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S6.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S6.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperplane section of <math id="S6.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{L}(n-1)\to\mathcal{L}(n)\to\mathcal{L}_{H}(n)\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{L}(n-1)\to\mathcal{L}(n)\to\mathcal{L}_{H}(n)\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">By induction on <math id="S6.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume there is a number <math id="S6.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))\leq B\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}\text{ for all }n\geq 0;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))\leq B\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}\text{ for all }n\geq 0;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p22.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.3.1" class="ltx_text">moreover, <math id="S6.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> works for every <math id="S6.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))-\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n-1))\leq B\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}\text{ for all }n\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))-\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n-1))\leq B\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}\text{ for all }n\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p22.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p22.4.1" class="ltx_text">But <math id="S6.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n+r-1}{r}+\binom{n+r-1}{r-1}=\binom{n+r}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n+r-1}{r}+\binom{n+r-1}{r-1}=\binom{n+r}{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, induction on <math id="S6.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq B\tbinom{n+r}{r}\text{ for all }n\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq B\tbinom{n+r}{r}\text{ for all }n\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall <math id="S6.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\subset\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>;
so we may take <math id="S6.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B</annotation></semantics></math>. But if <math id="S6.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{p+n+r}{r}\leq\binom{p+r}{r}\binom{n+r}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{p+n+r}{r}\leq\binom{p+r}{r}\binom{n+r}{r}</annotation></semantics></math> since every monomial of degree <math id="S6.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+n</annotation></semantics></math> is
the product of one of degree <math id="S6.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and one of degree <math id="S6.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>; so we may
take <math id="S6.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B\binom{p+r}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}:=B\binom{p+r}{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is projective. Then there is an integer <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that, if <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is any
invertible sheaf on <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S6.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular, and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))\text{ for all }n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))\text{ for all }n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S6.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:=\dim(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:=\dim(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and proceed by induction on <math id="S6.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then both
assertions are trivial. So assume <math id="S6.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, let us establish the asserted equation. Given an <math id="S6.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, fix
<math id="S6.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample. Take effective divisors <math id="S6.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S6.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(F)=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(q)\text{ and }\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(G)=\mathcal{L}(q)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(F)=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(q)\text{ and }\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(G)=\mathcal{L}(q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p25.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p25.2.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S6.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, plainly <math id="S6.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are
numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Form <math id="S6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-F)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!F}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-F)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!F}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-G)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!G}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-G)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!G}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Tensor them with <math id="S6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)</annotation></semantics></math>. We
get</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(n)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}(n+q)\to 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(n)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}(n+q)\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.6.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex3b"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex3b.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(n)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{G}(n+q)\to 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(n)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q)\to\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{G}(n+q)\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">Apply <math id="S6.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\bullet)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\bullet)</annotation></semantics></math> and subtract. We get</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))-\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p-1})(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}_{G}^{\otimes p}(n+q))-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{F}^{\otimes p}(n+q))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))-\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p-1})(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}_{G}^{\otimes p}(n+q))-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{F}^{\otimes p}(n+q)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p26.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.3.1" class="ltx_text">By induction, the right hand side varies as a polynomial in <math id="S6.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is independent of <math id="S6.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence there are polynomials <math id="S6.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\phi_{1}(n)p+\phi_{0}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=\phi_{1}(n)p+\phi_{0}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.6.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p26.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p26.4.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S6.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="S6.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(n)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(n)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">By induction, there is an integer <math id="S6.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S6.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-regular for every <math id="S6.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S6.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}(n))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}_{F}(n))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S6.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S6.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{2}-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{2}-i</annotation></semantics></math>, and for every <math id="S6.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, owing to
Sequence (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6.1</span></a>), there is an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q))\text{ for $i\geq 2$, for $n\geq n_{2}$, and for every $p$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>, for </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mtext>, and for every </mtext><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+q))\text{ for $i\geq 2$, for $n\geq n_{2}$, and for every $p$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p27.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p27.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, for each <math id="S6.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, each <math id="S6.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="S6.p27.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="S6.p27.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+jq))=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n+jq))=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p27.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p27.3.1" class="ltx_text">by Serre’s Theorem. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex7a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=0\text{ for $i\geq 2$, for $n\geq n_{2}$ and for every $p$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>, for </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mtext> and for every </mtext><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))=0\text{ for $i\geq 2$, for $n\geq n_{2}$ and for every $p$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="S6.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\geq\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\geq\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
and every <math id="S6.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Take <math id="S6.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\max(n_{2},n_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\max(n_{2},n_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Owing to
Equation (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6.2</span></a>), then <math id="S6.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
as <math id="S6.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(n)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(n)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> or as <math id="S6.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\to-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\to-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(n)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(n)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, by Lemma (<a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 6.5. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>), there is a number <math id="S6.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}(n))\leq B</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S6.p28.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. This contradiction means
<math id="S6.p28.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence Equation (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6.2</span></a>) yields
<math id="S6.p28.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p28.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, in order to prove there is an <math id="S6.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that every <math id="S6.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular, we must modify Mumford’s
original work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, Lect. 14]</cite> because these <math id="S6.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> are not ideals.
However, as Mumford himself points out <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, pp. 102–103]</cite>, the
hypothesis that his sheaf <math id="S6.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is an ideal enters only through the
bound <math id="S6.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{I}(n))\leq\binom{n+r}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{I}(n))\leq\binom{n+r}{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, this bound can
be replaced by the bound <math id="S6.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 6.5. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>. Of course, in addition, we must use the fact we
just proved, that all the <math id="S6.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same Hilbert polynomial.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective and integral of dimension <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=c_{1}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf on <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Say</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}a_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}\text{ and }a:=\int\ell h^{r-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}a_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}\text{ and }a:=\int\ell h^{r-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;a_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;a_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq a_{r}\binom{n+r}{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq a_{r}\binom{n+r}{r}</annotation></semantics></math> for
all <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, modifying Mumford’s work
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, pp. 102–103]</cite> slightly, show there is a polynomial <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
depending only on <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular with
<math id="S6.Thmthm7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\Phi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\Phi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general, show there is a polynomial <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular with <math id="S6.Thmthm7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\Psi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r};a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\Psi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r};a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/S</annotation></semantics></math> be a group scheme. For <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}\colon G\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}\colon G\to G</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
<math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th power map. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set defined by the formula</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}:=\textstyle\bigcup_{n&gt;0}\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}:=\textstyle\bigcup_{n&gt;0}\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of the connected components of the
identity <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{s}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{s}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a commutative group scheme locally of
finite type. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open group subscheme, and forming it
commutes with extending <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S6.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact,
then it is closed and of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, <math id="S6.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open and closed group subscheme of
finite type, and forming it commutes with extending <math id="S6.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, the <math id="S6.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th
power map <math id="S6.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous, and forming it commutes with extending
<math id="S6.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; also, <math id="S6.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homomorphism since <math id="S6.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative. So
<math id="S6.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the filtered union of the open and closed group subschemes
<math id="S6.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and forming them commutes with extending <math id="S6.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S6.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open group subscheme, and forming it commutes with
extending <math id="S6.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S6.p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact, then
<math id="S6.p30.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of finitely many of the <math id="S6.p30.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}^{-1}G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so
<math id="S6.p30.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed; also, then <math id="S6.p30.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type since <math id="S6.p30.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is
locally of finite type.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, and <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a commutative group scheme locally of
finite type. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group subscheme of finite type.
Show <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Thus, if <math id="S6.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type, then it
is the largest group subscheme of finite type.)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.11</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf
on <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding point. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S6.Thmthm11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.12</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists
and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open
group subscheme, and forming it commutes with extending <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover,
if <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, then <math id="S6.Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed and of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 4.17. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a>, <math id="S6.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally of finite type.
So owing to Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 6.9. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.9</span></a>, we need only prove <math id="S6.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact when <math id="S6.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective. Since forming <math id="S6.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
commutes with extending <math id="S6.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we may also assume <math id="S6.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically
closed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">At the very end of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, we proved that
the invertible sheaves <math id="S6.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> form
a bounded family. In other words, there is a <math id="S6.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S6.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of finite
type and an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S6.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
appear among the fibers <math id="S6.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S6.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map <math id="S6.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\colon T\to\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\colon T\to\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Owing to
Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> and to Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 6.11. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>, we have <math id="S6.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)\supset\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)\supset\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, so is <math id="S6.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)</annotation></semantics></math>;
whence, so is any subspace of <math id="S6.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S6.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact, as needed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.13</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective
and geometrically integral. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective.
If also <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically normal, show <math id="S6.Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.14</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>, if <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, then
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> does exist and represent <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> according to
Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm3" title="Corollary 4.18.3. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.3</span></a>. In fact, this corollary asserts
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
complete; <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> need not be projective. Furthermore, although we used
projective methods to prove Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>, we can infer it
whenever <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is complete, as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is complete. By Chow’s lemma, there is a projective variety
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a surjective map <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5" title="Theorem 4.18.5. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.5</span></a>,
the induced map</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\colon\IPic_{X/k}\to\IPic_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\colon\IPic_{X/k}\to\IPic_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is of finite type. Set <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=(\gamma^{*})^{-1}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=(\gamma^{*})^{-1}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is of finite type since <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is by
Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>. Now, plainly <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}\subset\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. So since <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homomorphism,
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}\subset\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}\subset\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math>; whence, <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}\subset H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}\subset H</annotation></semantics></math> (in fact, the two are equal by Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 6.10. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>). Since
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is open, it is therefore a subscheme of finite type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, in Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, Conditions (a)–(e) continue to
make sense and to remain equivalent whenever <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is complete. Indeed,
our proofs of the implications</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(a)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(e)}\Longleftrightarrow\textup{(b)}\text{ and }\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(f)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(c)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(a)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(d)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(e)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>(b)</mtext><mtext> and </mtext><mtext>(c)</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(f)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(b)</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(a)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(d)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(e)}\Longleftrightarrow\textup{(b)}\text{ and }\textup{(c)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(f)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(b)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p3.2" class="ltx_p">work without change. However, we used projective methods to prove
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{(b)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(b)</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>(c)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{(b)}\Longrightarrow\textup{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, we can infer this implication
whenever <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is complete, as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be as above. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, Let <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed curve. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}\cdot[Y^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[\gamma Y^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}\mathcal{L}\cdot[\gamma Y^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> by the Projection Formula; hence, the former number
vanishes as the latter does.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represent <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\lambda\in\IPic_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\lambda\in\IPic_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now,
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{*}\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{*}\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> and
to Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 6.11. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>. So <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in H:=(\gamma^{*})^{-1}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in H:=(\gamma^{*})^{-1}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X^{\prime}/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The inclusion <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by an invertible sheaf
<math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some fppf covering <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to H</annotation></semantics></math>. (Although <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is
algebraically closed, possibly <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> does
not hold universally, so we cannot simply take <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=H</annotation></semantics></math>.) Replace <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by
an open subscheme so that <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type and surjective.
Since <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type, so is <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm14.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point that maps to <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in H</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, for every <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, plainly <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}</annotation></semantics></math>
is numerically equivalent to <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. So similarly <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{t_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\simeq\mathcal{M}_{t_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="S6.Thmthm14.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{p}\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{p}\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (c) holds.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.15</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary subset of <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> the
corresponding family of classes of invertible sheaves on the fibers of
<math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense of Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Exercise 4.5. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact (with the induced topology) if and only if <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bounded</span> in the following sense: there exist an <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of finite
type and an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every class in
<math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a fiber <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.Thmthm15.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.16</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski locally over <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and flat
with integral geometric fibers. Then <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open and
closed group subscheme of finite type, and forming it commutes with
changing <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If also <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, and <math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, then
<math id="S6.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">The second assertion follows from the first and
Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>. The first assertion is local on <math id="S6.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; so, to
prove it, we may assume <math id="S6.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> asserts <math id="S6.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is locally of
finite type. So the <math id="S6.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are subgroups of the
<math id="S6.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and forming <math id="S6.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with
extending <math id="S6.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm9" title="Lemma 6.9. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.9</span></a>.
However, plainly <math id="S6.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}=\bigcup_{s\in S}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}=\bigcup_{s\in S}\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> as
sets. Hence, forming <math id="S6.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with changing <math id="S6.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>;
moreover, in order to infer <math id="S6.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a group subscheme, we need
only prove it is open.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, a subset <math id="S6.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of a topological space <math id="S6.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is open or closed if
(and only if), for every member <math id="S6.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of an open covering of <math id="S6.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, the
intersection <math id="S6.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\cap B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\cap B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is so in <math id="S6.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, in order to infer
<math id="S6.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open and closed group subscheme, we need only prove
that, for any affine open subscheme <math id="S6.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the
intersection <math id="S6.p36.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\cap\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\cap\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is open and closed in <math id="S6.p36.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> also asserts <math id="S6.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents
<math id="S6.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the inclusion <math id="S6.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some étale covering <math id="S6.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Replace <math id="S6.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by an open subscheme so that <math id="S6.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to U</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type and
surjective. Let <math id="S6.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of <math id="S6.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S6.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalent to <math id="S6.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the preimage of
<math id="S6.p37.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p37.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> owing to Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 6.11. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>. So we have to
prove <math id="S6.p37.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is open and closed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is affine, it is quasi-compact, so of finite type. Hence <math id="S6.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
is of finite type. So it has only finitely many connected components.
But, for every pair <math id="S6.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,\,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,\,n</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="S6.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}^{\otimes p}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}^{\otimes p}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on each connected component of <math id="S6.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,
Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> implies <math id="S6.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is open and closed, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove <math id="S6.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type. Let <math id="S6.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be the
corresponding family of classes of invertible sheaves on the fibers of
<math id="S6.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>. By Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 6.15. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.15</span></a>, we need only prove <math id="S6.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is
bounded. At the very end of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, we
proved essentially this statement when <math id="S6.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of an
algebraically closed field, and <math id="S6.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, but not necessarily
integral. We can argue similarly here, but must make two important
modifications.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, if a class in <math id="S6.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
on a fiber <math id="S6.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a field containing the field <math id="S6.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> of a
point <math id="S6.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p40.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
owing to Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>. But <math id="S6.p40.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> can vary with
<math id="S6.p40.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, it must remain the same on each connected component
of <math id="S6.p40.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. And the matter in question is local on <math id="S6.p40.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. So we may and must
assume <math id="S6.p40.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text">Second, at the end of the proof of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>, when we modified
Mumford’s work, we used the bound <math id="S6.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 6.5. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>; in fact, in the induction step, we implicitly used
the corresponding bounds for various subschemes of <math id="S6.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Unfortunately,
it is not clear, in general, how these bounds vary with <math id="S6.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. But
in place of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>, we can use Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 6.7. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>, which
provides a uniform <math id="S6.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular for every <math id="S6.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> representing a class in <math id="S6.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.17</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski
locally over <math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and is flat with geometrically integral fibers. For
each <math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the algebraic closure of the residue field
<math id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the torsion group</span></p>
<table id="S6.E1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})\bigm/\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})\bigm/\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.17.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm17.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is finite, and its order is bounded.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S6.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, the order of their quotient is equal to the
number of connected components of <math id="S6.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This number
is finite because <math id="S6.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type by
Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, <math id="S6.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="S6.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> again by Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>, so equal to <math id="S6.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> essentially by Definition <a href="#S6.Thmthm8" title="Definition 6.8. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>. But, since
<math id="S6.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type by Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a>, the number
of connected components of <math id="S6.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}\otimes k_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant for <math id="S6.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in
a nonempty open subset of <math id="S6.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV3</a>, 9.7.9]</cite>. Hence the number
is bounded by Noetherian induction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.18</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and smooth, its geometric fibers are
irreducible, and <math id="S6.Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian. Show that <math id="S6.Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.19</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. If <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and it represents
<math id="S6.Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open group subscheme of
finite type. This fact can be derived from Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a>
through a series of reduction steps; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, Thm. 4.7, p. 647]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian in addition. Then, whether or not
<math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, the torsion group (<a href="#S6.E1a" title="In Corollary 6.17. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.17.1</span></a>) is finite
and its order is bounded. This fact follows from the preceding one via
the proof of Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm17" title="Corollary 6.17. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.17</span></a>, since there is
an nonempty open subscheme <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\red}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext class="undefined">\red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\red}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{V}/V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{V}/V}</annotation></semantics></math>
exists and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{V}/V)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math> by Grothendieck’s
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm2" title="Theorem 4.18.2. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm19.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm19.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the rank of the corresponding “Néron–Severi” group</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1g.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})\bigm/\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})\bigm/\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X_{k_{s}^{\prime}}/k_{s}^{\prime}}(k_{s}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm19.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is finite and bounded. This fact is far deeper; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, Thm. 5.1,
p. 650, and Rem. 5.3, p. 652]</cite>, and see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Za35</a>, pp. 121–124]</cite>.
Moreover, the rank is arithmetic in nature: its value need not be a
constructible function of <math id="S6.Thmthm19.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>; a standard example is discussed in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR</a>, p. 235]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.20</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat with integral geometric fibers.
Given a polynomial <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathbb{Q}[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathbb{Q}[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}\subset\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}\subset\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of points representing invertible sheaves <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
<math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are open and closed subschemes of finite type; they are
disjoint and cover; and forming them commutes with changing <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If
also <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, then <math id="S6.Thmthm20.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, the <math id="S6.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint and cover, and forming them
commutes with changing <math id="S6.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The rest of the proof is similar to the
proof of Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a>. In fact, the present case is simpler
because the sheaves in question have the same Hilbert polynomials by
hypothesis; there is no need to appeal to Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> nor to
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.21</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is locally projective over <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and flat, and its geometric
fibers are integral curves. Given an integer <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}\subset\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}\subset\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of points representing
invertible sheaves <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm21.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Show the <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are open and closed subschemes of finite type;
show they are disjoint and cover; and show that forming them commutes
with changing <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm21.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Show there is no abuse of notation: the fiber of <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> over
<math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is the connected component of <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\IPic_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Show
there is no torsion: <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Show each
<math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is an fppf-torsor under <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, the
latter acts naturally on the former, and the two become isomorphic after
base change by an fppf-covering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm21.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm21.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Show the <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are quasi-projective if <math id="S6.Thmthm21.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and
<math id="S6.Thmthm21.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.22</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is another important case where <math id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>,
namely, when <math id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an Abelian <math id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Indeed, the equation holds
if it does on each geometric fiber of <math id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>; so we may assume that <math id="S6.Thmthm22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>
is the spectrum of an algebraically closed field. In this case,
a modern proof was given by Mumford <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm70</a>, Cor.2, p. 178]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_eg">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 6.23</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> can fail if a geometric fiber of <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is
reducible. For example, let <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the spectrum of a field <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of two disjoint lines. For each pair <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> be the invertible sheaf that restricts to <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>
on the first line and to <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(b)</annotation></semantics></math> on the second.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By Riemann’s Theorem, <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}_{a,b}(n))=(a+b)+2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}_{a,b}(n))=(a+b)+2n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. And it is
easy to see that <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of copies of
<math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(k)</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>; compare with
Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 4.15. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a>. Moreover, <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the
point with index <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, each set <math id="S6.Thmthm23.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite,
and so not of finite type.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.24</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> can be modified as follows. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is
Noetherian. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat with integral geometric
fibers of dimension <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a subset <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
of finite type if, in the Hilbert polynomials <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{r}a_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{r}a_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding invertible sheaves, <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
remains bounded from above and below, and <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{r-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{r-2}</annotation></semantics></math> remains bounded from
below alone. Moreover, <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type if, instead, <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell h^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell h^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> remains bounded from above and below, and <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell^{2}h^{r-2}</annotation></semantics></math> remains bounded from below alone, where <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> are as
usual.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These facts can be derived from Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> by reducing to
the case where the fibers are normal and by showing, in this case using
simple elementary means, that the given bounds imply bounds on all the
<math id="S6.Thmthm24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, Thm. 3.13, p. 641]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm24.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm24.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The first fact is essentially equivalent, given
Theorems <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm18.Thmsbsthm5" title="Theorem 4.18.5. ‣ Remark 4.18. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.18.5</span></a>, to the following fact.
Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, and <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat with
fibers whose irreducible components have dimension at least 3. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
be a relative effective divisor whose associated sheaf is <math id="S6.Thmthm24.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the induced map of Picard functors is representable by maps of
finite type. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, Thm. 3.8, p. 636]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm24.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm24.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The latter fact was first proved directly using the “equivalence
criterion” mentioned in Remark <a href="#S5.Thmthm8" title="Remark 5.8. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> by Grothendieck
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. C-10]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.25</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat with integral geometric fibers.
Then the connected components of <math id="S6.Thmthm25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are open and closed
subschemes of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">By construction, <math id="S6.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Noetherian. Hence, its
connected components are open and closed; see the proof of
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Now, a connected component is always contained in
any open and closed set it meets. Hence the connected components of
<math id="S6.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> are of finite type owing to Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.26</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm25" title="Corollary 6.25. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.25</span></a>, <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> must be projective, not simply proper,
nor even projective Zariski locally over <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, Grothendieck
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, Rem. 3.3, p. 232-07]</cite> gave an example where <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> has
a connected component that is not of finite type: here <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve
with two components that meet in two points, such as the union of a
smooth conic and a line in the plane over an algebraically closed field,
and <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective over a neighborhood of each component of <math id="S6.Thmthm26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm27.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.27</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat with integral geometric fibers.
For <math id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the </span>n<span id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">th power map
<math id="S6.Thmthm27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}\colon\IPic_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}\colon\IPic_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is of finite type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text">Owing to Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm25" title="Corollary 6.25. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.25</span></a>, we need only prove that, given any
connected component <math id="S6.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the preimage <math id="S6.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is
of finite type too. Since <math id="S6.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="S6.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, the inclusion <math id="S6.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
an invertible sheaf <math id="S6.p46.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p46.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some étale covering <math id="S6.p46.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p47.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S6.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S6.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(p,q):=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}^{p}(q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(p,q):=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}^{p}(q))</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S6.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,\,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,\,q</annotation></semantics></math>, and
form the set <math id="S6.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of points <math id="S6.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p47.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}^{p}(q))=\psi(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}^{p}(q))=\psi(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.4]</cite>, the set <math id="S6.p47.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is open, and so
is its complement. Hence their images are open in <math id="S6.p47.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and plainly
these images are disjoint. But <math id="S6.p47.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Hence <math id="S6.p47.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\varphi_{n}^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\varphi_{n}^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math>. Represent <math id="S6.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by an invertible sheaf
<math id="S6.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S6.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(m,q):=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{m}(q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(m,q):=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{m}(q))</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="S6.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(m,q)=\sum a_{i}(m)\binom{q+i}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(m,q)=\sum a_{i}(m)\binom{q+i}{i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial. Now,
<math id="S6.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}(\lambda)\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}(\lambda)\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S6.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(mn,q)=\psi(m,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(mn,q)=\psi(m,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S6.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}(mn)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}(mn)</annotation></semantics></math> is
independent of the choice of <math id="S6.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S6.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is
too. Set <math id="S6.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n):=\theta(1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n):=\theta(1,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}^{-1}U\subset\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}^{-1}U\subset\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> implies <math id="S6.p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is
of finite type.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.28</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmthm27" title="Corollary 6.27. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.27</span></a> holds in greater generality. Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper, and assume <math id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/k)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for <math id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the nth power map
<math id="S6.Thmthm28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}\colon\IPic_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}\colon\IPic_{X/S}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is of finite type. This fact can be derived from the corollary through
a series of reduction steps similar to those used to generalize
Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, Thm. 3.6, p. 635]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm29.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exercise 6.29</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian, and <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and flat. Assume
<math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
be an arbitrary subset of <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding set
of Hilbert polynomials. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is finite if <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-compact. Show <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> has only one element if <math id="S6.Thmthm29.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Answers to all the exercises</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The exercises are not meant to be tricky, but are designed to help you
check, solidify, and expand your understanding of the ideas and methods.
So to promote your own mathematical health, try seriously to do each
exercise before you read its answer here. Note that the answer key is
the same number as the exercise key.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is local, <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial; so Definitions 
<a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Definition 2.1. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> yield the first isomorphism,
<math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{X/S}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{X/S}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an invertible sheaf on <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose the isomorphism
class of <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> maps to <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a
Zariski covering <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and an isomorphism <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}\colon\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}\colon\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of Zariski open subschemes
of <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. One of them contains the closed point, so is equal to <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Restricting <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an isomorphism <math id="A1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="A1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, represent <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> by an
invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a suitable Zariski covering
<math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a copy of <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as an open and closed
subscheme. Restricting <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represent <math id="A1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an isomorphism
<math id="A1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> between the two pullbacks of <math id="A1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Restricting
<math id="A1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\times_{T}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\times_{T}T</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="A1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, yields an isomorphism between <math id="A1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> too represents <math id="A1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="A1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\to\Pic_{(X/S)\zar}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin local with algebraically closed residue field. Then
every étale <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type is a direct product of
Artin local algebras, each isomorphic to <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, owing to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV4</a>, 17.6.2 and
17.6.3]</cite>. So if <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is any étale covering, then <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a disjoint union of open subschemes, each a copy of <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
reasoning as above, we conclude <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(A)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=k</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraically closed field. Then any fppf
covering <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> has a section; indeed, at any closed point of <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the local ring is essentially of finite type over <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and so the residue
field is equal to <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by the Hilbert Nullstellensatz. This point is not
necessarily isolated in <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; nevertheless, reasoning essentially as
above, we conclude <math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X}(k)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X}(k)\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 2.4. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The extension <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is étale. So if the two pullbacks of
<math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic, then
<math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> defines an element <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take an indeterminate <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, and identify <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[z]/(z^{2}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[z]/(z^{2}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by extension of scalars, <math id="A1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
becomes identified with <math id="A1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}[z]/(z-1)(z+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}[z]/(z-1)(z+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. So, by the Chinese
Remainder Theorem, <math id="A1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the product
<math id="A1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}\times\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}\times\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Correspondingly, <math id="A1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\otimes 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\otimes 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\otimes c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\otimes c</annotation></semantics></math> are identified
with <math id="A1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,c)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,\overline{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,\overline{c})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conjugate
of <math id="A1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, but this fact is not needed here.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="A1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic, over <math id="A1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, to the
disjoint union of two copies of <math id="A1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, for any field <math id="A1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, an
invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined, up to isomorphism,
by a single integer its Euler characteristic <math id="A1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Cor. 6.17, p. 145]</cite>. Hence, the two pullbacks of <math id="A1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic. Thus
<math id="A1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a <math id="A1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">(Similarly, the isomorphism class of <math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
independent of the choice of the isomorphism <math id="A1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon X_{\mathbb{C}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon X_{\mathbb{C}}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is independent too. But this fact too is
not needed here.)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we must show <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is not in the image of
<math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. By way of contradiction, suppose
<math id="A1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is. Then <math id="A1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> arises from an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. A priori, the pullback <math id="A1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be isomorphic to
<math id="A1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather, these two invertible sheaves need only become
isomorphic after they are pulled back to <math id="A1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is some étale
<math id="A1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">However, cohomology commutes with flat base change. So</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L})=\rank_{A}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}|X_{A})=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\textup{H}^{0}(\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1))=2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\rank</mtext><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L})=\rank_{A}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}|X_{A})=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}\textup{H}^{0}(\varphi^{*}\mathcal{O}(1))=2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, <math id="A1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero section. It defines an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{L}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly <math id="A1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!D})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!D})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, <math id="A1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="A1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="A1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-point. Thus <math id="A1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is not in the image of
<math id="A1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\zar</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\zar}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Exercise 2.6. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">First of all, we have <math id="A1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k)=\Pic_{(X_{k}/k)\fppf}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\fppf</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\fppf}(k)=\Pic_{(X_{k}/k)\fppf}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>
essentially by definition, because a map <math id="A1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes is an
fppf-covering if and only if it is an fppf-covering when viewed as a map
of <math id="A1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. And a similar analysis applies to the other three
functors. Now, <math id="A1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}\colon X_{k}\to k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}\colon X_{k}\to k</annotation></semantics></math> has a section; indeed, <math id="A1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is of
finite type and <math id="A1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed, and so any closed point of
<math id="A1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has residue field <math id="A1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by the Hilbert Nullstellensatz. Hence, by
Part 2 of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, the <math id="A1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points of all four functors are
the same. Finally, <math id="A1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}(k)=\Pic(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}(k)=\Pic(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="A1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is
trivial whenever <math id="A1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has only one closed point.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Whether or not <math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally, all
four functors have the same geometric points by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>;
in fact, given an algebraically closed field <math id="A1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points of
these functors are just the elements of <math id="A1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Exercise 3.2. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition, a section in <math id="A1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an
injection <math id="A1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image is an ideal <math id="A1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="A1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal of an
effective divisor <math id="A1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking inverses yields <math id="A1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we have a map
<math id="A1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\to|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\to|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the section is multiplied by a unit in <math id="A1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the
injection <math id="A1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by the same unit, so has
the same image <math id="A1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>; so then <math id="A1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is unaltered. Conversely, if <math id="A1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
arises from a second section, corresponding to a second isomorphism <math id="A1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, then these two isomorphism differ by an automorphism
of <math id="A1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is given by multiplication by a unit in
<math id="A1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>; so then the two sections differ by multiplication
by this unit. Thus <math id="A1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\hookrightarrow|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\hookrightarrow|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, given <math id="A1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>, by definition there exist an isomorphism
<math id="A1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\!X}(-D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\!X}(-D)</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal <math id="A1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>,
the inclusion <math id="A1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an injection <math id="A1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\hookrightarrow\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter corresponds to a section in
<math id="A1.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields <math id="A1.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> via the procedure of the first
paragraph. Thus <math id="A1.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}|\mathcal{L}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="undefined">\reg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{L})_{\reg}\big/\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}^{*})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}|\mathcal{L}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Exercise 3.5. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in D+E</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin D\cap E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin D\cap E</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> is a relative
effective divisor at <math id="A1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="A1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="A1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> on a
neighborhood of <math id="A1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. So suppose <math id="A1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in D\cap E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in D\cap E</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> says <math id="A1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-flat at <math id="A1.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and each of <math id="A1.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>
is cut out at <math id="A1.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by one element that is regular on the fiber <math id="A1.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
through <math id="A1.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the product of the two elements. Plainly, it cuts
out <math id="A1.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and it too is regular on <math id="A1.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> is a
relative effective divisor at <math id="A1.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> again.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Exercise 3.8. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a relative effective divisor <math id="A1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math>. Each fiber <math id="A1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
is of dimension <math id="A1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. So its Hilbert polynomial <math id="A1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{O}_{D_{t}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{O}_{D_{t}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>
is constant. Its value is <math id="A1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{D_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{D_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is just
the degree of <math id="A1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">The assertions are local on <math id="A1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>; so we may assume <math id="A1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.
Then <math id="A1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by an open subscheme
<math id="A1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}\subset\IHilb_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}\subset\IHilb_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>. And
<math id="A1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of open and closed subschemes of
finite type <math id="A1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IHilb_{X/S}^{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IHilb_{X/S}^{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> that parameterize the subschemes with
Hilbert polynomial <math id="A1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="A1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}:=\IDiv_{X/S}\bigcap\IHilb_{X/S}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⋂</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IHilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}:=\IDiv_{X/S}\bigcap\IHilb_{X/S}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
<math id="A1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> have all the desired properties.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general, whenever <math id="A1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is separated, <math id="A1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> represents
<math id="A1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Hilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the diagonal subscheme <math id="A1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset X\times X</annotation></semantics></math> is the
universal subscheme. Indeed, the projection <math id="A1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism, so <math id="A1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Hilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, given any <math id="A1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map
<math id="A1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:T\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, note <math id="A1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta=\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta=\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}\subset X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}\subset X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph subscheme of <math id="A1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, because the <math id="A1.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-points of both
<math id="A1.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are just the pairs <math id="A1.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(gp,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(gp,p)</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="A1.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{g}\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Hilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{g}\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, let <math id="A1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Hilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed
subscheme of <math id="A1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times T</annotation></semantics></math>. The projection <math id="A1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\Gamma\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\Gamma\to T</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, and
its fibers are finite; hence, it is finite by Chevalley’s
Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 4.4.2]</cite>. So <math id="A1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\Spec(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\Spec(\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="A1.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free, being flat and finitely
generated over <math id="A1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!T}</annotation></semantics></math>. And forming <math id="A1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with
passing to the fibers, so its rank is 1. Hence <math id="A1.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\pi_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Hence <math id="A1.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the
graph of a map <math id="A1.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon T\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon T\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. So, <math id="A1.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta=\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times g)^{-1}\Delta=\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> by the
above; also, <math id="A1.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the only map with this property, since a map is
determined by its graph. Thus <math id="A1.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Hilb</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hilb_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="A1.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\subset X\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\subset X\times X</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal subscheme.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">In the case at hand, <math id="A1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore representable by an
open subscheme <math id="A1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>. In fact, its
proof shows <math id="A1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is formed by the points <math id="A1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> where the fiber
<math id="A1.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor on <math id="A1.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="A1.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="A1.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>-rational
point for any <math id="A1.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>; so <math id="A1.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor if and only if <math id="A1.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
is regular at <math id="A1.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, <math id="A1.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular at
<math id="A1.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A1.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, set <math id="A1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:=X_{0}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:=X_{0}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{i}\subset X\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{i}\subset X\times T</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph
subscheme of the <math id="A1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th projection. By the above analysis, <math id="A1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{i}\in\Div_{X/S}^{1}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{i}\in\Div_{X/S}^{1}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma:=\sum\Gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma:=\sum\Gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in\Div_{X/S}^{m}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\Div_{X/S}^{m}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> owing to Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Exercise 3.5. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and to the additivity of
degree. Plainly <math id="A1.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represents the desired <math id="A1.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point of
<math id="A1.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be the algebraically closure of
<math id="A1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="A1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\textup{H}^{0}(X_{K},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{K}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\textup{H}^{0}(X_{K},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{K}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, <math id="A1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is
finite dimensional as a <math id="A1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space; so <math id="A1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an Artin ring.
Since <math id="A1.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, <math id="A1.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is not a product of two nonzero rings by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.8.6.1]</cite>; so <math id="A1.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an Artin local ring. Since <math id="A1.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is
reduced, <math id="A1.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced; so <math id="A1.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a field, which is a finite extension
of <math id="A1.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed, therefore <math id="A1.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=K</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
cohomology commutes with flat base change, consequently
<math id="A1.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">The isomorphism <math id="A1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{H}^{0}(X,\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="A1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}\to f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}\to\textup{H}^{0}(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}\to f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}\to\textup{H}^{0}(X_{s},\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">So the second map is a surjection. Hence this map is an isomorphism by
the implication (iv)<math id="A1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>(iii) of Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> with
<math id="A1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the first map is an
isomorphism too.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="A1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\to f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\to f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective at <math id="A1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, denote its cokernel by <math id="A1.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since tensor product is right
exact and since <math id="A1.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}\to f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}\to f_{*}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\otimes k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism, <math id="A1.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\otimes k_{s}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\otimes k_{s}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. So by Nakayama’s lemma, the stalk <math id="A1.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, as
claimed</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module associated to <math id="A1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. Then <math id="A1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is free at <math id="A1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> by
the implication (iv)<math id="A1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>(i) of Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. And
<math id="A1.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rank\mathcal{Q}_{s}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rank\mathcal{Q}_{s}=1</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the isomorphism in (<a href="#S3.E1a" title="In 3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10.1</span></a>) with <math id="A1.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. But, with <math id="A1.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the isomorphism becomes
<math id="A1.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SHom(\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\SHom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SHom(\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
too is free of rank 1 at <math id="A1.p28.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the surjection <math id="A1.p28.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\to f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\to f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism at <math id="A1.p28.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p28.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary, <math id="A1.p28.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, let <math id="A1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary <math id="A1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Then <math id="A1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}\colon X_{T}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> too
is proper and flat, and its geometric fibers are reduced and connected.
Hence, by what we just proved, <math id="A1.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{T}*\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!T}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{T}*\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S3.Thmthm14" title="Exercise 3.14. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>, <math id="A1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. So
by Yoneda’s Lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, (0,1.1.4), p. 20]</cite>, there exists a <math id="A1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\LinSys_{\mathcal{L}/X/S}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> possessing the required universal property.
And <math id="A1.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the identity map <math id="A1.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon L\to L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon L\to L</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof
of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a> now shows
<math id="A1.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{L}}(W)=(\mathcal{L}|X_{L})\otimes f_{L}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{L}}(W)=(\mathcal{L}|X_{L})\otimes f_{L}^{*}\mathcal{O}_{L}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Exercise 4.2. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">The structure sheaf <math id="A1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a section <math id="A1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon S\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon S\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image is a subscheme, which is closed if
<math id="A1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is separated, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, Cors. (5.1.4), p. 275, and (5.2.4),
p. 278]</cite>. Let <math id="A1.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\subset T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\subset T</annotation></semantics></math> be the pullback of this subscheme under
the map <math id="A1.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="A1.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the third
property holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">Both <math id="A1.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> define the same map <math id="A1.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So, since <math id="A1.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally, the
Comparison Theorem, Theorem, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, implies that there
exists an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> such that the first property
holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the second property. Then <math id="A1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}{\mathcal{N}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}{\mathcal{N}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t\colon T^{\prime}\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t\colon T^{\prime}\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is also defined by <math id="A1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{X_{T^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{X_{T^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, <math id="A1.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda t</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="A1.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon S\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon S\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="A1.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. So, since
the first property holds, <math id="A1.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}t^{*}{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T^{\prime}}\simeq f_{T^{\prime}}^{*}t^{*}{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="A1.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}\simeq t^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}\simeq t^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma (<a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>). Thus the second
property holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, suppose the pair <math id="A1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{1},\,\mathcal{N}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{1},\,\mathcal{N}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> also possesses the first
property. Taking <math id="A1.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to be the inclusion of <math id="A1.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="A1.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude
that <math id="A1.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}\subset N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}\subset N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{1}\simeq\mathcal{N}|N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{1}\simeq\mathcal{N}|N</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A1.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{1},\,\mathcal{N}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{1},\,\mathcal{N}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> possess the second property too. Then, similarly, <math id="A1.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\subset N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\subset N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="A1.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{1}\simeq\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{1}\simeq\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Yoneda’s Lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA G</a>, (0,1.1.4), p. 20]</cite>, a universal sheaf <math id="A1.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists if and only if <math id="A1.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="A1.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="A1.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Then, for any invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
plainly <math id="A1.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a universal sheaf. Moreover, if
<math id="A1.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a universal sheaf, then <math id="A1.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\prime}\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> by the definition with
<math id="A1.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=1_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=1_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="A1.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!S}\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}f_{*}\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> holds universally. If <math id="A1.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaves
<math id="A1.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\simeq\mathcal{N}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Part 2 of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, if also <math id="A1.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section, then
<math id="A1.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> does represent <math id="A1.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; so then <math id="A1.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists.
Furthermore, the curve <math id="A1.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 2.4. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> provides an
example where no <math id="A1.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, because <math id="A1.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>, but <math id="A1.p38.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not
since the two functors differ.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.11" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say <math id="A1.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, for any
<math id="A1.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="A1.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime})\et}(T)=\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime})\et}(T)=\Pic_{(X/S)\et}(T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p39.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p39.2.1" class="ltx_text">which holds essentially by definition, since a map of <math id="A1.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes is an
étale-covering if and only if it is an étale-covering when viewed as
a map of <math id="A1.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes. However,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime})(T)=\IPic_{X/S}(T)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime})(T)=\IPic_{X/S}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p39.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p39.3.1" class="ltx_text">because the structure map <math id="A1.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed. Since the right-hand
sides of the two displayed equations are equal, so are their left-hand
sides. Thus <math id="A1.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}\times_{S}S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X_{S^{\prime}}/S^{\prime})\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. Of
course, a similar analysis applies when <math id="A1.p39.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents one of
the other relative Picard functors.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">An example is provided by the curve <math id="A1.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbf{P}^{2}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> of
Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 2.4. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. Indeed, since the functors <math id="A1.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}</annotation></semantics></math> differ, <math id="A1.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not
representable. But <math id="A1.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by the Main
Theorem, <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Finally, since <math id="A1.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="A1.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>-point,
all its relative Picard functors are equal by the Comparison Theorem,
<a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.12" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm5" title="Exercise 4.5. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text">An <math id="A1.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on an <math id="A1.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map <math id="A1.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(k)\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(k)\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>; assign
its image to <math id="A1.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, given any field <math id="A1.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="A1.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the
pullback <math id="A1.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned the same scheme point of
<math id="A1.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider an <math id="A1.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on an <math id="A1.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represent
the same class, then there is a <math id="A1.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> containing both <math id="A1.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="A1.p42.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, then both <math id="A1.p42.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p42.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are assigned the same scheme point of <math id="A1.p42.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, if <math id="A1.p42.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p42.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are assigned the same point, take
<math id="A1.p42.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to be any algebraically closed field containing both <math id="A1.p42.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p42.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A1.p42.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p42.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> define the same map
<math id="A1.p42.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(k^{\prime\prime})\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(k^{\prime\prime})\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p42.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≃</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}\simeq\mathcal{L}^{\prime}|X_{k^{\prime\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> or <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Exercise 2.6. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, given any scheme point of <math id="A1.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be the
algebraic closure of its residue field. Then <math id="A1.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Spec(k)\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Spec(k)\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined by an <math id="A1.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 2.3. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> or
<a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Exercise 2.6. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. So the given point is assigned to <math id="A1.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the
classes of invertible sheaves on the fibers of <math id="A1.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> correspond
bijectively to the scheme points of <math id="A1.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.13" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 4.7. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">An <math id="A1.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="A1.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\colon T\to\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon T\to\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a relative effective
divisor <math id="A1.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. So the composition <math id="A1.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h\colon T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h\colon T\to P</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponds to the invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="A1.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq(1\times\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq(1\times\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{P}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p44.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, if <math id="A1.p44.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is viewed as a
<math id="A1.p44.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme via <math id="A1.p44.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p44.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> defines a <math id="A1.p44.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="A1.p44.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="A1.p44.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, the assignment <math id="A1.p44.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\mapsto\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\mapsto\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is
functorial in <math id="A1.p44.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if <math id="A1.p44.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is viewed as a <math id="A1.p44.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme via
<math id="A1.p44.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is a natural map <math id="A1.p44.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> from its functor of
points to <math id="A1.p44.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, <math id="A1.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Indeed, let <math id="A1.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a
<math id="A1.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. A <math id="A1.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="A1.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by
a relative effective divisor <math id="A1.p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p45.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{P}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{P}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p45.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p45.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p45.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p45.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> define the same
<math id="A1.p45.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="A1.p45.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to P</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A1.p45.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> defines the structure map. And <math id="A1.p45.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> defines the composition <math id="A1.p45.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}h</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p45.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\colon T\to\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon T\to\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the map defined by <math id="A1.p45.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p45.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\Lambda(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\Lambda(h)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p45.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>
is determined by <math id="A1.p45.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>; hence, <math id="A1.p45.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, <math id="A1.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}</annotation></semantics></math>. But
<math id="A1.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> too represents <math id="A1.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\LinSys</mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\LinSys_{\mathcal{P}/X\times P/P}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm13" title="Theorem 3.13. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="A1.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-schemes.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.14" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 4.10. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p47.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, suppose <math id="A1.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjection. Given a map of
étale sheaves <math id="A1.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon F\to H</annotation></semantics></math> such that the two maps <math id="A1.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\to H</annotation></semantics></math> are
equal, we must show there is one and only one map <math id="A1.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A1.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in G(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in G(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis, there exist an étale covering
<math id="A1.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\to T</annotation></semantics></math> and an element <math id="A1.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
have the same image in <math id="A1.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A1.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}:=T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}:=T^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the two
images of <math id="A1.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> define an element <math id="A1.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="A1.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F\times_{G}F)(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F\times_{G}F)(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the two maps <math id="A1.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\to H</annotation></semantics></math> are equal, the two
images of <math id="A1.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(T^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(T^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are equal. But these two images are
equal to those of <math id="A1.p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p48.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a sheaf,
therefore <math id="A1.p48.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of a unique element <math id="A1.p48.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in H(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in H(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p49.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p49.1.1" class="ltx_text">Note <math id="A1.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in H(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in H(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the choice of <math id="A1.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta^{\prime}\in F(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, let <math id="A1.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{\prime}\in F(T_{1}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{\prime}\in F(T_{1}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be a second
choice. Arguing as above, we find <math id="A1.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\zeta_{1}^{\prime})\in H(T_{1}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\zeta_{1}^{\prime})\in H(T_{1}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p49.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> have the same image in <math id="A1.p49.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(T_{1}^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(T_{1}^{\prime}\times_{T}T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. So
<math id="A1.p49.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> also leads to <math id="A1.p49.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p50.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define a map <math id="A1.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(T)\to H(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(T)\to H(T)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A1.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\mapsto\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\mapsto\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly this map
behaves functorially in <math id="A1.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there is a map of sheaves <math id="A1.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Plainly, <math id="A1.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A1.p50.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon F\to H</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="A1.p50.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is
the only such map, since the image of <math id="A1.p50.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p50.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined
by the image of <math id="A1.p50.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p50.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the latter must map to
<math id="A1.p50.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(\zeta^{\prime})\in H(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p50.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the coequalizer of
<math id="A1.p50.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="A1.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the coequalizer of <math id="A1.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\rightrightarrows F</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the étale subsheaf <math id="A1.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the presheaf
whose <math id="A1.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-points are the images in <math id="A1.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(T)</annotation></semantics></math> of the elements of <math id="A1.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the map <math id="A1.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="A1.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. So the two maps <math id="A1.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times_{G}F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times_{G}F\to H</annotation></semantics></math> are equal. Since <math id="A1.p51.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the coequalizer, there is a map <math id="A1.p51.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> so
that <math id="A1.p51.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G\to H</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A1.p51.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to H</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p51.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G\to H\hookrightarrow G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G\to H\hookrightarrow G</annotation></semantics></math> is
equal to <math id="A1.p51.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p51.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to H\hookrightarrow G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to H\hookrightarrow G</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A1.p51.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> by uniqueness.
Therefore, <math id="A1.p51.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p51.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to G</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjection.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.15" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> implies each connected
component <math id="A1.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> lies in an increasing union of open
quasi-projective subschemes of <math id="A1.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in one of
them since <math id="A1.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact. So <math id="A1.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective. But <math id="A1.p52.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
has only finitely many components <math id="A1.p52.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p52.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.16" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm12" title="Exercise 4.12. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.12</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A1.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists by
Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. If <math id="A1.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists, then <math id="A1.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
is, by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 4.7. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, the structure map of the bundle <math id="A1.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the coherent sheaf on <math id="A1.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
associated to <math id="A1.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>. In particular, <math id="A1.p53.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective Zariski locally over <math id="A1.p53.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p54.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p54.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general, forming <math id="A1.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with extending <math id="A1.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. Similarly, forming <math id="A1.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> does too. But
a map is proper if it is after an fppf base extension by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.7.1(vii)]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p55.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p55.1.1" class="ltx_text">However, <math id="A1.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is fppf. Moreover, <math id="A1.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X}\colon X\times X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X}\colon X\times X\to X</annotation></semantics></math>
has a section, namely, the diagonal. So use <math id="A1.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a base extension.
Then, by Exercises <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, a universal sheaf
<math id="A1.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> exists. Therefore, <math id="A1.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper by the first case.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.17" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm13" title="Exercise 4.13. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p56.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p56.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s use the ideas and notation of
Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm12" title="Exercise 4.12. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.12</span></a>. Now, <math id="A1.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Div_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\Div</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Div_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Exercise 3.8. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. Hence the Abel map <math id="A1.p56.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a
natural map <math id="A1.p56.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon X_{0}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon X_{0}\to P</annotation></semantics></math>, and forming <math id="A1.p56.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with extending <math id="A1.p56.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
But a map is a closed embedding if it is after an fppf base extension by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.7.1(xii)]</cite>. So we may assume <math id="A1.p56.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p57" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p57.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p57.1.1" class="ltx_text">The function <math id="A1.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\mapsto\deg\mathcal{P}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\mapsto\deg\mathcal{P}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally constant. Let
<math id="A1.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be the open and closed subset where the function’s value is
<math id="A1.p57.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="A1.p57.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})=\IDiv_{X/S}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the above.
Therefore, <math id="A1.p57.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p57.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon X_{0}\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon X_{0}\to P</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the
structure map of <math id="A1.p57.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})</annotation></semantics></math>. So it remains to show that this
structure map is a closed embedding.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p58.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p58.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="A1.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in W</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{k_{\lambda}}(\mathcal{Q}\otimes k_{\lambda})=\dim_{k_{\lambda}}\textup{H}^{0}(X_{\lambda},\mathcal{P}_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{k_{\lambda}}(\mathcal{Q}\otimes k_{\lambda})=\dim_{k_{\lambda}}\textup{H}^{0}(X_{\lambda},\mathcal{P}_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
<math id="A1.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> has two independent global sections. Each defines an
effective divisor of degree 1, which is a <math id="A1.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-rational point
<math id="A1.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since neither section is a multiple of the other, the <math id="A1.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are
distinct. Hence the sections generate <math id="A1.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. So they define a
map <math id="A1.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\colon X_{\lambda}\to\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon X_{\lambda}\to\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA II</a>, 4.2.3]</cite> or
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. II, 7.1, p. 150]</cite>. Then <math id="A1.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is birational since each
<math id="A1.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the scheme-theoretic inverse image of a <math id="A1.p58.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-rational
point of <math id="A1.p58.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{1}_{k_{\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p58.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. But, by
hypothesis, <math id="A1.p58.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is of arithmetic genus at least 1. So there is
a contradiction. Therefore, <math id="A1.p58.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{k_{\lambda}}(\mathcal{Q}\otimes k_{\lambda})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{k_{\lambda}}(\mathcal{Q}\otimes k_{\lambda})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p59" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p59.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p59.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Nakayama’s lemma, <math id="A1.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> can be generated by a single element on a
neighborhood <math id="A1.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p59.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. So there is a surjection <math id="A1.p59.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!V}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{Q}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!V}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal{Q}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. It defines a closed embedding <math id="A1.p59.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{V})\hookrightarrow\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{O}_{\!V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{V})\hookrightarrow\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{O}_{\!V})</annotation></semantics></math>. But the structure map <math id="A1.p59.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{O}_{\!V})\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{O}_{\!V})\to V</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isomorphism. Hence <math id="A1.p59.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{V})\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{V})\to V</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed embedding. But
<math id="A1.p59.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in W</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary. So <math id="A1.p59.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})\to W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q}_{W})\to W</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed
a closed embedding.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.18" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 4.15. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p60" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p60.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p60.1.1" class="ltx_text">Representing <math id="A1.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is similar to representing <math id="A1.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X^{\prime}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> in
Example <a href="#S4.Thmthm14" title="Example 4.14. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>, but simpler. Indeed, On <math id="A1.p60.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times_{S}\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{S}\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, form
an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p60.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> by placing <math id="A1.p60.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="A1.p60.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th copy of
<math id="A1.p60.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it suffices to show this: given any <math id="A1.p60.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="A1.p60.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and any
invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p60.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p60.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist a unique <math id="A1.p60.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map
<math id="A1.p60.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon T\to\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon T\to\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and some invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p60.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p60.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p60.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p61.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p61.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, we may assume <math id="A1.p61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Then the function
<math id="A1.p61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\mapsto\chi(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\mapsto\chi(X_{t},\mathcal{L}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on <math id="A1.p61.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.11]</cite>. Now, <math id="A1.p61.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective space of dimension
at least 1 over the residue field <math id="A1.p61.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; so <math id="A1.p61.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.p61.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp. 6.4, p. 132, and Cors. 6.16
and 6.17, p. 145]</cite>. Hence <math id="A1.p61.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A1.p61.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p62.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p62.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A1.p62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{L}^{-1}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{L}^{-1}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p62.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p62.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(X_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(X_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p62.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(X_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})=k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Serre’s explicit computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, 2.1.12]</cite>. Hence
<math id="A1.p62.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, and forming it commutes with
changing the base <math id="A1.p62.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, owing to the theory in Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p63.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p63.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A1.p63.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=f_{\smash{T}*}\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the natural map
<math id="A1.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Forming <math id="A1.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with changing <math id="A1.p63.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, since
forming <math id="A1.p63.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> does. But <math id="A1.p63.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism on the fiber over each
<math id="A1.p63.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p63.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\otimes k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\otimes k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Hence <math id="A1.p63.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective by
Nakayama’s lemma. But both source and target of <math id="A1.p63.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible; so
<math id="A1.p63.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Hence <math id="A1.p63.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p64.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p64.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon T\to\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon T\to\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the composition of the structure map <math id="A1.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to S</annotation></semantics></math>
and the <math id="A1.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th inclusion <math id="A1.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="A1.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\times q)^{*}\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the only such <math id="A1.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map. Thus <math id="A1.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>
represents <math id="A1.p64.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p64.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is a universal sheaf.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.19" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S4.Thmthm16" title="Exercise 4.16. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p65.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p65.1.1" class="ltx_text">First of all, <math id="A1.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Now,
<math id="A1.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\mathbb{C}}\simeq\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A1.p65.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}\times_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}\times_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}\simeq\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> by Exercises <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 4.4. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and
<a href="#S4.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 4.15. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a>. The induced automorphism of <math id="A1.p65.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the identity; indeed, a point of this scheme corresponds to an
invertible sheaf on <math id="A1.p65.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and every such sheaf is isomorphic
to its pullback under any <math id="A1.p65.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-automorphism of <math id="A1.p65.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, by descent theory, <math id="A1.p65.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}=\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}=\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p66.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p66.1.1" class="ltx_text">The above reasoning leads to a second proof <math id="A1.p66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable. Indeed, set <math id="A1.p66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\Pic_{(X/\mathbb{R})\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the above,
the pair <math id="A1.p66.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C})\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C})\rightrightarrows P\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C})\otimes_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C})\rightrightarrows P\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is representable by the pair <math id="A1.p66.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}\rightrightarrows\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}\otimes_{\mathbb{R}}\mathbb{C}}\rightrightarrows\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose
coequalizer is <math id="A1.p66.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, in the category of
étale sheaves, the coequalizer is <math id="A1.p66.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> owing to Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 4.10. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p67" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p67.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p67.1.1" class="ltx_text">Notice in passing that <math id="A1.p67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}=\IPic_{\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{R}}/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/\mathbb{R}}=\IPic_{\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{R}}/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, <math id="A1.p67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{X/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not representable owing to
Exercise <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 2.4. ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, where as <math id="A1.p67.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{R}}/\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{\mathbf{P}^{1}_{\mathbb{R}}/\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
representable owing to Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 4.15. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.20" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p68.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p68.1.1" class="ltx_text">Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a> implies <math id="A1.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective. Hence <math id="A1.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
is projective if <math id="A1.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.7.1]</cite>, an <math id="A1.p68.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme
is proper if it is so after an fppf base change, such as <math id="A1.p68.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>.
But <math id="A1.p68.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X}\colon X\times X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X}\colon X\times X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> has a section, namely, the diagonal. Thus we may
assume <math id="A1.p68.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p69.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p69.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using the Valuative Criterion for Properness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 4.7,
p. 101]</cite>, we need only check this statement: given an <math id="A1.p69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="A1.p69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
of the form <math id="A1.p69.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Spec(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Spec(A)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p69.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a valuation ring, say with
fraction field <math id="A1.p69.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="A1.p69.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="A1.p69.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\Spec(K)\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Spec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\Spec(K)\to Z</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an
<math id="A1.p69.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map <math id="A1.p69.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>. We do not need to check the extension is unique if it
exists; indeed, this uniqueness holds by the Valuative Criterion for
Separatedness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 4.3, p. 97]</cite> since <math id="A1.p69.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective,
so separated.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p70" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p70.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p70.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has a section, <math id="A1.p70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> arises from an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p70.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A1.p70.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Comparison). ‣ 2. The several Picard functors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. We have to extend <math id="A1.p70.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.p70.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, this extension defines a map <math id="A1.p70.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> extending
<math id="A1.p70.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p70.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="A1.p70.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="A1.p70.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is closed and <math id="A1.p70.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is
integral.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p71" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p71.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p71.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly it suffices to extend <math id="A1.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A1.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>. So replacing
<math id="A1.p71.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> if need be, we may assume <math id="A1.p71.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero section. It is
regular since <math id="A1.p71.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is integral. So <math id="A1.p71.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> has a divisor <math id="A1.p71.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p71.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D)=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D)=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p72" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p72.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p72.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}\subset X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\subset X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the closure of <math id="A1.p72.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="A1.p72.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and
<math id="A1.p72.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, so <math id="A1.p72.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is regular by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 6.5.2]</cite>, so
factorial by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 21.11.1]</cite>. Hence <math id="A1.p72.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor. And <math id="A1.p72.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> extends <math id="A1.p72.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.21" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S5.Thmthm16" title="Exercise 5.16. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p73" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p73.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p73.1.1" class="ltx_text">Serre’s Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Thm. 5.2, p. 228]</cite> yields <math id="A1.p73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p73.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p73.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p73.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n)=\chi(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n)=\chi(\Omega^{2}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\textup{H}^{1}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})-\textup{H}^{2}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\textup{H}^{1}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})-\textup{H}^{2}(\Omega^{2}_{\!X})+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p73.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p73.2.1" class="ltx_text">Serre duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Cor. 7.13, p. 247]</cite> yields <math id="A1.p73.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{i}(\Omega^{i}_{\!X})=\dim\textup{H}^{2-i}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{i}(\Omega^{i}_{\!X})=\dim\textup{H}^{2-i}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p73.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. And
<math id="A1.p73.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=1</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A1.p73.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and geometrically
integral. So</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{O}_{X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p73.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p73.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm14" title="Corollary 5.14. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a> yields <math id="A1.p73.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\IPic_{X/S}\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\IPic_{X/S}\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>, with
equality in characteristic 0.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.22" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S5.Thmthm17" title="Exercise 5.17. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.17</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p74.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p74.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A1.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which exists by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. By
Exercises <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 3.11. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, there exists a universal
sheaf <math id="A1.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p74.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times P</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p75" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p75.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p75.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p75.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension 0 everywhere by
Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>. Let <math id="A1.p75.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a relative effective divisor on
<math id="A1.p75.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p75.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected <math id="A1.p75.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Then <math id="A1.p75.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>
defines a map <math id="A1.p75.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:T\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:T\to P</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p75.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p75.2.1" class="ltx_text">for some invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p75.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p75.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, <math id="A1.p75.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and
<math id="A1.p75.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is discrete and reduced; so <math id="A1.p75.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is constant. Set <math id="A1.p75.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:=\tau T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:=\tau T</annotation></semantics></math>, and view <math id="A1.p75.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> as an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p75.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p75.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A1.p75.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{T}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{T}=(1\times\tau)^{*}\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p75.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p76" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p76.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p76.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the converse. Again by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Exercise 4.3. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, there is a
coherent sheaf <math id="A1.p76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p76.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})=\IDiv_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, <math id="A1.p76.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero and locally free at any closed point
<math id="A1.p76.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> representing an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p76.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p76.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p76.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Subsection <a href="#S3.Thmthm10" title="3.10The module 
            
              Q
            
          . ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>; for example, take <math id="A1.p76.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p76.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p77" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p77.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p77.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected open neighborhood of <math id="A1.p77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on which
<math id="A1.p77.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is free. Let <math id="A1.p77.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset\mathbf{P}(\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> be the preimage of <math id="A1.p77.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="A1.p77.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal relative effective divisor on <math id="A1.p77.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}/T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the
natural map <math id="A1.p77.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon T\to U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth with irreducible fibers. So <math id="A1.p77.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected. Moreover, <math id="A1.p77.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the map defined by <math id="A1.p77.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p78" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p78.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p78.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{M}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{T}}(D)\simeq\mathcal{M}_{T}\otimes f_{T}^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for some
invertible sheaves <math id="A1.p78.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p78.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p78.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p78.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p78.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon T\to U</annotation></semantics></math>
is also defined by <math id="A1.p78.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Say <math id="A1.p78.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in P</annotation></semantics></math> represents <math id="A1.p78.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="A1.p78.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> factors through the inclusion of the closed point <math id="A1.p78.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="A1.p78.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover, since <math id="A1.p78.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and surjective, its image,
the open set <math id="A1.p78.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, is just the reduced closed point <math id="A1.p78.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, there
is an automorphism of <math id="A1.p78.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that carries 0 to <math id="A1.p78.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, namely,
“multiplication” by <math id="A1.p78.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p78.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth of dimension 0 at
0. Therefore, <math id="A1.p78.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p79" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p79.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p79.1.1" class="ltx_text">In characteristic 0, a priori <math id="A1.p79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm14" title="Corollary 5.14. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.14</span></a>.
Now, <math id="A1.p79.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon T\to U</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. Hence, <math id="A1.p79.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth too. But the
preceding argument shows that, if the condition holds for this <math id="A1.p79.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="A1.p79.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.23" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S5.Thmthm23" title="Exercise 5.23. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.23</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p80" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p80.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p80.1.1" class="ltx_text">By hypothesis, <math id="A1.p80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim X_{s}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim X_{s}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>; so <math id="A1.p80.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence the <math id="A1.p80.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 5.19. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.19</span></a>, so
of dimension <math id="A1.p80.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm13" title="Corollary 5.13. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>. Hence, by
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a>, the <math id="A1.p80.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X_{s}/k_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> form a family of finite
type, whose total space is the open subscheme <math id="A1.p80.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="A1.p80.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="A1.p80.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="A1.p80.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> again by
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 5.19. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.19</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p81" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p81.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p81.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="A1.p81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p82" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p82.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p82.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="A1.p82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, then <math id="A1.p82.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective over <math id="A1.p82.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. Alternatively, use Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> and
Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm20" title="Proposition 5.20. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.20</span></a> again to conclude <math id="A1.p82.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, so
projective since it is quasi-projective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p83" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p83.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p83.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, assume <math id="A1.p83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, and let us prove <math id="A1.p83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth, Since <math id="A1.p83.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat, we need only prove each <math id="A1.p83.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.
So we may replace <math id="A1.p83.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by the spectrum of the algebraic closure of <math id="A1.p83.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="A1.p83.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p83.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, indeed <math id="A1.p83.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbf{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbf{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Ex. 1.8(b),
p. 298]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p84" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p84.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p84.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.p84.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subscheme where <math id="A1.p84.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth.
Then there is a closed embedding <math id="A1.p84.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\colon X_{0}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\colon X_{0}\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm13" title="Exercise 4.13. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>. Its image consists of points <math id="A1.p84.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
representing invertible sheaves of degree <math id="A1.p84.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a rational point
<math id="A1.p84.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and define an automorphism <math id="A1.p84.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p84.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A1.p84.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(\kappa):=\kappa\lambda^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(\kappa):=\kappa\lambda^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p84.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta A</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed
embedding of <math id="A1.p84.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p84.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p85" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p85.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p85.1.1" class="ltx_text">By assumption, <math id="A1.p85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. So <math id="A1.p85.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. Hence
<math id="A1.p85.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> is proper since <math id="A1.p85.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is separated. Hence <math id="A1.p85.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in
<math id="A1.p85.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A1.p85.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="A1.p85.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A1.p85.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is integral and the ground
field is algebraically closed. Hence <math id="A1.p85.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}=X</annotation></semantics></math>; in other words, <math id="A1.p85.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.24" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Exercise 6.4. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p86" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p86.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p86.1.1" class="ltx_text">As before, by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>, there is an <math id="A1.p86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that every <math id="A1.p86.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by its global sections. So there is a section that
does not vanish at any given associated point of <math id="A1.p86.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; since these points
are finite in number, if <math id="A1.p86.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a general linear combination of the
corresponding sections, then <math id="A1.p86.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes at no associated point.
So <math id="A1.p86.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is regular, whence defines an effective divisor <math id="A1.p86.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="A1.p86.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{N}^{-1}(-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)=\mathcal{N}^{-1}(-m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p87" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p87.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p87.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly <math id="A1.p87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is numerically equivalent to <math id="A1.p87.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> too. So
<math id="A1.p87.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{N}^{-1}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{N}^{-1}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6.6. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>. Hence
the sequence <math id="A1.p87.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(-D)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>
yields</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!D}(n))=\psi(n)\text{ where }\psi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n-m))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!D}(n))=\psi(n)\text{ where }\psi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n))-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(n-m)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p88" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p88.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p88.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset\IDiv_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset\IDiv_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open and closed subscheme
parameterizing the effective divisors with Hilbert polynomial <math id="A1.p88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A1.p88.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="A1.p88.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme of finite type. Let <math id="A1.p88.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the invertible
sheaf on <math id="A1.p88.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the universal divisor; set <math id="A1.p88.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{M}^{\prime}(-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{M}^{\prime}(-n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a rational point <math id="A1.p88.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p88.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the <math id="A1.p88.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> numerically equivalent to <math id="A1.p88.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>
form a bounded family.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.25" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 6.7. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p89" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p89.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p89.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;a_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;a_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A1.p89.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonzero section. It
defines an effective divisor <math id="A1.p89.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly 0. Hence</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle 0\leq\int h^{r-1}[D]=\int h^{r-1}\ell-\int h^{r}=a-a_{r}&lt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle 0\leq\int h^{r-1}[D]=\int h^{r-1}\ell-\int h^{r}=a-a_{r}&lt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p89.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p89.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is absurd. Thus <math id="A1.p89.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p90" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p90.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p90.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperplane section of <math id="A1.p90.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{L}(n-1)\to\mathcal{L}(n)\to\mathcal{L}_{H}(n)\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{L}(n-1)\to\mathcal{L}(n)\to\mathcal{L}_{H}(n)\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p90.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p90.2.1" class="ltx_text">It yields the following bound:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))-\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n-1))\leq\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))-\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n-1))\leq\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(A.<a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 6.7. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p90.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p90.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p90.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n+i}{i}-\binom{n-1+i}{i}=\binom{n+i-1}{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n+i}{i}-\binom{n-1+i}{i}=\binom{n+i-1}{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence
also yields the following formula:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r-1}a_{i+1}\binom{n+i}{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r-1}a_{i+1}\binom{n+i}{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p91" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p91.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p91.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p91.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=\chi(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))=a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, owing to Equation (<a href="#A1.E1" title="In Answer . ‣ Appendix A Answers to all the exercises ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A..1</span></a>), induction on <math id="A1.p91.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> yields
<math id="A1.p91.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq a_{1}(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(n))\leq a_{1}(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p92" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p92.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p92.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, <math id="A1.p92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p92.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-regular. Set <math id="A1.p92.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L}(-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L}(-1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p92.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p92.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular by Mumford’s conclusion at the
bottom of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm66</a>, p. 102]</cite>. But</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))-\chi(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0-a_{1}(-1+1)-a_{0}=-a_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(-1))-\chi(\mathcal{L}(-1))=0-a_{1}(-1+1)-a_{0}=-a_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p92.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p92.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus we may take <math id="A1.p92.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{1}(u_{0}):=-u_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{1}(u_{0}):=-u_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.p92.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an indeterminate.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p93" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p93.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p93.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we may take <math id="A1.p93.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible by Bertini’s
Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Se50</a>, Thm. 12, p. 374]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jo79</a>, Cor. 6.7, p. 80]</cite>.
Set <math id="A1.p93.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}:=c_{1}O_{\!H}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}:=c_{1}O_{\!H}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p93.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}:=c_{1}\mathcal{L}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="A1.p93.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\ell_{1}h_{1}^{r-2}=\int\ell h^{r-2}[H]=a&lt;a_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\ell_{1}h_{1}^{r-2}=\int\ell h^{r-2}[H]=a&lt;a_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So by induction on <math id="A1.p93.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))\leq a_{r}\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{H}(n))\leq a_{r}\tbinom{n+r-1}{r-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p93.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p93.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, owing to Equation (<a href="#A1.E1" title="In Answer . ‣ Appendix A Answers to all the exercises ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A..1</span></a>), induction on <math id="A1.p93.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>
yields the desired bound.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p94" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p94.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p94.1.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, we may assume <math id="A1.p94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p94.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-regular where
<math id="A1.p94.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}:=\Phi_{r-1}(a_{1}\dotsc,a_{r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}:=\Phi_{r-1}(a_{1}\dotsc,a_{r-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A1.p94.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=m_{1}+\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=m_{1}+\dim\textup{H}^{1}(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))</annotation></semantics></math>. By Mumford’s same work, <math id="A1.p94.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p94.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular.
But</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=m_{1}+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))-\chi(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=m_{1}+\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))-\chi(\mathcal{L}(m_{1}-1))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\textstyle m_{1}+a_{r}\binom{m_{1}-1+r}{r}-\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}a_{i}\binom{m_{1}-1+i}{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\textstyle m_{1}+a_{r}\binom{m_{1}-1+r}{r}-\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}a_{i}\binom{m_{1}-1+i}{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p94.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p94.2.1" class="ltx_text">The latter expression is a polynomial in <math id="A1.p94.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p94.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. So it is a polynomial <math id="A1.p94.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p94.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> alone, as
desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p95" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p95.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p95.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general, consider <math id="A1.p95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{L}(-a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{L}(-a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{N}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}b_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}\text{ where }b_{i}:=\sum_{j=0}^{r-i}a_{i+j}(-1)^{j}\binom{a-i-j}{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{N}(n))=\textstyle\sum_{0\leq i\leq r}b_{i}\binom{n+i}{i}\text{ where }b_{i}:=\sum_{j=0}^{r-i}a_{i+j}(-1)^{j}\binom{a-i-j}{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p95.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p95.2.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A1.p95.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:=c_{1}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:=c_{1}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p95.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b:=\int\nu h^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b:=\int\nu h^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle b=\int\ell h^{r-1}-a\int h^{r}=a-aa_{r}\leq 0&lt;a_{r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle b=\int\ell h^{r-1}-a\int h^{r}=a-aa_{r}\leq 0&lt;a_{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p95.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p95.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence <math id="A1.p95.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p95.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-regular where <math id="A1.p95.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\Phi_{r}(b_{0},\dotsc,b_{r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\Phi_{r}(b_{0},\dotsc,b_{r-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
But the <math id="A1.p95.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomials in <math id="A1.p95.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p95.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence there
is a polynomial <math id="A1.p95.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="A1.p95.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p95.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\Psi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r};a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\Psi_{r}(a_{0},\dotsc,a_{r};a)</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.26" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 6.10. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p96" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p96.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p96.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the algebraic closure of <math id="A1.p96.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p96.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\otimes k^{\prime}\subset G^{\tau}\otimes k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\otimes k^{\prime}\subset G^{\tau}\otimes k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p96.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A1.p96.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}\otimes k^{\prime}=(G\otimes k^{\prime})^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}\otimes k^{\prime}=(G\otimes k^{\prime})^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm10" title="Exercise 6.10. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>. Thus we may assume <math id="A1.p96.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p97" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p97.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p97.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="A1.p97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset\bigcup_{h\in H(k)}hG^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset\bigcup_{h\in H(k)}hG^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A1.p97.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is open, so <math id="A1.p97.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hG^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hG^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is
too. And <math id="A1.p97.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact. So <math id="A1.p97.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> lies in finitely many
<math id="A1.p97.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hG^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hG^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p97.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{0}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{0}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> has finite index in <math id="A1.p97.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(k)G^{0}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(k)G^{0}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="A1.p97.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="A1.p97.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{n}\in G^{0}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{n}\in G^{0}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="A1.p97.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p97.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}(H)\subset G^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}(H)\subset G^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="A1.p97.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.27" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 6.11. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p98" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p98.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p98.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="A1.p98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, plainly <math id="A1.p98.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="A1.p98.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
And <math id="A1.p98.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically equivalent to <math id="A1.p98.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only
if <math id="A1.p98.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{n}\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{n}\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm10" title="Proposition 5.10. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>. So
<math id="A1.p98.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p98.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalent to <math id="A1.p98.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="A1.p98.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> by Definitions <a href="#S6.Thmthm1" title="Definition 6.1. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>
and <a href="#S6.Thmthm8" title="Definition 6.8. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.28" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm13" title="Exercise 6.13. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.13</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p99" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p99.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p99.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Main). ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> implies <math id="A1.p99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and represents
<math id="A1.p99.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pic_{(X/S)\et}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\et</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pic_{(X/S)\et}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p99.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type by
Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>. Hence <math id="A1.p99.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-projective
by Exercise <a href="#S4.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 4.11. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p100" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p100.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p100.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is also geometrically normal. Since <math id="A1.p100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
quasi-projective, to prove it is projective, it suffices to prove it is
complete. By Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>, forming <math id="A1.p100.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>
commutes with extending <math id="A1.p100.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. And by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA IV2</a>, 2.7.1(vii)]</cite>, a
<math id="A1.p100.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme is complete if (and only if) it is after extending <math id="A1.p100.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. So
assume <math id="A1.p100.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is algebraically closed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p101" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p101.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p101.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="A1.p101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over the <math id="A1.p101.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="A1.p101.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cosets
<math id="A1.p101.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="A1.p101.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. So finitely many cosets
cover, since <math id="A1.p101.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open by Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and
since <math id="A1.p101.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact, Now, <math id="A1.p101.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is projective
by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, so complete, And <math id="A1.p101.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed
again by Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>. Therefore, <math id="A1.p101.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
complete.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.29" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 6.15. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.15</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p102" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p102.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p102.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded. Then <math id="A1.p102.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a map <math id="A1.p102.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\colon T\to\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p102.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)\supset\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)\supset\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.p102.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian,
plainly so is <math id="A1.p102.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)</annotation></semantics></math>; whence, plainly so is any subspace of
<math id="A1.p102.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p102.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p103" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p103.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p103.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="A1.p103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact. Since <math id="A1.p103.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is
locally of finite type by Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm17" title="Proposition 4.17. ‣ 4. The Picard scheme ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a>, there is an open
subscheme of finite type containing any given point of <math id="A1.p103.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. So
finitely many of the subschemes cover <math id="A1.p103.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote their union by
<math id="A1.p103.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p104" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p104.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p104.1.1" class="ltx_text">The inclusion <math id="A1.p104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by an invertible sheaf
<math id="A1.p104.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p104.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some fppf covering <math id="A1.p104.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to U</annotation></semantics></math>. Replace <math id="A1.p104.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be an
open subscheme so that <math id="A1.p104.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to U</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type and surjective. Since
<math id="A1.p104.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type, so is <math id="A1.p104.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="A1.p104.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.p104.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> map to <math id="A1.p104.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p104.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the class of <math id="A1.p104.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.30" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm18" title="Exercise 6.18. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.18</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p105" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p105.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p105.1.1" class="ltx_text">Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm16" title="Theorem 6.16. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.16</span></a> asserts <math id="A1.p105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is of finite type. So
it is projective by Exercise <a href="#S5.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 5.7. ‣ 5. The connected component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.31" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm21" title="Exercise 6.21. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.21</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p106" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p106.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p106.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly, replacing <math id="A1.p106.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by an open subset, we may assume <math id="A1.p106.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is
projective and <math id="A1.p106.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Given <math id="A1.p106.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="A1.p106.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X_{s}}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p106.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A1.p106.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="A1.p106.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, set
<math id="A1.p106.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n):=m+\psi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n):=m+\psi(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p107" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p107.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p107.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A1.p107.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only
if <math id="A1.p107.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> represents an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p107.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="A1.p107.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. And
<math id="A1.p107.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A1.p107.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. But,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\deg(\mathcal{L}(n))+\psi(0)=\deg(\mathcal{L})+\psi(n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L}(n))=\deg(\mathcal{L}(n))+\psi(0)=\deg(\mathcal{L})+\psi(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p107.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p107.2.1" class="ltx_text">by Riemann’s Theorem and the additivity of <math id="A1.p107.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\bullet)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\bullet)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="A1.p107.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\phi}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. So Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmthm20" title="Theorem 6.20. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.20</span></a> yields all
the assertions, except for the two middle about <math id="A1.p107.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p108" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p108.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p108.1.1" class="ltx_text">To show <math id="A1.p108.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}=\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, similarly we need only show
<math id="A1.p108.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\mathcal{L}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\mathcal{L}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A1.p108.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p108.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalent to <math id="A1.p108.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for, by Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm11" title="Exercise 6.11. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>, the latter holds if and only if
<math id="A1.p108.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\IPic^{\smash{\tau}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly, we may assume <math id="A1.p108.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> lives on a
geometric fiber of <math id="A1.p108.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the two conditions on <math id="A1.p108.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> are
equivalent by Theorem  <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p109" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p109.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p109.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p109.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>deg</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg</annotation></semantics></math> is additive, multiplication carries <math id="A1.p109.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\times\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}\times\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> set-theoretically into <math id="A1.p109.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="A1.p109.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>
acts on <math id="A1.p109.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> since these two sets are open in <math id="A1.p109.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p110" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p110.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p110.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.p110.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is flat with integral geometric fibers, its smooth locus
<math id="A1.p110.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> provides an fppf covering of <math id="A1.p110.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Temporarily, make the base
change <math id="A1.p110.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\to S</annotation></semantics></math>. After it, the new map <math id="A1.p110.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\to S</annotation></semantics></math> has a section. Its
image is a relative effective divisor <math id="A1.p110.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and tensoring with <math id="A1.p110.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(mD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(mD)</annotation></semantics></math> defines the desired isomorphism from <math id="A1.p110.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="A1.p110.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p111" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p111.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p111.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, to show there is no abuse of notation, we must show the fiber
<math id="A1.p111.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S})_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/S})_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. To do so, we instead make the base
change to the spectrum of an algebraically closed field <math id="A1.p111.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\supset k_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\supset k_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A1.p111.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="A1.p111.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-rational point <math id="A1.p111.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and again tensoring with <math id="A1.p111.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(mD)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(mD)</annotation></semantics></math> defines an isomorphism from <math id="A1.p111.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{0}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="A1.p111.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math>. So it suffices to show <math id="A1.p111.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/k}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected for
some <math id="A1.p111.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p112" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p112.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p112.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\colon X_{0}^{m}\to\IDiv_{X/k}^{m}\to\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IDiv</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\colon X_{0}^{m}\to\IDiv_{X/k}^{m}\to\IPic^{\smash{m}}_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> be composition of the
map <math id="A1.p112.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of Exercise <a href="#S3.Thmthm8" title="Exercise 3.8. ‣ 3. Relative effective divisors ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> and the Abel map. Since <math id="A1.p112.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
integral, so is the <math id="A1.p112.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-fold product <math id="A1.p112.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence it suffices to show
<math id="A1.p112.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective for some <math id="A1.p112.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p113" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p113.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p113.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm7" title="Exercise 6.7. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>, there is an <math id="A1.p113.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that every
invertible sheaf on <math id="A1.p113.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="A1.p113.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p113.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-regular. Set
<math id="A1.p113.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(m_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(m_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Then every invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p113.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p113.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of
degree <math id="A1.p113.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p113.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-regular, so generated by its global sections.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p114" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p114.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p114.1.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, for each singular point of <math id="A1.p114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a global section
that does not vanish at it. So, since <math id="A1.p114.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite, a general linear
combination of these sections vanishes at no singular point. This
combination defines an effective divisor <math id="A1.p114.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p114.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(E)=\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(E)=\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="A1.p114.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.32" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Answer <a href="#S6.Thmthm29" title="Exercise 6.29. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.29</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.p115" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p115.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p115.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is quasi-compact. Then, owing to
Exercise <a href="#S6.Thmthm15" title="Exercise 6.15. ‣ 6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.15</span></a>, there exist an <math id="A1.p115.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="A1.p115.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of finite type
and an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p115.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p115.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that every polynomial
<math id="A1.p115.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="A1.p115.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n)=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n)=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.p115.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.4]</cite>, the number of <math id="A1.p115.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is at most
the number of connected components of <math id="A1.p115.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p115.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p116" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p116.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p116.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A1.p116.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Then its closure is too. So we may
assume <math id="A1.p116.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. Give <math id="A1.p116.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> its reduced subscheme
structure. Then the inclusion <math id="A1.p116.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mtext class="undefined">\IPic</mtext><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow\IPic_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by
an invertible sheaf <math id="A1.p116.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p116.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for some fppf covering <math id="A1.p116.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="A1.p116.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in T</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="A1.p116.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(n):=\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t}(n))</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="A1.p116.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
and form the set <math id="A1.p116.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of points <math id="A1.p116.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p116.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p116.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}(n))=\phi(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{M}_{t^{\prime}}(n))=\phi(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III2</a>, 7.9.4]</cite>, the set <math id="A1.p116.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is open,
and so is its complement. Hence their images are open in <math id="A1.p116.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and
plainly these images are disjoint. But <math id="A1.p116.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Hence
<math id="A1.p116.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}=T</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A1.p116.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi=\{\phi\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi=\{\phi\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Basic intersection theory</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix contains an elementary treatment of basic intersection
theory, which is more than sufficient for many purposes, including the
needs of Section <a href="#S6" title="6. The torsion component of the identity ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The approach was originated in
1959–60 by Snapper. His results were generalized and his proofs were
simplified immediately afterward by Cartier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ca60</a>]</cite>. Their work was
developed further in fits and starts by the author.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The index theorem was proved by Hodge in 1937. Immediately afterward,
B. Segre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Se37</a>, § 1]</cite> gave an algebraic proof for surfaces, and
this proof was rediscovered by Grothendieck in 1958. Their work was
generalized a tad in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kl71</a>, p. 662]</cite>, and a variation appears below
in Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm27" title="Theorem B.27 (Hodge Index). ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.27</span></a>. From the index theorem,
Segre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Se37</a>, §6]</cite> derived a connectedness statement like
Corollary <a href="#A2.Thmthm29" title="Corollary B.29. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.29</span></a> for surfaces, and the proof below is basically
his.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Abelian category of coherent
sheaves <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> whose support <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper over an
Artin subscheme of <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, a 0-dimensional Noetherian closed
subscheme. For each <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full subcategory
of those <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\Supp\mathcal{F}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\Supp\mathcal{F}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the “Grothendieck group” of <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>,
namely, the free Abelian group on the <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo short exact
sequences. Abusing notation, let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> also denote its class. And if
<math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is a subscheme, then let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> also
denote the class. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subgroup generated by <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi\colon\mathbf{K}\to\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\colon\mathbf{K}\to\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the homomorphism induced by the Euler
characteristic, which is just the alternating sum of the lengths of the
cohomology groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the endomorphism of
<math id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the following formula:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined since tensoring with <math id="A2.Thmthm1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
preserves exact sequences.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subscheme with
<math id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective divisor such that
<math id="A2.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\cdot[Y]=[D]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\cdot[Y]=[D].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The left side is defined since <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The equation
results from the sequence <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(-D)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(-D)\to\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\to\mathcal{O}_{\!D}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(-D)\simeq\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(-D)\simeq\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.3</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{M}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{M}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following relations hold:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1b"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})+c_{1}(\mathcal{M})-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{M});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})+c_{1}(\mathcal{M})-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{M});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})+c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})+c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, <math id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> commute.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\mathcal{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})\mathcal{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; thus the
third relation holds.
Plainly, each side of the first relation carries
<math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> into</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{M}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}+\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{M}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}-\mathcal{M}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}+\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{M}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the first relation holds. It and the third relation imply the
second. Furthermore, the first relation implies that <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> commute.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.4</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
irreducible components of <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with their induced
reduced structure, and let <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the length of the stalk of <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>
at the generic point of <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, in <math id="A2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\equiv\sum l_{i}\cdot[Y_{i}]\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\equiv\sum l_{i}\cdot[Y_{i}]\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The assertion holds if it does after we replace <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by a neighborhood of
the image of <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. So we may assume <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Noetherian.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{\prime}\subset\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{\prime}\subset\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the family of <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> for which the
assertion holds. Since length<math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bullet)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bullet)</annotation></semantics></math> is an additive function, <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is “exact” in the following sense: for any short exact sequence
<math id="A2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime}\to\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime}\to\mathcal{F}\to\mathcal{F}^{\prime\prime}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that two of the <math id="A2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>s belong
to <math id="A2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the third does too. Trivially, <math id="A2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
for any closed integral subscheme <math id="A2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="A2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{\prime}=\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{\prime}=\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> by the “Lemma of
Dévissage,” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, Thm. 3.1.2]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.5</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathbf{K}_{r}\subset\mathbf{K}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathbf{K}_{r}\subset\mathbf{K}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for
all <math id="A2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are
isomorphic at the generic point of each component of
<math id="A2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. So Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm4" title="Lemma B.4. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> implies <math id="A2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.6</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A2.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}\otimes\mathcal{F}=\textstyle\sum_{i=0}^{r}\binom{m+i-1}{i}c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{i}\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}\otimes\mathcal{F}=\textstyle\sum_{i=0}^{r}\binom{m+i-1}{i}c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{i}\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> be an indeterminate, and consider the formal identity</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-x)^{n}=\textstyle\sum_{i\geq 0}(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}x^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-x)^{n}=\textstyle\sum_{i\geq 0}(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}x^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Replace <math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-y^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-y^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="A2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=-m</annotation></semantics></math>, and use the familiar identity</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}=\binom{m+i-1}{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}=\binom{m+i-1}{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">to obtain the formal identity</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{m}=\textstyle\sum\binom{m+i-1}{i}(1-y^{-1})^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{m}=\textstyle\sum\binom{m+i-1}{i}(1-y^{-1})^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">It yields the assertion, because
<math id="A2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(L)^{i}\mathcal{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(L)^{i}\mathcal{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;r</annotation></semantics></math> owing to Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma B.5. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.7</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Snapper)<span id="A2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{n}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{n}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},\dotsc,m_{n}\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},\dotsc,m_{n}\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Euler characteristic
<math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi\bigl(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{\otimes m_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathcal{L}_{n}^{\otimes m_{n}}\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\bigl(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{\otimes m_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathcal{L}_{n}^{\otimes m_{n}}\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a polynomial in the <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1e" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1e.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi\bigl(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{\otimes m_{1}}\otimes\dotsm\otimes\mathcal{L}_{n}^{\otimes m_{n}}\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)\textstyle=\sum a(i_{1},\dotsc,i_{n})\binom{m_{1}+i_{1}-1}{i_{1}}\cdots\binom{m_{n}+i_{n}-1}{i_{n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi\bigl(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{\otimes m_{1}}\otimes\dotsm\otimes\mathcal{L}_{n}^{\otimes m_{n}}\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)\textstyle=\sum a(i_{1},\dotsc,i_{n})\binom{m_{1}+i_{1}-1}{i_{1}}\cdots\binom{m_{n}+i_{n}-1}{i_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum i_{j}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum i_{j}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> and where
<math id="A2.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(i_{1},\dots,i_{n}):=\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{i_{1}}\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{n})^{i_{n}}\mathcal{F}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(i_{1},\dots,i_{n}):=\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{i_{1}}\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{n})^{i_{n}}\mathcal{F}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The theorem follows from Lemmas <a href="#A2.Thmthm6" title="Lemma B.6. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a> and <a href="#A2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma B.5. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.8</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, repetitions allowed. Let
<math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the <span id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intersection
number</span> or <span id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intersection symbol</span> by the formula</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}:=\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\bigr)\in\mathbb{Z}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}:=\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\bigr)\in\mathbb{Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>, then also write <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> for the number.
If <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for a divisor <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then also write
<math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r}\cdot\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r}\cdot\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>, or just <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="A2.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.9</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1)<span id="A2.Thmthm9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2) (symmetry and additivity)<span id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The symbol <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric in the <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, it is a
homomorphism separately in each <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(3)<span id="A2.Thmthm9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Set <math id="A2.Thmthm9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\oplus\dotsb\oplus\mathcal{L}_{r}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\oplus\dotsb\oplus\mathcal{L}_{r}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1g.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\chi\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{i}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>i</mi></msup></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\chi\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{i}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (1) results from Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma B.5. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>. So the symbol is a
homomorphism in each <math id="A2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the relations asserted in
Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma B.3. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>. Furthermore, the symbol is symmetric owing to the
commutativity asserted in Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma B.3. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>. Part (3) results from
the definitions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary B.10</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\,\mathcal{L}_{2}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\,\mathcal{L}_{2}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1h" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1h.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\mathcal{F}=\chi(\mathcal{F})-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F})-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{2}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F})+\chi(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{L}_{2}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\mathcal{F}=\chi(\mathcal{F})-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F})-\chi(\mathcal{L}_{2}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F})+\chi(\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{L}_{2}^{-1}\otimes\mathcal{F}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The assertion is a special case of Part (3) of
Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary B.11</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dotsc,D_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dotsc,D_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be effective divisors on <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=D_{1}\cap\dotsm\cap D_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=D_{1}\cap\dotsm\cap D_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\cap\Supp F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\cap\Supp F</annotation></semantics></math> is
finite, and at each of its points, <math id="A2.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is Cohen–Macaulay. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1i" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1i.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r}\cdot\mathcal{F})=\length\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F}_{Z})\text{ where }\mathcal{F}_{Z}:=\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!Z}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\length</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D_{1}\dotsm D_{r}\cdot\mathcal{F})=\length\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F}_{Z})\text{ where }\mathcal{F}_{Z}:=\mathcal{F}\otimes\mathcal{O}_{\!Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="A2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="A2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}\in\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}\in\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{L}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> be the section defining <math id="A2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\oplus\dotsb\oplus\mathcal{L}_{r}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{L}_{1}^{-1}\oplus\dotsb\oplus\mathcal{L}_{r}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Form the corresponding Koszul
complex <math id="A2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">⋀</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and its cohomology
sheaves <math id="A2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">⋀</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\chi\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup></mstyle><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\chi\bigl(\mathcal{H}^{i}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">owing to Part (3) of Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a> and to the
additivity of <math id="A2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, essentially by definition,
<math id="A2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)=\mathcal{F}_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">⋀</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}\bigl(\bigl(\bigwedge^{\bullet}\mathcal{E}\bigr)\otimes\mathcal{F}\bigr)=\mathcal{F}_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. And by standard local algebra, the higher <math id="A2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish.
Thus the assertion holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.12</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
…, <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional irreducible components of
<math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> given their induced reduced structure, and let
<math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the length of the stalk of <math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> at the generic point of
<math id="A2.Thmthm12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1j" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1j.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i}l_{i}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y_{i}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\sum_{i}l_{i}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y_{i}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm4" title="Lemma B.4. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> and Parts (1) and (2) of
Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.13</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> a closed
subscheme with <math id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective
divisor such that <math id="A2.Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{r}|Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{r}|Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1k" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1k.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r-1})[D]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r-1})[D].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.11" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma B.2. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.14</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. If all
the <math id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are relatively ample and if <math id="A2.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\notin\mathbf{K}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\notin\mathbf{K}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1l" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1l.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.12" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Proceed by induction on <math id="A2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\mathcal{F}=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\mathcal{F}=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> essentially by definition, and <math id="A2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathcal{F})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathcal{F})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\notin\mathbf{K}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\notin\mathbf{K}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Owing to Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm12" title="Lemma B.12. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.12</span></a>, we may assume
<math id="A2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is integral. Owing to Part (2) of
Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>, we may replace <math id="A2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> by a multiple, and
so assume it is very ample. Then, for the corresponding embedding of
<math id="A2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, a hyperplane section <math id="A2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonempty effective divisor such that
<math id="A2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{r}|Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}(D)\simeq\mathcal{L}_{r}|Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the assertion results from
Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm13" title="Lemma B.13. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a> and the induction hypothesis.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.15</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1m" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1m.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})g_{*}\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})g_{*}\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.13" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by hypothesis, <math id="A2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is
proper over an Artin subscheme of <math id="A2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\Supp\mathcal{G}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\Supp\mathcal{G}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>;
furthermore, <math id="A2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is separated. Hence, the restriction <math id="A2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g|\Supp\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g|\Supp\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
is proper; so <math id="A2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\Supp\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\Supp\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. And by the dimension
theory of schemes of finite type over Artin schemes, <math id="A2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim g(\Supp\mathcal{G})\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim g(\Supp\mathcal{G})\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="A2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{G}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{G}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define a map <math id="A2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\mapsto\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\mapsto\sum_{i=0}^{r}(-1)^{i}\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. It induces a homomorphism <math id="A2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}g_{*}\colon\mathbf{K}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}g_{*}\colon\mathbf{K}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="A2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(Rg_{*}(\mathcal{G}))=\chi(\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(Rg_{*}(\mathcal{G}))=\chi(\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> owing to
the Leray Spectral Sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, <span id="A2.p18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span>-12.2.4]</cite> and to the
additivity of <math id="A2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, <span id="A2.p18.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span>-11.10.3]</cite>. Furthermore, <math id="A2.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\otimes\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}(\mathcal{G})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">⟶</mo><mo>∼</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\otimes\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}(\mathcal{G})\mathrel{\mathop{\kern 0.0pt\hbox{$\longrightarrow$}}\limits^{\sim}}\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A2.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EGA III1</a>, <span id="A2.p18.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span>-12.2.3.1]</cite>. Hence <math id="A2.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L})Rg_{*}(\mathcal{G})=Rg_{*}(c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})\mathcal{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L})Rg_{*}(\mathcal{G})=Rg_{*}(c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})\mathcal{G})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\textup{R}g_{*}\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>R</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\textup{R}g_{*}\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, <math id="A2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}g_{*}\mathcal{F}\equiv\textup{g}_{*}\mathcal{F}\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>g</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}g_{*}\mathcal{F}\equiv\textup{g}_{*}\mathcal{F}\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>,
because <math id="A2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>R</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{R}^{i}g_{*}\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> since, if <math id="A2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the locus where <math id="A2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> has fibers of dimension at least
1, then <math id="A2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim g(W)\leq r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim g(W)\leq r-1</annotation></semantics></math>. So Part (1) of Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>
yields the asserted formula.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.16</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Projection Formula)<span id="A2.Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be an
<math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
be an integral subscheme with <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X^{\prime}/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=gY^{\prime}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=gY^{\prime}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, give <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> its induced reduced structure, and let
<math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)</annotation></semantics></math> be the degree of the function field extension if finite and
be <math id="A2.Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if not. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1n" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1n.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y^{\prime}]=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y^{\prime}]=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})[Y].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.14" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm4" title="Lemma B.4. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> yields <math id="A2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\equiv\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)[Y]\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{*}\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\equiv\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)[Y]\bmod\mathbf{K}_{r-1}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. So the assertion results from Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm15" title="Lemma B.15. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.15</span></a>
and from Part (1) of Theorem  <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.17</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field, and let <math id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be the spectrum of
an extension field. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{F}_{r}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1o" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1o.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1,T})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r,T})\mathcal{F}_{T}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1,T})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r,T})\mathcal{F}_{T}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.15" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">The base change <math id="A2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to S</annotation></semantics></math> preserves short exact sequences. So it
induces a homomorphism <math id="A2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\colon\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X_{T}/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\colon\mathbf{K}_{r}(X/S)\to\mathbf{K}_{r}(X_{T}/T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly
<math id="A2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the Euler characteristic. The assertion now follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.18</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a flat
coherent sheaf on <math id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Supp\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Supp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Supp\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and of relative
dimension <math id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the function</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1p" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1p.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\mapsto\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}_{y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm18.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is locally constant.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.16" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">The assertion results from Definition <a href="#A2.Thmthm8" title="Definition B.8. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.8</span></a> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mm70</a>, Cor., top p. 50]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.19</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Call them <span id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">numerically equivalent</span>
if <math id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> for all closed integral
curves <math id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.20</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r};\,\mathcal{N}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{N}_{r}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{L}_{r};\,\mathcal{N}_{1},\dotsc,\mathcal{N}_{r}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are numerically equivalent
for each <math id="A2.Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1q" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1q.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{r})\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{r})\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.17" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="A2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the assertion results from Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm12" title="Lemma B.12. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.12</span></a>.
Suppose <math id="A2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
by Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm5" title="Lemma B.5. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p23.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p23.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, <math id="A2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3b" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{2})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{2})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{N}_{2})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{3})\dotsm c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{r})\mathcal{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p23.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p23.3.1" class="ltx_text">Continuing in this fashion yields the assertion.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.21</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-map. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L},\,\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1)<span id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are numerically equivalent, then so are
<math id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm21.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2)<span id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Conversely, when <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective, if <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are numerically equivalent, then so are <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm21.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.18" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed integral curve with <math id="A2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X^{\prime}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X^{\prime}/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=g(Y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=g(Y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and give <math id="A2.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> its induced reduced
structure. Then Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm16" title="Proposition B.16 (Projection Formula). ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.16</span></a> yields</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1r"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1r.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[Y^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[Y^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1r.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]\text{ and }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]\text{ and }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex2e"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{N})[Y^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{N})[Y^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2e.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})[Y]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})[Y].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p24.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p24.2.1" class="ltx_text">Part (1) follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="A2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and surjective. Let <math id="A2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be a
closed integral curve with <math id="A2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a
complete curve in the fiber <math id="A2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> over a closed point <math id="A2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
since <math id="A2.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, there exists a complete curve <math id="A2.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
mapping onto <math id="A2.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, let <math id="A2.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the generic point, and <math id="A2.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\in g^{-1}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\in g^{-1}Y</annotation></semantics></math> a closed point; let <math id="A2.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the closure of <math id="A2.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A2.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> its
induced reduced structure. Plainly <math id="A2.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X^{\prime}/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{Y^{\prime}}\in\mathbf{F}_{1}(X^{\prime}/S)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(Y^{\prime}/Y)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The two equations displayed above now yield Part (2).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.22</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin, and <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a proper curve. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define its <span id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">degree</span> <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1s" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1s.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L}):=\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L}):=\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a divisor on <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Define its <span id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">degree</span> <math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(D)</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A2.Thmthm22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(D):=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(D):=\deg(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.23</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin, and <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a proper curve.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1)<span id="A2.Thmthm23.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The map <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\colon\Pic(X)\to\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\colon\Pic(X)\to\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm23.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2)<span id="A2.Thmthm23.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective divisor. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1t" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1t.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(D)=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!D})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(D)=\dim\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{O}_{\!D}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm23.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(3) (Riemann’s Theorem)<span id="A2.Thmthm23.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L})=\deg(\mathcal{L})+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L})=\deg(\mathcal{L})+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm23.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm23.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(4)<span id="A2.Thmthm23.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Suppose <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is integral, and let <math id="A2.Thmthm23.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be the
normalization map. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L})=\deg(g^{*}\mathcal{L})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L})=\deg(g^{*}\mathcal{L}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.19" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (1) results from Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a> (2). And Part
(2) results from Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm13" title="Lemma B.13. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>. As to Part (3), note <math id="A2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=-\deg(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=-\deg(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> by Part (1). And the definitions yield <math id="A2.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})-\chi(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(\mathcal{L}^{-1})=\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})-\chi(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus Part (3) holds. Finally,
Part (3) results from the definition and the Projection Formula.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.24</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin, and <math id="A2.Thmthm24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a proper surface. Given a divisor <math id="A2.Thmthm24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="A2.Thmthm24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1u" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1u.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D):=1-\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D):=1-\chi\bigl(c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.25</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is Artin, and <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a proper surface. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> be
divisors on <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1v" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1v.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D+E)=p_{a}(D)+p_{a}(E)+(D\cdot E)-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D+E)=p_{a}(D)+p_{a}(E)+(D\cdot E)-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm25.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, if <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is effective, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2g" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2g.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)=1-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!D});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)=1-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!D});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in other words, <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the </span>arithmetic genus<span id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of <math id="A2.Thmthm25.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.20" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">The assertions result from Lemmas <a href="#A2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma B.3. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> and <a href="#A2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma B.2. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.26</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemann–Roch for surfaces)<span id="A2.Thmthm26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field, and <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a reduced, projective,
equidimensional, Cohen–Macaulay surface. Let <span id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> be a dualizing
sheaf, and set <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}:=\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}:=\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a divisor on
<math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; furthermore,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1w" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1w.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)=\frac{(D^{2})+(D\cdot\mathcal{K})}{2}+1\quad\text{and}\quad\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=\frac{(D^{2})-(D\cdot\mathcal{K})}{2}+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)=\frac{(D^{2})+(D\cdot\mathcal{K})}{2}+1\quad\text{and}\quad\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=\frac{(D^{2})-(D\cdot\mathcal{K})}{2}+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X/S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X/S</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein, that is, <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> for some
“canonical” divisor <math id="A2.Thmthm26.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2h" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2h.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)=\frac{\bigl(D\cdot(D+K)\bigr)}{2}+1\quad\text{and}\quad\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=\frac{\bigl(D\cdot(D-K)\bigr)}{2}+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)=\frac{\bigl(D\cdot(D+K)\bigr)}{2}+1\quad\text{and}\quad\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))=\frac{\bigl(D\cdot(D-K)\bigr)}{2}+\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.21" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced, <span id="A2.p28.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="A2.p28.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> is isomorphic to <math id="A2.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math> on a
dense open subset of <math id="A2.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK70</a>,  (2.8), p. 8]</cite>. Hence <math id="A2.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p29.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(D^{2}):=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}=-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{2}):=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}=-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> by Parts (1) and (2) of
Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>.
Now, the definitions yield</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex3d"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mathcal{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mathcal{K})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3d.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(\mathcal{L}-2\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(\mathcal{L}-2\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex4b"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex4b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}+\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-3\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+2\mathcal{L}^{-1}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}\text{\quad and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}+\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-3\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+2\mathcal{L}^{-1}-\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}\text{\quad and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}-\mathcal{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}-\mathcal{K})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(\mathcal{L}-\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})=\mathcal{L}-\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{1}(\mathcal{L})(\mathcal{L}-\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})=\mathcal{L}-\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}+\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p29.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p29.2.1" class="ltx_text">But, <math id="A2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{i}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is dual to <math id="A2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2-i}(\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2-i}(\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math>
by duality theory; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Cor. 7.7, p. 244]</cite>, where <math id="A2.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> needn’t
be taken algebraically closed. So <math id="A2.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{L})=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{L})=\chi(\mathcal{L}^{-1}\otimes\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="A2.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=\chi(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})=\chi(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D^{2})+(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=2(\chi(\mathcal{L}^{-1})-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}))\quad\text{and}\quad(D^{2})-(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=2(\chi(\mathcal{L})-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D^{2})+(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=2(\chi(\mathcal{L}^{-1})-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}))\quad\text{and}\quad(D^{2})-(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=2(\chi(\mathcal{L})-\chi(\mathcal{O}_{\!X})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p29.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p29.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now, <math id="A2.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=\mathcal{L}^{-1}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D))\,\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=\mathcal{L}^{-1}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
first assertion follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <span id="A2.p30.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="A2.p30.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> is invertible. Then <math id="A2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}=\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> owing to the definitions. And <math id="A2.p30.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}^{-1})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}^{-1})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math> by Part (2) of
Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>. Therefore, <math id="A2.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=(D\cdot K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D\cdot\mathcal{K})=(D\cdot K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence Part (2) of Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a> yields the second
assertion.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm27.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B.27</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hodge Index)<span id="A2.Thmthm27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field, and <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a geometrically
irreducible complete surface. Assume there is an <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is
numerically equivalent to <math id="A2.Thmthm27.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\!X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\!X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.22" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may extend the ground field to its algebraic closure owing to
Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm17" title="Proposition B.17. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.17</span></a>. Furthermore, we may replace <math id="A2.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by its
reduction; indeed, the hypotheses are preserved due to
Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm12" title="Lemma B.12. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.12</span></a>, and the conclusion is preserved due to
Definition <a href="#A2.Thmthm19" title="Definition B.19. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.19</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Chow’s Lemma, there is a surjective map <math id="A2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
integral projective surface. Furthermore, we may replace <math id="A2.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by its
normalization. Now, we may replace <math id="A2.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>
by <math id="A2.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the hypotheses are preserved
due to the Projection Formula, Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm16" title="Proposition B.16 (Projection Formula). ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.16</span></a>. And the
conclusion is preserved due to Part (2) of Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm21" title="Proposition B.21. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.21</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">By way of contradiction, assume that there exists a closed integral
subscheme <math id="A2.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})\mathcal{O}_{\!Y}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="A2.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be the blowing-up along <math id="A2.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=g^{-1}Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=g^{-1}Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> the
exceptional divisor. Let <math id="A2.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dotsc,E_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dotsc,E_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the irreducible components
of <math id="A2.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and give them their induced reduced structure.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, it has only finitely may singular points. Off
them, <math id="A2.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor, and <math id="A2.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Hence one of the
<math id="A2.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="A2.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> maps onto <math id="A2.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and the remaining <math id="A2.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> map onto points.
Therefore, <math id="A2.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E_{1}]=\int c_{1}(g\mathcal{L})[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E_{1}]=\int c_{1}(g\mathcal{L})[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E_{i}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E_{i}]=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> by the Projection Formula. Hence
Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm12" title="Lemma B.12. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.12</span></a> yields <math id="A2.p34.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})[E]=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})[Y]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The latter is nonzero by the new assumption, and the former is equal to
<math id="A2.p34.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(E))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(E))</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm13" title="Lemma B.13. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A2.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:=\mathcal{O}_{\!X^{\prime}}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, by the Projection Formula, <math id="A2.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{H})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{H})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{H})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{H})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(g^{*}\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s
prove this situation is absurd. First, replace <math id="A2.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p35.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p35.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be an ample invertible sheaf on <math id="A2.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{1}:=\mathcal{G}^{\otimes m}\otimes\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{1}:=\mathcal{G}^{\otimes m}\otimes\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1x" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1x.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=m\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{G})+\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=m\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{G})+\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p36.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p36.2.1" class="ltx_text">by additivity (see Part (2) of Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm9" title="Theorem B.9. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>). Now,
<math id="A2.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{M})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence there is an <math id="A2.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A2.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and so that <math id="A2.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is ample.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A2.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{\otimes q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes p}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{\otimes q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, additivity yields</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex2i"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2i.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2i.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\textstyle p^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}+q^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2},\text{ and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textstyle p^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}+q^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2},\text{ and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex3e"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3e.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3e.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\textstyle p\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})+q\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textstyle p\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})+q\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p37.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p37.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, there are <math id="A2.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,\,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,\,q</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A2.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A2.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L}_{1})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Replace <math id="A2.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is
ample, <math id="A2.p37.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p37.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="A2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}:=\mathcal{L}^{\otimes n}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{1}:=\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{1}:=\mathcal{L}\otimes\mathcal{H}^{a}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Take <math id="A2.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A2.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is ample. By additivity,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4c" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4c.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=n\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}-a\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})=n\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}-a\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p38.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p38.2.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="A2.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A2.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}_{1})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then additivity
and Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm14" title="Proposition B.14. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.14</span></a> yield</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5a"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5a.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&lt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&lt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex6a"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=n^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}+\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=n^{2}\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})^{2}+\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})^{2}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p38.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p38.3.1" class="ltx_text">But this situation stands in contradiction to the next lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.28</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field, and <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is an integral
surface. Let <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\in\Pic(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
these conditions are equivalent:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm28.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm28.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm28.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_phantom"><span style="visibility:hidden">i</span></span>(i)<span id="A2.Thmthm28.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For every ample sheaf <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm28.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(i<sup id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>)<span id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For some ample sheaf <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm28.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm28.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(ii)<span id="A2.Thmthm28.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For some <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.Thmthm28.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{0}(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.23" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose (ii) holds. Then there exists an effective divisor <math id="A2.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="A2.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n}\simeq\mathcal{O}_{\!X}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. And <math id="A2.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence (i) results as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1y" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1y.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{N})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})[D]&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{N})=\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})[D]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p39.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p39.2.1" class="ltx_text">by symmetry, by Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm13" title="Lemma B.13. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>, and by Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm14" title="Proposition B.14. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.14</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">Trivially, (i) implies (i<sup id="A2.p40.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.p40.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>). Finally, assume (i<sup id="A2.p40.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A2.p40.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">′</span></sup>), and let’s prove
(ii). Let <span id="A2.p40.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="A2.p40.m3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> be a dualizing sheaf for <math id="A2.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; then <span id="A2.p40.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="A2.p40.m5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> is
torsion free of rank 1, and <math id="A2.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is dual to <math id="A2.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(\mathcal{L},\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\Hom</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(\mathcal{L},\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$})</annotation></semantics></math> for any coherent sheaf <math id="A2.p40.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.p40.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>; see
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">FGA</a>, p. 149-17]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK70</a>, (1.3), p. 5, and (2.8), p. 8]</cite>, and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ha83</a>, Prp, 7.2, p. 241]</cite>. Set <math id="A2.p40.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}:=\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}:=\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}-\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\in\mathbf{K}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="A2.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible and <math id="A2.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero. Then
there is a nonzero map <math id="A2.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="1.730em">ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\to\mbox{\LARGE$\omega$}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is injective since
<math id="A2.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is integral. Let <math id="A2.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be its cokernel. Then</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2j" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2j.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\mathcal{F}-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\text{ in }\mathbf{K}_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\mathcal{F}-c_{1}(\mathcal{L}^{-1})\mathcal{O}_{\!X}\text{ in }\mathbf{K}_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p41.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p41.2.1" class="ltx_text">Hence Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmthm14" title="Proposition B.14. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.14</span></a>, symmetry, and additivity yield</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3f" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3f.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})\mathcal{K}\geq\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})\mathcal{K}\geq\int c_{1}(\mathcal{L})c_{1}(\mathcal{H}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="A2.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{L})=n\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{L})=n\int c_{1}(\mathcal{H})c_{1}(\mathcal{N})</annotation></semantics></math> by additivity. But <math id="A2.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})c_{1}(\mathcal{H})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
by hypothesis. Hence <math id="A2.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>H</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{H}^{2}(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="A2.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4d" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4d.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})=\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}\,\binom{n+1}{2}+a_{1}n+a_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\mathcal{N}^{\otimes n})=\textstyle\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}\,\binom{n+1}{2}+a_{1}n+a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p42.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p42.2.1" class="ltx_text">for some <math id="A2.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\,a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\,a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by Snapper’s Theorem, Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm7" title="Theorem B.7 (Snapper). ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.7</span></a>.
But <math id="A2.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int c_{1}(\mathcal{N})^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis. Therefore, (ii) holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmthm29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmthm29.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary B.29</span></span><span id="A2.Thmthm29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmthm29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectrum of a field, and
<math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a geometrically irreducible projective <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-fold with <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,\,E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,\,E</annotation></semantics></math> be effective divisors, with <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> possibly trivial. Assume <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is
ample. Then <math id="A2.Thmthm29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D+E</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.24" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text">Plainly we may assume the ground field is algebraically closed and <math id="A2.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is reduced. Fix <math id="A2.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A2.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nD+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nD+E</annotation></semantics></math> is ample; plainly we may replace
<math id="A2.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nD+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nD+E</annotation></semantics></math> and 0.
Proceeding by way of contradiction, assume <math id="A2.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the
disjoint union of two closed subschemes <math id="A2.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p43.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="A2.p43.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.p43.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are divisors; so <math id="A2.p43.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D_{1}+D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D_{1}+D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">Proceed by induction on <math id="A2.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A2.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, since <math id="A2.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
are disjoint, <math id="A2.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(D_{1}\cdot D_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D_{1}\cdot D_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> by Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmthm13" title="Lemma B.13. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>.
Now, <math id="A2.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is ample. Therefore, Proposition
<a href="#A2.Thmthm14" title="Proposition B.14. ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.14</span></a> yields</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1z" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1z.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(D_{1}^{2})=(D.D_{1})&gt;0\text{ and }(D_{2}^{2})=(D.D_{2})&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> and </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(D_{1}^{2})=(D.D_{1})&gt;0\text{ and }(D_{2}^{2})=(D.D_{2})&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p44.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p44.2.1" class="ltx_text">These conclusions contradict Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmthm27" title="Theorem B.27 (Hodge Index). ‣ Appendix B Basic intersection theory ‣ The Picard scheme" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.27</span></a>
with <math id="A2.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}:=\mathcal{O}_{X}(D_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}:=\mathcal{O}_{X}(D_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{X}(D_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}:=\mathcal{O}_{X}(D_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, suppose <math id="A2.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a general hyperplane section of
<math id="A2.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is integral by Bertini’s Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Se50</a>, Thm. 12,
p. 374]</cite>. And <math id="A2.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is not a component of <math id="A2.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}:=D\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}:=D\cap H</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}^{\prime}:=D_{i}\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}^{\prime}:=D_{i}\cap H</annotation></semantics></math>. Plainly <math id="A2.p45.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p45.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint, and
<math id="A2.p45.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}=D_{1}^{\prime}+D_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}=D_{1}^{\prime}+D_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; also, <math id="A2.p45.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is ample. So induction yields the desired
contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
